Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz wrote:

> In article <34593B4A.904@earthlink.net> macoby@earthlink.net writes:
> >The more mainstream variants of Unix are the ones that have large
> >numbers of applications ported to them.
> >Would you like more!! A name -- Try Solaris !
>
> Agreed, that is *the* mainstream Unix. Now, does it run on IA64 ?
> The corollary of HP's strategy for dumping of PA for IA64 was
>
>         "there will be no chips other than IA64 in the future"
>
> (i.e. at least we can't see how we can keep it competitive performance
>  wise to IA64 without a fab, etc.).
>
> Digital very much thinks that Alpha *can* be kept faster than anything
> else on the planet, including IA64, but we aren't stupid or blind either
> (well maybe we are some of the time, but that's besides this point).
>

I don't want to piss on your fire but Alpha CPU performance has not resultedin the
performance benefits that have meaning to purchasers of commercial
servers (Digitals best chance of staying in the hardware business).

Lets inject a little dose of reality here. Despite having had the fastest
processor
on the planet for the last three years (barring 1 month of HP supremacy) Digital
still languish in the NT server performance league even behind their own Digital
Intel boxes on NT TPC-C results. In fact Digital has never published a competative

NT TPC-C result when compared with its Intel/NT competitors.

In the UNIX server market SUN, HP and SGI all have better TPC-C results and NCR,
SUN,
Sequent, Pyramid and HP all have better TPC-D results. If you look at Application
benchmarks
such as SAP or BaaN again the same story. HP, Sun, Sequent and IBM all have better
SAP
numbers and HP, Sun and Sequent all have better BaaN numbers.

The are lots of why's but one answer may be look at the backplane in the
Turbolaser
what are those nice 600 Mhz processors doing in a 1.2 GB backplane. Put in
perspective
again. In large scale servers only IBM to my knowledge has a slower backplane than

the Turbolaser and Sun SGI and HP have backplanes an order of magnitude faster,
even a
Sun E450 has a faster backplane the the TurboLaser and it is only a quad CPU box.

Now I know there may be new systems coming out, galaxies are on the horizon
(though they
are targeted at OpenVMS and the current crop of Alpha announcements are not going
to help calm potential customers for them), but everyone else has a roadmap as
well and
are just as adept at selling futures, you can only get revenue for actual
products.

> SPARC certainly hasn't really kept up with any of the other chips in
> terms of performance, though overall system performance isn't bad
> when you use armies of them. So, pick any of the following scenarios:
>

You miss the point, an argument to a commercial IS director that goesSun is bad
because they use more CPU's is not going to impress him if
Sun are faster and cheaper.

>       - The only mainstream UNIX is Solaris on SPARC and x86.
>         This likely to loose in performance to a Unix that
>         runs on IA64 at the high end and to Linux on the low
>         end due to price and support. Remember that Digital Unix
>         will likely not be ported to IA64 systems from Digital,
>         but to IA64 systems *in general*. (i.e. Compaq, SGI, etc.).
>

You supposition is that Solaris will not be ported to IA64. Your othersupposition
is that despite having a full 64 bit UNIX (Solaris 2.7) out
a couple of years before any IA64 systems ship that Solaris will not
pick up any 64 bit UNIX applications.

Your other implicit supposition is the business customers actually
care if their applications are 32 or 64 bit.

>       - Solaris also ports to IA64. Invalidates all investments
>         in current SPARC software, but other than that, you have
>         a point.
>

Remind me what the potential Digital strategy was (porting DU to IA64)guess what
that will do to DU on Alpha sales, what happens if OpenVMS is
ported to IA64.

Strange that the Solaris X86 port has not materially affected SPARC sales we would

have noticed by now.


