: In article <63abvt$5uo@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@kar.dec.com (Burkhard
: Neidecker-Lutz) writes: 
: > Since we stated that VMS will be Alpha only and we are committed to
: > indefinitely support VMS, I take that as a statement for continued
: > development of Alpha.

: Let me repeat part of what was just said:
:   WE ARE COMMITTED TO INDEFINITELY SUPPORT VMS

Yeah, I read that too, but (sorry Burkhard) there is much less there
than meets the eye.  In the first place, there is no way in hell you
or Bob Palmer himself can convince me that Digital will support VMS
forever.  They'll support it until they end support, and not a day
longer.  What I *think* this public pronouncement really means is only
that Digital hasn't yet set a date to terminate VMS support/development.
No surprise there, if VMS revenue is still positive.

But what really bothers me about this thread is that it's all about
the Intel deal and whether it spells the ultimate demise of Alpha.
Yet here comes a Digital employee who uses Digital's reputed support
for VMS as an argument in favor of Alpha's continued viability!  Am
I the only one around here who sees the irony in this argument?

- JB



