>The key, though, is that VMS needs to be MARKETED, and sales folks
>whose arms need to be twisted before they'll suggest it need to find
>other jobs, to be replaced by others who will...

But it need not necesarily be marketed by DEC.  I have reason to believe
that there are other companies who would be willing to to move lots of
inexpensive OpenVMS workstations, and would even do some of the advertising
to accomplish this. Basically they just need Digital to give them an
opportunity to do so, by knocking the price down to something sane for the
OS itself, and do the same for some of the software (compilers absolutely,
layered software if at all possible, through a bit of pressure, or an
outright subsidy, on/to the vendors of same), and on the required firmware
(perhaps by bundling that with a motherboard that clone makers can
assemble.)  Look, if they can give the Samba folks an OpenVMS Alpha to work
on, perhaps they can be motivated to contract Corel to let a couple of
Digital employees hang out for a while, bring their own Alphas, and produce
a "Corel Office" for OpenVMS, which would pretty much cover most of our
"productivity" software needs.  (Somehow I don't think Microsoft is
interested in this market, and Lotus/IBM has similar reasons for wishing to
strangle it.) 

