In article <slrn465ea5a.s6i.matt@freud.mec.edu>, matt@MEC.Edu (Matt Bancroft) writes:
:The old versions of samba had a buffer overflow on the UNIX side that
:allowed remote users to obtain Super User Access. It would seem probable
:that the VMS version also had the bug. Anyone sucessfully exploited buffer
:overruns under VMS? Its usually quite difficult to find bugs in the stock
:DEC programs cuz DEC is quite picky about who they let look at their source
:code...

  Matt, you appear to be confusing DIGITAL with another vendor -- OpenVMS
  source listings CD-ROMs are available, and source listings have been
  available for many years.  Source listings for operating systems from
  other vendors may or may not be available -- access to the OpenVMS
  source listings is most certainly available.

  The current price for a source listings kit (this is *not* a source kit)
  is circa US$2K.  As I've mentioned elsewhere, this kit contains the
  results of an OpenVMS build -- only a few modules have been expurgated.

  I've seen various buffer-overrun-based UNIX bugs over the years, and
  this class of bug tends to be somewhat harder to exploit on OpenVMS
  systems, due to various differences in the design, implementation,
  and configuration of the security models on OpenVMS and UNIX. 

  Of course, the presence of a security hole is one of those things that
  can not easily be ruled out -- if you *do* find what you believe is a
  security hole in OpenVMS, please contact DIGITAL directly, and we will
  work with you to determine the extent and resolve any security problem
  found.  In deference to other customers and other sites, please do *not*
  post potential or actual security reports into a newsgroup -- while most
  folks here have an honest interest in this, there are a few folks that
  follow newsgroups that will use this information for nefarious purposes.)

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
