:In article <63acjv$t1c@usenet.pa.dec.com>,
:Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz <neideck@kar.dec.com> wrote:
:>In article <63576e$gcn$1@joe.rice.edu> maliyekk@rice.edu (Anil Thomas Maliyekkel) writes:
:>>Larry Kilgallen (kilgallen@eisner.decus.org) wrote:
:>>: In article <6342ds$ak1$2@joe.rice.edu>, maliyekk@rice.edu (Anil Thomas Maliyekkel) writes:
:>>: > Who is seriously going to want Digital UNIX on IA64, and who
:>>: > is going to waste time porting their software to it when there
:>>: > will be other more mainstream UNIXes?
:>
:>Like what ? There are only two I would consider mainstream: SCO and Solaris.
:
:>
:>Neither is 64-bit. Solaris is *about* to become 64-bit, but has zero
:>64-bit applications today. And SUN is not porting it to IA64. Digital
:>Unix has several thousand applications and *all* of them are 64-bit
:>by necessity (if you ever tried to write a 32-bit application on Digital
:>Unix you know how painful that is).
:>
:>>Well, that's why there is SCO Unix and Solaris x86.
:>
:>Both of which make a great 64-bit Unix for IA64.

    When you talk about 64 bit, you have to talk about why.
    Very few applications actually *need* 64 bit environments.
    And having a 32 bit UNIX does not preclude large filesystems
    or large files, just large (> 2G) mmap()'s.  64 bits is nice,
    but does not provide a huge economic incentive.   You can
    still have 64 bit integers in 32 bit OS's for 'the things that
    count' without slowing them down.

    64 bit OS's also tend to require the use of a larger page size 
    (16K vs 4K) and while this reduces TLB misses and pagetable 
    overhead in larger systems (> 512MB of memory), it tends to make
    small memory configurations (< 512MB of memory) less efficient 
    then their 32 bit counterparts for what most people use them
    for. 

    I think the only thing a 64 bit OS is good for is if you need
    to stick more then 4G of physical ram in the box.  There
    is a market for that, obviously, but not a huge one.

    Also, I wouldn't leave FreeBSD or Linux out of the 32 bit list.
    In fact, we have found FreeBSD to be an order of magnitude more
    reliable then any other commercial UNIX and run it on most of our
    machines now.  The only contender that even comes close is
    solaris, and while SUN has made great strides in reliability
    they still need to work on their security.  FreeBSD is
    certainly as modern as UNIX gets and is missing only one or
    two minor items (but software RAID never worked well anyway,
    even on SGI's and Solaris boxes... better to use a hardware
    solution for that).  The only other major qualifier is that FreeBSD
    is currently a single-cpu kernel.  Solaris and SGI are the kings
    of multi-cpu installations.

    However, we have found multi-cpu 'enterprise' systems to be
    2x to 5x the cost of N single-cpu servers for the equivalent
    power.   To make an enterprise box cost effective you
    have to attach at least 2 gigs of memory, 100 gigs of 
    disk, and multiple 100BaseT's to it, and you have to run
    it at 80% capacity.  Anything less you might as well run on
    one or more PPro 200 or P-II 300's or even single-cpu alpha
    boxes because you will almost certainly saturate your disks long 
    before you saturate the cpu.  That's our experience, at least.

    To me, the speed which DEC is able to attain on their alpha
    is irrelevant when I have nothing to run that actually requires
    that much cpu.  By the time I need a second cpu I will have
    already spent tens of thousands of dollars on the hard disks
    and memory to go with it.  The cost of the cpu is irrelevant
    at that point.

						-Matt

