Guy Harris wrote:
> 
> Eric D. Osborne <OsborneEr@mail.dec.com> wrote:
> >6. The port of Digital UNIX to Merced is a stroke of brilliance.
> >Consider that HP, the current UNIX market leader by dollar volume, has
> >already admitted that they are tanking PA RISC. The shift to Merced will
> >require both a 32-64 bit jump
> 
> PA-RISC 2.0 (as implemented by the PA8xxx chips) is 64-bit.  I think
> HP-UX 11.x will be a 64-bit OS.
> 
That doesn't change my point. All shipping versions of HP/UX are 32 bit 
environments. The change from 32 to full 64 bit is VERY non-trivial I
personally ported a couple million lines of code from HP and Sun to
Alpha
in the early days. It's astounding how many people do stuff like this :

int i;
void *p;

i = (int) p;
/* do some kind of manipulation */
p = (void *)i;

THIS WILL BREAK when you have 64 bit pointers and 32 bit ints. There are
tons of
similar problems that have been induced by people assuming that ints,
longs, and
pointers are the same length. Some are much more subtle than the above.

Bottom line, HP customers are in for a rock road on the 32 bit to 64 bit
jump,
regardless of whether it is done on PA RISC or on IA64. No matter how
good a job
HP does, when you factor in all the third party stuff, it will take a
long time to
get right.

> >and a byte-order swap.
> 
> This presumes that IA-64 will be little-endian-only.  I don't know that
> it will be.  I would not assume that it would be, given who one of the
> partners in IA-64 (and, arguably, the major contributor to IA-64) is,
> and the byte order of their UNIX machines.

Fair enough. Actually, many of the modern processors (including MIPS and
ALPHA) can be
run with either byte order. If they don't flip the byte order, though,
it's a safe bet
that HP will have a lot of trouble building machines than can run both
NT and their UNIX,
which would clearly be a competitive disadvantage. Thus my comment. It
is true that HP may
still avoid the byte order issue, though.
