In article <63cfne$cr8@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@kar.dec.com (Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes:

> The minor issue you are ignoring is that you would need 64 bit *software*
> to actually use any of it. So far there are two companies offering any
> 64 bit production operating systems: SGI and Digital. Most applications for
> IRIX are written to the 32-bit API of the operating system, so most of
> them don't count. *All* of the Digital Unix applications are 64-bit,
> because the system doesn't *have* a 32 bit API. (OpenVMS is 64-bit
> today as well, but again, most applications make use of it's 32 bit API).

There is nothing wrong with a 32 bit application if performance will
not be enhanced by going to 64 bits.  The VMS command SHOW SYSTEM,
for example, is implemented in a 32 bit program.  Thus "percentage
of programs on a system which use the 64 bit capability" is not a
figure of merit.

If a system cannot support 64 bit addressing for programs, of course,
that is a different matter.


Larry Kilgallen



