DEC does not have to rent a facility.  They send the master for the
21964 to Intel (in a format non-semiconductor folk like me do not know)
and tell Intel how many they need by what date.  When they get the
output, they get a bill from Intel which is enough lower than what
they were paying before to cause DEC to have signed the settlement.

The public statements have said that Intel will make chips for DEC.
They have not said all future Alphas will be made in the Hudson FAB
which was sold.  Perhaps that plant will be making Pentiums in the
future when Alphas are too fast to be made in that facility and IA64s
are still just for servers and not for PCs.

