The key thought here is that to a degree at least DEC can influence
what people want. 

There was a lot of early hype about NT...freely revealed internal
interfaces, security, scalability, etc. etc., which has not been
well fulfilled. Crash the latest & greatest NT with a nonpriv'd 3
instruction program, for instance...and consider how few of the
NT internal interfaces are doc'd. Want a list of NT system services?
Good luck...

Once the technical problems begin to be seen by decision makers who
initially looked only at the hype it is possible to get something like
VMS looked at again; but that will not happen, unless the something
like VMS has enough marketing presence to be considered.

Strangely enough, there even DO exist desktop applications that run
on VMS (though grossly out of line with pricing for the same class
apps on a desktop running Wintel). Much COULD be fixed, provided
the Powers that Be at DEC had the guts to fix them. Ending the
"get the last nickel out of the suckers running VMS" mindset (where
it still exists) is part of that. A 10 SPECmark workstation running
VMS with a suite of apps ought to be costed at roughly what a similar
workstation on Wintel would be, for starters. Factors of 1.2 or 1.5
in cost of software could kinda be understood, I guess, but factors of
10 or worse are mighty hard to live with.

The key, though, is that VMS needs to be MARKETED, and sales folks
whose arms need to be twisted before they'll suggest it need to find
other jobs, to be replaced by others who will...

So much for grump of the day...
glenn



