A fab (fabrication facility) doesn't get the right to make chips
ad nauseam once someone hires it to make chips for it. Rather, it
offers its services & equipment to make chips for someone who has
already the right to do so. The case with Intel is perhaps more
complex, in that IF the cross licensing enables it, Intel may have
the right to make Alpha, and DEC MAY have the right to build
x86 chips. In addition to having these rights, one must of course
also have a laid-out design, which can be a separate issue.

Whether either Intel or DEC have any interest in trying to design
or make chips outside their prior range of interests is something
else again. Besides which, getting a correct design is by no means
simple and may involve additional information which may be licensed
separately or simply kept as trade secrets...

I suspect long term that DEC being able to build Alphas with current
technology, instead of spending billions every few years on a new
fab plant, is a very sensible move. The cost of these facilities
can easily eat every spare cent they can get their hands on. (That's
why RCA got out of the solid state business some years ago...)



