>Lets face it, Digital did not succeed in making Alpha *THE* chip.

Not yet, but we are getting there (it's a slow uphill battle). 

>I do
>not know the financials, but I would not be surprised if ALPHA was a
>financial failure considering the high incremental costs for each new
>generation and the relatively small number of chips actually sold.

We never made money off Alpha chips alone. We sold about US $15 billion
of *systems* around Alphas so far. Remember, we are *not* a chips-only
company (and now are a no-fabs company, which is quite a change).

>If even Intel complains about the high costs of FAB plants for its mass
>production chips, could Digital realistically continued pour money into
>Alpha in the long term considering it isn't getting anywhere with the

The fab costs are one of the stated reasons to go to a fabless model.
We just have to be careful to avoid being locked into a single source
so no single foundry can dictate our chips schedules. With Samsung
*and* Intel this scheme can work (and Mitsubishi).

>chip with regards to market penetration, and considering it will have
>absolutely no hopes once Intel has its own 64 bit chip on the market ?

Ironically, Intel having it's 64 bit chip out (in 1-2 years) may finally
*help* to reach market penetration. A lot of people don't (yet) *needed*
64 bit, so it wasn't as much of an add-on differentiator to other chips
as it should have been. Now with Intel (as the last in the industry) to
enter that camp, it suddenly becomes important (like MMX, which noboy needs
but everybody talks about because of the Intel marketing machinery). 

The minor issue you are ignoring is that you would need 64 bit *software*
to actually use any of it. So far there are two companies offering any
64 bit production operating systems: SGI and Digital. Most applications for
IRIX are written to the 32-bit API of the operating system, so most of
them don't count. *All* of the Digital Unix applications are 64-bit,
because the system doesn't *have* a 32 bit API. (OpenVMS is 64-bit
today as well, but again, most applications make use of it's 32 bit API).

>I *think* that ALPHA has about 5 years max left into it. At that point,
>DEC's jewels (UNIX and NT) will have been ported to INTEL and Alpha will

NT isn't our jewel, it's Microsoft's jewel. We just run it for them...

