A small spark of gloom in a post which otherwise MADE MY DAY.  Sure, I
don't mind paying more for VMS---you get what you pay for.  However, in
maximising profits, there is an optimum---too expensive and you sell too
few, though individual profits are high, too cheap and you don't make
enough, although you sell very many.  I have a feeling that this optimum
pricing lies in a place more competitive to other desktop systems.  In
other words, the machine above, at that price---or $100 more,
even---running VMS.  You would make a bit less per machine than you do
now, but you would sell A LOT MORE MACHINES.  VMS used to be quite
common at university research institutes and similar places, whereas now
at many places people are using more and more unix.  The reason is
always the same---money.  Sure, there are secondary reasons, like more
needed software being supported on unix, but this is a consequence of
the majority moving to unix in the first place.  It's a vicious circle,
but the primary cause was the price difference.  Even if one doesn't
make a profit in this arena, the personnel turnover is rather large, and
people who learned VMS at university will move into industry and
business and have a boss with a serious expense account and convince him
to spend that money on VMS.  It's just plain good business sense, which
is why Digital should do it.  Of course, I'm happy that it would benefit
me as well. 


