> >: In article <6342ds$ak1$2@joe.rice.edu>, maliyekk@rice.edu (Anil Thomas Maliyekkel) writes:
> >: > Who is seriously going to want Digital UNIX on IA64, and who
> >: > is going to waste time porting their software to it when there
> >: > will be other more mainstream UNIXes?
>
> Like what ? There are only two I would consider mainstream: SCO and Solaris.
>
> Neither is 64-bit. Solaris is *about* to become 64-bit, but has zero
> 64-bit applications today. And SUN is not porting it to IA64. Digital
> Unix has several thousand applications and *all* of them are 64-bit
> by necessity (if you ever tried to write a 32-bit application on Digital
> Unix you know how painful that is).
>

Despite repeated attempts at benchmarketing most of the 64 bit performanceclaims have been shown
to be exactly that claims particularly in the commercial
space.

Analysis of TPC-C, TPC-D, BaaN, SAP etc published benchmarks show
no discernable magic bullet effect of having a 64 bit architecture and a 64 bit
OS. Despite having fast CPU's and 64 bit UNIX Digital do not have products that
can demonstrate leadership in any of these benchmarks.

If Digital is to be successfull at all it has to capture more commercial server
business the desktop NT margins are too low.

> >Well, that's why there is SCO Unix and Solaris x86.
>
> Both of which make a great 64-bit Unix for IA64.
>
> >Intel is interested in FX-32 as a Unix x86 to IA64 translator.
>
> Where did you get that impression ?
>
> >Still will the investment in maintenance and development be worth
> >it for moving Digital UNIX over to IA64 when they won't be market
> >leaders?
>
> Who is the market leader in 64-bit Unixes for IA64 ? The only announced
> Unix for IA64 so far was the HP/SCO project, both of which have a long
> history of 64-bit Unixes. Ah, well, ah, no. Both have *zero* experience
> with 64-bit Unix. We've had a industry strength 64bit Unix since 1993.
> And lest you think this is simple, let me just remind you that you need
> to have drivers for all kinds of peripherals on popular I/O busses (PCI...)
> with drivers that *support 64-bit* kernel environments. None of that
> stuff is easy, unless you have gone through the pains of it already.
>

Well this may come as a suprise to NCR who have signed a deal to port Solaristo NCR systems
including forthcoming IA64 machines.

You need to read more Analysts reports, Gartner, IDC, Meta etc almost universally
write Digital UNIX off, not for any technical reasons but because of lack of ISV support.
Paradoxically Digitals great white hope NT is making this worse since all the analysts
report that traditional UNIX ISV's are all reducing their supported UNIX platforms
in order to cope with the resource issues surrounding their entry into the NT
market, these same analysts generally predict a 3 UNIX future. In alphabetical
order. AIX, HP-UX/SCO Merge and Solaris.

ISV's do not bet their resources on sentiment or technology niceties that is clear
from the Microsoft experience they bet their resources on market share and
sadly Digital UNIX has lost too much of that to be a serious player.

Without the ISV's you are back to the FX!32 scenario nice technology useless
business proposition (where is the ISV support)

I would suggest a rapid crash port of OpenVMS to IA64 when it becomes

available.
Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK






