This post is kind of long, so I apologize for that in advance.

Let me preface this posting by saying that everything that I'm about to
say
is a reflection of my own opinion, and should in no way be construed to
represent
the opinions of Digital Equipment Corporation.

I left Digital in early 1995, and subquently worked as a senior staff
member for
the department of computer and information science at the Ohio State
University,
where I was chartered with managing a very large mixed Sun and HP shop
(about 700
systems, I think). Anyway, in April I chose to come back to Digital for
a number of
reasons, and this Intel/Digital agreement has done nothing to change my
view. In fact,
I am extremely excited.

Let me explain why that is so. I realize that my arguments won't be
convincing to
many of you, because frankly I think many people simply WANT to believe
the worst,
but please consider the following:

1.  Digital, like any other publicly held company, exists for one reason
and one reason
only - to make a buck. Each and every action taken is taken with that in
mind. Strategy
(while sometimes rather poorly chosen or implemented) is ALWAYS
undertaken with that as
it's ultimate goal.

2. Given item 1., it's pretty obvious that there is no deeply rooted
conspiracy to 
pipe Alpha or OVMS in order to sell out to the mindless minions of
Wintel. The goal is
to make money, in the best way possible.

3. Rather, the length of time that Digital continues to push both Alpha
and OVMS will 
clearly depend upon the amount of time that the market continues to WANT
those 
products. Digital still makes rather huge amounts of money off of both,
and will 
quite obviously continue to sell them as long as that's true. The
ultimate span of
time that Alpha and OVMS are offered will ultimately not be determined
by Digital,
but by you folks out there in the customer base. If you buy in
sufficient quantity to
warrant continued investment by Digital, the products will be offered.
If you stop 
buying, Digital will ultimately be forced by simple economics to
discontinue production.
This ain't rocket science. It's the nature of capitalism.

4. So, where does this leave us with the Intel thing? Well, it's been
pretty well 
known for some time that Hudson was a financial drain, and that very
large investments
will be required to be able to produce future generations of the chip.
Digital owns a
fab plant mostly to produce Alpha, and both the continued operational
expenses and 
the future investments required to produce Alpha are difficult to
amortize across the
limited number of chips that Digital produces. A company like Intel HAS
to make those
kinds of investments, because their entire business depends upon it.
Remember, Digital's
primary business isn't (and never has been) chip production. Digital
builds systems.
This gives Digital the opportunity to build systems without incurring
the ongoing 
expense of making the chips. The agreement doesn't hurt Intel, because
they have to have
state of the art chip production facilities no matter what, and it
eliminates a lot of
expense for Digital. It's a win-win.

5. The idea that Intel would intentionally try to avoid delivering Alpha
chips to
Digital is pretty silly. First, Digital would sue them blind. Secondly,
the FTC would
be all over them so fast it would make their heads spin. Third, the bad
PR would NOT
be something they would want. And fourth, Digital has other partners
(Mitsubishi,
Samsung) who could fill in the gap. Actually, given the expense relief
Digital gets
out of this deal, I would warrant a guess that Digital has just improved
the long
term viability of Alpha by a very large margin.

6. The port of Digital UNIX to Merced is a stroke of brilliance.
Consider that HP, the current UNIX market leader by dollar volume, has
already admitted that they are tanking PA RISC. The shift to Merced will
require both a 32-64 bit jump and a byte-order swap.
HP will have a very bumpy road ahead of them for probably a year or two
after they get
working Merced hardware. Digital UNIX on Merced will face neither of
these issues. And
Digital can now state with perfect sincerity that it will offer UNIX on
the same Merced
processor that HP intends to use, and that the UNIX in question will be
the same safe,
mature, and stable UNIX that is on ALPHA. In the near term, that makes
Alpha a FAR 
safer choice than HP/UX - at least for the next 3 years. That should
actually help to
EXPAND the sales of Alpha products, with the obvious benefit of
improving the
likelyhood that Alpha will survive long-term. Alpha will continue to
have performance
advantages over Merced, so even when DU is available on that platform,
what incentive
will existing customers have to jump off Alpha? I don't know many people
who are 
anxious to move to a slower system.

7. If DU and NT can help to extend the life of Alpha, that helps to
prolong the life
of OVMS on the platform as well.

You know, I was with Digital during the MIPS fiasco, and I was extremely
disappointed
in what Digital did. But this is NOT a parallel situation. The Alpha
architecture
was clearly superior to MIPS, and time has proven that. Dropping the
MIPS products
would have been an economic necessity over time. Digital's management of
the 
transisition was poor, and I think handled very badly. I won't defend
it. But
this time around, the existing Alpha architecture is superior to the new
thing.
Some customers want Intel systems - that is why we make them now (in far
larger
volumes than we make Alpha systems). But the customers that want the
advantages of
Alpha are willing to pay a premium, and I expect that to continue in the
future. With
a little luck, though, the elimination of the fab plant expenses will
allow Digital
to eventually reduce the premium that customers pay for Alpha, making it
more
attractive to customers and more competitive with Merced.

Finally, let me point out that the decline in the sales of VMS, and in
Digital's 
fortune over the last few years, can be ultimately summed up in one
single sentence :
Digital wasn't selling what customers wanted. Regardless of how
vehemently Digital,
or any of you, defend VMS or VAX or whatever, the simple truth is that
large numbers
of customers defected to cheaper RISC based UNIX systems and to PCs.
Digital must
address that reality to survive, and offer customers what they want.
This agreement
positions Digital as the low risk vendor in the industry. Premier UNIX.
Premier NT.
The world's best performance now, and for the next several years at a
minimum.
The continued stability and features of OVMS. And the availability of
the
next generation of Intel's commodity hardware, running both NT and
Digital's high
quality UNIX implementation.

There's no down side here, folks. 

Eric D. Osborne
Digital Equipment Corporation



