I won't re-post OsborneEr@mail.dec.com's long article, but do have to make 
a few comments:


Yes, indeed DIGITAL always was a SYSTEM company, in the sense that you could 
(and would) get a full system, including all components in HW as well as SW 
you need to build your own stuff:

I/O and Storage, CPU and memory, terminals, printers, ...whatever else HW, 
decent OS (only ONE still alive, though. DU or NT are far from it) and all the 
tools neded to run  large clusters, to maintain them, decent compilers, a decent
database system to build apps on and many basic apps as well. AND there was 
excellent, knowledgable support available. Yes, did cost, but yes, was worth it.

Things -at least in perception, often based on experience- have changed.

Let me take a Mercedes as an analogon:

Of course it will be extremely simple to find the Mercedes dealer closest to you.
Of course he will be willing and able to find out about every possible selection
of car and options including pricing for you, and all spares etc. you will
ever need later. And of course if you already have a Mercedes, you will get
this same spare in 2002 as well as now - from the very same Mercedes Dealer. Of
course, at Mercedes prices.
Of course, no one wants to know that Mercedes gearboxes are built eg. by ZF,
tires by Conti or Pirelli or others, brakes by ATE, brake liners by Lukas or 
Jurid, Electronics by Bosch, Siemens, and others, instrumentation by VDO, 
etcpp....
What I want to know is that Mercedes is in charge, and that they will ensure
that there is ONE SINGLE SOURCE for me as customer where I can get that stuff, 
guaranteed. Of course, knowing all of the above, and loving to repair a car myself,
I can get parts and options directly from the manufacturers in many cases, and 
maybe even save money. But it's my own risk, my own time, and my own "fun".

Digital once was quite close to Mercedes.

But now, the engine will built by GM, the gear box is roumored to be dropped 
sometime, the driving wheel I have to try to buy at CA, the tires must be
purchased from ORACLE, and the Mercedes dealer is hiding 5000 feet underground
in some old mine some 100 miles out in the dessert. And many options or parts
you have now will not be available for newer models anymore, and maybe not even
work with or be available for your current Mercedes after the next 
maintenance (OS upgrade).

Would you then think that maybe Mercedes is a thing you should in future better 
avoid? Even though what Mercedes you can still get hold of -somehow, at
Rolls or Ferrari prices- is still technically as good as a Mercedes?



If you're right and DIgital is a SYSTEM company in the way Mercedes is a CAR
company, then why do they behave as if they were a dying spare part selling
scrap yard? Hidden 5000 feet deep in a old mine 100 miles out in the dessert??

Sigh

Uwe



