In article <34569DB9.12D3@virgin.net>, Lisa <lisa.s@virgin.net> writes:
> I have just bought a Digital Hard Drive model DSP3105 - 1 Gig
> for use with my Apple Mac.
> When I turn the Power on for this external drive, the disk
> does not spin up automatically ??
> 
> I have to run a SCSI utility, and scan the SCSI bus,
> before the drive will spin up, and can then be mounted for
> use.
> 
> Is this a jumper setting, can I do anything about it ??
>
Probably labelled MS (Motor Start) or DS (Delay Spin) or SD (Spin Delay)
according to various sources, including the Quantum support page (these
disks were sold off to Quantum).  Pop a jumper in and try it.

All Digital disks used to be this way, and earlier ones didn't have jumpers -
the console ROMs were smart enough to spin the disks up.  Occasionally a
variable start delay according to disk ID was used, so a full bus came in
like a children's round, each disk a few seconds after the one before.
