Well, the whole point is that Alpha's cost-performance might just be much 
better that Merced's: in fact you may expect in late 1999 that Samsung 
Alpha 21364 with well over 100 SPECint95 might be much cheaper than the 
first ~50 SPECint95 Merced from Intel...

The other thing is that this agreement gives Digital Alpha designers more 
freedom to work with other foundries - Samsung is a good example with 
their aggressive marketing and roadmap for 0.25um and 0.18um process 
implementation, and they also can design their own Alpha derivatives. And 
there are also Mitsubishi and (possibly) AMD. So Alpha is actually 
getting out of Digital as an industry-standard architecture with a 
potential to rival IA-32 and IA-64 as the next major uP arch for the next 
decade. It's just that Intel will make money in any case...

