Hi:

Now that everybody has put in their 2 cents, here is mine.

Selling the FAB facility makes sense if DEC can get the Alpha chips at a
cheaper price than manufacturing them.  And, remember, this is an age of
cooperation, not competition.  Having said that, it makes me uneasy that
DEC gave the manufacturing rights to Intel.  That's like Boeing giving the
rights to produce the 747 to Lockheed.  

Second, Digital still owns VMS.  It does differentiate them from the rest
of the crowd.  Our VMS systems have been up for days, I don't remember the
last time it inadvertently shut down.  However, I don't see the commitment
to VMS from DEC that we VMS users have to the OS.  We know it is the best
OS available, and, now that Netscape and Java (soon) will ship with VMS, it
will be a better OS.  

I see the future model of computing being around the NC not WNT.  Why pay
$2K - $3K per desktop, when $600 can be spent on a NC with a built in
browser, X/Motif, and Java?  VMS is beautifully positioned for this new
paradigm - what's old is new again.  I hope the upper management at DEC
understand this.  But, I think this is in vain for they have found a new
religion in WNT.

When I was at the last local DECUS, the DEC rep said that VMS is for
specialized markets that need continuous computing with WNT and Unix are
for the commoners.

I just have one request to Terry, for those of us not attending Anaheim,
please post daily reports to this newsgroup.  This DECUS won't be boring.


