Try again.

Digital has a 5+ year head start on the others in tuning a 64 bit UNIX
system for large servers.  They've set world records in TPC benchmarks
and in SPECmarks which means they have smart OS and compiler engineers
as well as systems and peripherals designers/implementors/integrators,
etc.

Given a reasonable ABI (since Intel has written these in the past,
there's no reason to think they won't write one for Merced),  there's
no reason generic Merced binaries can't be run on a Digital Merced box
running Digital UNIX, and be the best-of-breed solution.  Goodbye to
the 'Digital has no applications' bug-a-boo!!!

So then you go to netscape.com and download netscape-ia64.tar.gz and
try it on DEC/NCR/HP/whom-ever-else-joins-the-party and if it runs
best on Digital's hardware/OS combo, you use the Digital product, etc.  

This allows Digital to attempt to grow into other folk's installed
bases instead of having to bleed dry it's current one forever.
Whether they succeed or not is debatable but it's clear that 
Alpha is viewed as a niche product that many customers immediately
rule out because it's not mainstream.  

