In article <34550785.900@world.std.com>, "T. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>It appears that DEC has a long-term commitment from Intel. And of
>course, there's always Mitsubishi and Samsung, both of whom now are
>producing Alpha chips.

It's a good thing that these other two are still in it, because if by some
miracle the Alpha ever began to seriously compete with Merced you could be
certain Intel would find some way to make it extremely difficult for
Digital to produce Alpha chips at the now Intel controlled FAB.  A
contract is nice, but when Intel brings its lawyers into play it could
become hideously expensive and time consuming for DEC to make them comply
with its terms.

>FWIW, Palmer said that DEC remains committed to producing multiple
>generations of Alpha.

What else is he going to say?  For the sake of argument, assume the truth
is the exact opposite, that all Alpha development stops immediately - could
he say that?  Of course not, because then everybody would know that the
Alpha had been committed to DECstation status, and sales would tank
instantaneously. For similar reasons, DEC would have to hang onto the Alpha
development team, even if they just had them locked in a room playing
pinochle all day long, because to do otherwise would be viewed as an 
admission of the death of the processor.

>It's a bit early to write Alpha off. 

Perhaps.  If you were to review the history of Digital's MIPS based
products (in the back issues of SKD, of course) I'm pretty confident that
you'll find that the correct point to have written these off was long
before Digital finally announced that they had done so, but probably very
close to the early Alpha announcements.  Does history repeat itself? 

