It's been a long time since I worked with Decserver 200 but I think you can
set some ports up as a reverse Lat so that terminals (or PC's acting as
terminals) could connect to a port on one server and then go out through a
port set up as a reverse Lat.

This gave me a virtuial RS-232 connection between a users terminal and the
RS 232 port on a piece of equipment that a user needed to access. I used
this to allow users access to some shared shared resources such as modems
(this is ancient history I think the modems where 2400 or 4800 baud), and
mainframe systems that could only be accessed by RS 232 connection.

The connection from the terminal to the lat port only required 3 wires; the
send data, recieve data, and ground line (2,3,7).  The reverse lat port
would work with 3 wires but to ensure that when a user disconnedted from
the port the connection to the equipment was broken I had to use some of
the other RS232 control signals (all cables are null modem).

The primary use of the Dec Server 200 was to connect user terminals to a
VAX. At one time each user had a RS 232 null modem cable running from his
terminal to the VAX.  The back of the VAX looked like a snake pit with
several hundred cables connecting to it.  With the Dec Servers the only
connection to the VAX was an ethernet cable (a very welcome change).  The
reverse lat feature was an extra bonus it allowed me to give users access
to non DEC computers (also printers and modems) without having to run
seperate cables and swith boxes to each user.


I just had an other thought; some models of DS 200 ( all the ones I used )
had 25 pin RS232 connectors but I think some had a connector similar to a
RJ-11 phone jack (looked like RJ-11 but different keying)

