>> If DEC sells IA64 systems that seems a continuation of selling
>> x86 systems now.  Adding DEC Unix gives HP some competition on
>> their chosen chip.  They thought they had a Unix exclusive for
>> that family.
>
>A point well taken. Could prove to be real interesting. I believe that
>DEC is serious about the port, and I understand that it's well past the
>"technical feasibility" stage already.

Great, then they can get in line, since it is inconceivable that there will 
not also be Linux, NetBSD, Solaris, and SCO Unix for Merced (as they 
already all have Intel versions).  It would not be a huge stretch to see
AIX and Irix make the jump too. Probably Tandem (NonStop-UX) will be in
as well. Plenty of time for everybody to port, since no Merced systems
will ship for years.

So it's fat city for Unix buyers, as they can pretty reasonably expect to
be able to buy whatever hardware they want, and then choose from among:

AIX, Digital Unix, HP-Unix, Irix, Linux, NetBSD, NonStop-UX, Solaris, SCO Unix

That's great for the consumer, but it sucks for the Unix vendors.  I don't 
know how you folks rate the survival prospects among this lot, but I put
Digital Unix well down from the top of the pack, in no higher than fifth
position, since it will have no performance advantage, and a much smaller
installed base to migrate/draw from.  So what are we looking at here, maybe
5% of a market that is itself being consumed by WNT?  What compelling 
reason would anybody have to buy DU on Merced when given the other options?

At least if OpenVMS ran on Merced it would be a different OS, with 
different strengths and weaknesses, and it would not be at all unreasonable
to expect that it's market share would be greater than that for DU on
the exact same hardware.  (Assuming that for once Digital priced it to 
compete.)  Also, for what it's worth, OpenVMS doesn't run on
alpha now in the same sense that WNT does, it needs a big pile of hardware/
firmware glue to force some operations to be atomic.  I don't see that as
being purely a drawback though, because it is that very layer of glue that
lets OpenVMS do some things that the other OS's cannot. 

