From: helbig@multivac.jb.man.ac.uk (Phillip Helbig)
Subject: an open letter to Robert Palmer
Date: 1997/10/29
Message-ID: <6371rq$11l$1@probity.mcc.ac.uk>
X-Deja-AN: 286403480
Organization: University of Manchester, NRAL
Reply-To: helbig@multivac.jb.man.ac.uk
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


From a continuing thread of desperation in the newsgroups:

> > >From Terry's excerpt, it appeared that Alpha was truly dead, since
> > >Digital has agreed to develop future systems based on Intel's 64-bit
> > >microprocessor.
> > 
> > Read Palmer's words again, the exact quote is:
> > 
> >   It ensures long-term availability of Digital's Alpha-based offerings for
> >   Open VMS, Digital UNIX and Windows NT customers. In addition, Digital will
> >   develop Digital UNIX and Windows NT offerings on the IA-64 architecture.
> > 
> > Notice any OS missing from that last sentence?  
> 
> Yup. OVMS.
> 
> Anybody believe the first
> > sentence given the second???  
> 
> Why not? For heaven's sake, look at the size of the OVMS base. 434K
> systems (and who knows how many users) at last count. You really think
> DEC's gonna slaughter that cash cow?

Perhaps one of the hardware guys can comment on whether or not porting 
VMS to Intel is practicable.

> > Ie, they will NOT port OpenVMS, giving Palmer the final excuse to kill it,
> > or rather, effectively terminating further development.

Again, it might not be practicable to port to Intel.  That does not 
necessarily mean it will be killed.

> DEC had the opportunity to port OVMS to Intel. They gave up on it.
> Remember the EMERALD project? Or VMS-on-Mach?

> > Here Digital is set to roll out Galaxies, so it
> > couldn't be a better time to introduce a huge degree of uncertainty in the
> > Alpha's future, and all but announce that they will soon cease development
> > on OpenVMS. Yeah, right, who is going to buy into Galaxies under *those*
> > conditions?  

Galaxies might be a make-or-break situation for Digital.  Properly 
marketed, it could be a comeback comparable to that of Tom Jones.  Screw 
it up, and it could mean the end of VMS, at least in practical terms 
although some lip service might still be paid.

The arguments for keeping VMS are massive.  But so is the public 
perception that that is not what will happen.  In this business, 
perception is more important than truth, since the former can become a 
self-fulfilling prophecy and the latter often does not influence the 
former.

Digital Equipment Corporation, in the past, has arguably produced the
best hardware (the full range---terminals, disks, processors, you name
it) and the best software (operating systems, compilers,...) the world
has ever known.  I have never met ANYONE who, had he had the experience
to make a fair comparison, did not enormously prefer Digital.  Some
things have been sold off and/or discontinued, and up until now one
might can say that it had to be done.  I don't know enough to comment.
However, the ALPHA architecture is simply the best there is.  And VMS is
the best operating system there is.  Digital's compilers are among the
best in the world.  The combination is an unbeatable platform.  It seems
to me that it would be completely absurd not to play this trump card and
tell the world how good you are, aggressively market the stuff, and
remain the world leader in computing (in quality now but perhaps in
numbers as well in the future (everywhere but for home PCs perhaps, or
maybe even there, who knows?)). 

The VMS user base is a wonderful community.  It is also very true to 
Digital, continuing to buy---sometimes paying more than from the 
competition, which is OK if you get what you pay for---in the face of 
other recommendations from colleagues and perhaps even superiors.  There 
are a lot of systems out there doing important work.  Why give it up?

Despite any official statements to the contrary, this game is determined
by marketing (what did Bill Gates pay to use `Start Me Up'?) and public
perception, not by the facts.  A company which, however, has the facts
on its side AND can market this well would be a really nice company, one
which I would be very proud of.  However, VMS continues to be marketed
less than WNT and DUNIX and there has still been no REAL clarification
of the ALPHA-Intel future.  It does not matter what Digital has publicly
stated if the IMPRESSION is that it is not a good idea to invest in VMS
and ALPHA.  This will cause people not to do so, and they will die,
ostensibly because demand dropped---not the direct fault of
Digital---but this drop in demand will have been caused by bad
marketing---definitely the fault of Digital. 

Frankly, I do not understand what is going on.  Anything less than a
full commitment to ALPHA is absurd---it is the leading chip and can
continue to be and runs all three operating systems.  What more can one
want.  As far as VMS goes, every VMS customer is a Digital customer, who
will stay with Digital and probably buy almost exclusively Digital
hardware.  Every DUNIX customer can be enticed away to another unix much
easier than the VMS customer can.  And every WNT customer pays money to
Microsoft as well as Digital; what's more, as in the unix case, they can
migrate to non-Digital hardware.  I can understand that there are people
committed to unix and Digital should certainly try to have these
customers, if it is worth the development effort.  And if Digital can
sell more ALPHA systems to people who really need NT, then that's OK
too. 

However, what can be the possible reason to try to move VMS customers 
away from VMS?  If anything, Digital should, for the reasons above, be 
trying to move WNT and unix customers---whether they're buying from 
Digital now or not---to VMS, since VMS is, all other things being equal, 
the best business for Digital.  The only reason not to push VMS too 
strongly would be if it scared away too many new customers, thinking 
that Digital only `really' supports VMS and unix and WNT are to be 
better had elsewhere.  However, I'm sure that the present losses from 
VMS customers outweigh the gains from additional unix and WNT customers 
by ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE.  There are several factors here.  One, present 
VMS customers are hesitant about investing more.  If they decide to 
leave VMS, most will leave Digital as well, for fears that the same 
thing might happen with the next OS they move to if they stay with 
Digital.  Two, far too few new VMS customers are attracted.  Three, as 
mentioned above, one VMS customer should be worth more to Digital, both 
because he brings more revenue to Digital than an WNT customer (none 
going to microsoft) and also because he is more apt to continue to be a 
Digital customer.

If something similar happened in politics, the politicians in question 
would have had to leave office long ago.

Sure, the computing business is unpredictable.  Sure, things might be
completely different 30 years from now.  But a public commitment to
support VMS (on ALPHA of course) at least as long as the other operating
systems are supported, and a marketing strategy which puts VMS in at
least as much limelight as the other operating systems---telling the
world that Digital AND NO ONE ELSE has the best products in the
world---would REALLY increase Digital's revenues.  Much would be gained, 
and little, if anything, lost.  Is this too much to ask?

Despite all public statements, the proof that not enough is being done 
are threads like this in the newsgroups.  As long as they continue, more 
work needs to be done on the marketing and management side.

I'm just a small-time player in the VMS world who wonders why, with any 
other product I choose because of its quality and performance, I never 
even think about telling the manufacturer not to let it die.

What we need is a top-level web page at www.digital.com at which we can 
point anyone who doubts the sanity of continuing to rely on VMS and 
ALPHA.


