Once GCC gets properly optimized for Alpha, I'd bet that DEC Unix 
starts getting cannibalized big time by Linux (and freeBSD and...).
Rumor has it this is happening majorly to HP already. The unix market
is morphing into a truly open one (in that sources are available!)
from a mostly-proprietary one.

An interesting question to ask about this "NT-only" Alpha that
is rumored: if they actually blow the fuse that controls how memory
mapping is done, will that break Linux or FreeBSD as well as VMS
and DEC Unix?

It would in fact be much more interesting to see VMS ported to Merced
in the end, but I doubt resources for any such thing exist, nor will
unless someone starts telling folks what VMS does that NT or Unix
do not. Galaxies is a good start in this, just so it does not get
the "stealth marketing" treatment VMS has had. One can add simple
stuff (vis a vis nt) like correct argument checking for system
calls, write operations to disk that actually get the data to disk
or tell the application there was an error, and ability to handle
hundreds of users (or more) without crashing (and for that matter
ability to run without constant reference to the power switch).

It is not clear though that Merced will survive long-term. Given that
Intel will be making Alpha also, it is conceivable that it could
decide to make that the successor. If an EV7 or EV8 were to
have an x86 mode thrown in (seeing the cross licensing...), would
there be any point in the industry to using Merced, presuming that
Alpha turns out (as I expect) to have a performance advantage over
the Merced IA64 instructions when used with the output of real C++
(or other compiler) outputs? If Merced is faster, of course, it can
be expected to make Alpha marginal, but now that Alpha will have
access to a Cu process, whose fab DEC probably could not afford,
that becomes questionable.

Certainly if I were a designer of EV7, I'd be wondering what it would
take to bolt an x86 translator onto the side of Alpha. From Intel's
point of view, it would be a win-win proposition since whichever
model won, they'd be making it. HP would feel otherwise, but HP
may not be in the driver's seat there...



