In article <632l0f$9e5@blimpy.early.com>, jsb@early.com (Jonathan Boswell) writes:
>T. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:
>: Futher details at both firms' websites. The settlement seems quite
>: consistent with what I've reported in Shannon Knows DEC (and posted to
>: this newsgroup).
>
>I don't know about "both firms' websites, but a longer version of this
>press release is at:
>
>	http://www.intel.com/pressroom/archive/releases/CN102797.HTM
>
>From Terry's excerpt, it appeared that Alpha was truly dead, since
>Digital has agreed to develop future systems based on Intel's 64-bit
>microprocessor.  

Read Palmer's words again, the exact quote is:

  It ensures long-term availability of Digital's Alpha-based offerings for
  Open VMS, Digital UNIX and Windows NT customers. In addition, Digital will
  develop Digital UNIX and Windows NT offerings on the IA-64 architecture.

Notice any OS missing from that last sentence?  Anybody believe the first 
sentence given the second???  Are we all supposed to be so memory impaired 
that we do not recall the MIPS DECstations?

It gets much, much worse later on:

 . Digital will develop a full line of systems based on Intel's IA-64 
   processor family. 

Ie, the plan is to orphan Alpha systems, as with the MIPS DECstations.

 . Digital will port, with technical assistance from  Intel, its
   industry-leading Digital UNIX to Intel's IA-64. 

Ie, they will NOT port OpenVMS, giving Palmer the final excuse to kill it,
or rather, effectively terminating further development. 

 . Intel will obtain rights to manufacture and sell other non-Alpha Digital
   semiconductor products.

That sure sounds like the ARM business is going too. I suppose that there
is a remote hope that the Federal government will not approve of the
transfer, since it so blatantly adds to Intel's near monopoly in CPUs, in
particular, pretty much cedes to it the 64 bit arena. (Looks like it's time 
to write to the FTC :-( ).  If Digital wishes to acknowledge the obvious,
that its management is incapable of running any business competently, and
the CPU business in particular, well fine.  But at least consider selling 
that business to AMD or some other company that does know how to run such a
business, and that will compete with Intel head on.  I cannot see how as a
Digital customer, or in general, as a computer consumer, this deal benefits
me in any way.  Sell the business to AMD if it must be sold - they would
probably happily take on the legal action against Intel as part of the
deal. 

Moreover, 700 Million is a lot of money in absolute terms, but it's not 
very much for the business that Digital is selling.  It seems order of
magnitude to be about what Digital has invested, ie, where is the profit?
Perhaps some other company will offer more for the same sale? This reminds
me of the RDB sale, and the disk systems sale, where the sellers got a good
deal, and Digital's stockholders did not. 

Excuse me for being so pessimistic, but I expect only the worst from Mr.
Palmer, and as usual, he seems to have delivered in spades.  The timing of
this announcement was absolutely perfect - if the purpose was to lose money,
lots and lots of money. Here Digital is set to roll out Galaxies, so it 
couldn't be a better time to introduce a huge degree of uncertainty in the
Alpha's future, and all but announce that they will soon cease development
on OpenVMS. Yeah, right, who is going to buy into Galaxies under *those*
conditions?  The shift to Merced (blech) reminds me, anybody else recall
what happened to VAX sales when they started pushing Alpha before there was
anything that they could ship? 

Sign me, really, really, really disgusted,

David Mathog
