Robin Garner wrote:

> >   Under OpenVMS, the KZQSA is supported for tapes and CD-ROMs, only.
> >   No magnetic disks are supported.  Some folks have gotten this to
> >   configuration work, and some haven't -- it was not reliable, which
> >   is why it was not considered supported.
> 
> We used to have 20 or so systems with RZ28s running from KZQSAs under
> VMS 5.5-2.  One system had intermittent crashes that seemed to point
> back to this configuration, and all of them were somewhat bandwidth
> handicapped.  We moved to HSD10s shortly before upgrading to VMS 6.2.

I'd expect them to be "bandwidth handicapped."  Remember, the Qbus runs
at about 3.3Mb/sec, so that's your major limiting factor.  The SHAC
(DSSI adapter) has a private CPU interconnect, and can run much faster.
 
> We still had one system running an RZ28 from the KZQSA under 6.2,
> and it could only sustain around 20 I/Os per second before the CPU
> (a VAX 4000-505A) was saturated by interrupt stack activity, tracked
> back to one or other of the SCSI drivers using DECps.  I seem to
> remember the SCSI drivers got a major rewrite in one of the V6
> releases.

Ah, but the SCSI drivers have nothing to do with it.  A KZQSA is an MSCP
controller, and uses the same drivers as a KDA50 (SDI controller).  VMS
doesn't really see it as SCSI in the same sense as the embedded SCSI
controllers in a 3100 or 4000/60 or 90, for example.
 
> My experience in a nutshell: Under VMS 5.5, it's worth doing in
> a pinch if you're not too concerned about reliability or throughput,
> but under VMS 6, don't bother.

Uh, I have a client running such a config (VAX 4000-500, KZQSA, RRD45,
TLZ06 & 4 RZ28) running happily for several years now.  They've just
upgraded to VMS 7.1 without any difficulty whatsoever.

