In article <6358rg$ibk@gap.cco.caltech.edu>,
 <mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu> wrote:
>Read Palmer's words again, the exact quote is:
>
>  It ensures long-term availability of Digital's Alpha-based offerings for
>  Open VMS, Digital UNIX and Windows NT customers. In addition, Digital will
>  develop Digital UNIX and Windows NT offerings on the IA-64 architecture.
>
>Notice any OS missing from that last sentence?

Yes - but that's not too surprising.  OpenVMS - in anything like its
current incarnation - simply won't run on a generic
processor.  The Alpha requires PALcode to be loaded into it before
it'll run VMS, in particular.  Unless such a provision is in the IA-64,
it won't run real OpenVMS.  (You can debate how hard it'd be to make
a VMS-like OS that didn't need the processor support.)

That said, I'm not happy with the "settlement" either.  It'll help
DEC in the short term, but after a couple of years they'll be dead
in the water.

