pechter@lakewood.com (Bill Pechter) writes:

> OK Charlie Matco -- What's it all going to mean?

I'm just an interested softie, but to me the talk on this group has
been a bit too negative. Ok Intel is the biggest kid on the blockm,
but I think they really did blink first on this one. 

Before the lawsuit the game plan seemed to be to cut off all the
competition at the knees by vicious price-cutting at the low-end and
untouchable native x86 performance at the high-end. They were joking
about chasing the workstation vendors up the hill of performance, so
that when they reached the top there would be nowhere for them to go.

Well at the high-end Intel/X86 still doesn't go beyond 4-way for
almost anyone, so they weren't as close to the top of the hill as they
perhaps thought, especially since all the RISC vendors are still ahead
on floating-point, and Alpha has started to look surprisingly strong
again, especially at the end of last year with the 21264 and 21164PC
announcements, not to mention the subsequent announcement that Samsung
is gearing up to produce a million units of the 21164PC in 98. And
then you find that the plucky cloners are actually chasing Intel up
that very same hill, possibly leading them to be caught in a vice
between the real high-end and the real low-end - "Intel inside, not
the fastest, not the cheapest, but with the cutest adverts".

At this point, or so the rumour goes, Intel were becoming aware that
Merced would never fit on their _next_ process (the just launched
Tillamook type .25 process). This means the Pentium/Pro family must be
continuing to feed Intel's massive machine for years to come. And of
course Intel is in the middle of attempting a massive vendor lock-in
with proprietary slot technology i.e. they are now trying to outstrip
both the chip cloners and the m/b designers => higher r'n'd
costs. Could they, just possibly, maybe, have started to overreach
themselves?

Well I certainly think so, I think the DEC lawsuit coming when it did
called Intel's bluff. They couldn't be confident enough that they
could simultaneously a) corner the motherboard market and drive out
the cloners, b) continue to make enough money from the Pentium/PPro
family while we all wait for IA64, whilst c) actually winning the
lawsuit. It's likely that DEC's case was strong enough, at the very
least, to worry someone as paranoid as Andy Grove. It was much simpler
to just reach for the chequebook.

Whilst I'm happy for DEC, I am now even more alarmed about Intel's
power. In fact it seems to me this deal is not necessarily a good
thing from the competition point of view.

Chris
