Perhaps the US Federal Trade Comission will step in and block that deal
because it would give Intel too much of a Monopoly situation with
regards to the upcoming 64 bit chips. (OK, Alpha has been around but
you'll have to wait for MS to create a market demand for 64 bit chips
for eit her Alpha or Merced to take off).

A Reuters article quoted what looked like typical Digital propaganda. It
stipulated how Alpha ran "industry leading" Digital Unix and Windows NT
(no mention of VMS, of course). It also said that Digital would port DEC
Unix to the Intel Merced chip.

Remember when Digital unveiled basic clustering on NT "to provide
clustering right away , until MS releases the real product". ????

Well, I am getting the feeling now that:

Digital has 64 bit computing now (Alpha) until Intel comes out with its
own chip, at which point, Digital will focus on Intel architectures
only. DEC said that it would port DEC UNIX to Merced. Obviously, NT will
be ported to Merced.  Of course, no announcement about VMS.

And frankly, the market for VMS alone isn't big enough to justify the
costs of maintaining Alpha's speed up with the Intels of the world.
So once DEC moves it UNIX and NT to Intel chips, can we realistically
expect to see Alpha continue ? And if Alpha doesn't continue, what
happens of VMS ?


I think that Digital needs to make OFFICIAL and very PUBLIC commitments
for VMS right away. I think that DEC has no choice but to port VMS to
Intel.  I do not give much credence to DEC's promises it will keep up
Alpha in the long term.



