         DIGITAL SAYS INTEL AGREEMENT BOOSTS GROWTH STRATEGY

   ...Confirms Digital's dual Alpha and Intel platform strategy...

             ...Will port Digital UNIX to Intel IA 64 and
                 produce full line of IA 64 systems...
	
MAYNARD, Mass., October 27, 1997 - Digital Equipment Corporation today 
said its  agreement with Intel Corporation allows it to sharpen the 
focus on its growth strategy and  emphasize the products, services and 
solutions that enterprise customers demand.  Digital  also said the 
agreement enables it to realize all of the advantages of a platform 
strategy  that maximizes the value of its existing Alpha- and 
x86-based businesses, while  providing support for the Intel IA 64 
architecture in the future. 

"Our commitment to the leading architectures - Alpha and x86 today, 
with the  addition of IA 64 in the future -- is a key differentiator 
for Digital as it implements its  strategy," said Digital Chairman 
Robert B. Palmer.  "With this win-win agreement, we  will be able to 
provide our customers all the benefits of first-class x86 and 64-bit 
performance-oriented products and software with a clear road map to 
the future."

Digital will continue to invest in Alpha microprocessor design while 
Intel will manufacture the Alpha microprocessors to Digital's 
specifications.  Palmer said that  having Intel as a foundry for its 
Alpha technology is a more efficient business model  since it does not 
require the company to make substantial and continuing investments in  
semiconductor manufacturing.   

"The Alpha technology remains a critical element of Digital's overall 
strategy and  an important enabler to the enterprise solutions we 
deliver to customers," Palmer said.  "In fact, Digital's 
high-performance, enterprise computing platforms are more compelling  
than ever, because the company that pioneered 64-bit computing will 
provide the widest  choice of 64-bit solutions well into the 21st 
century." 

"We intend to maintain a performance and scalability advantage with 
Alpha and  are also committed to supporting these systems for as long 
as our customers need them,"  Palmer continued. "Digital will offer 
the broadest range of systems across the Alpha and  Intel 
architectures - from entry-level clients to the most powerful 64-bit 
enterprise  servers." 

As part of its strategy, Digital will: 

o Port its industry-leading 64-bit Digital UNIX operating system to 
Intel's IA 64   microprocessor architecture, for availability with the 
release of the first planned  implementation of IA 64, "Merced," 
anticipated in 1999; 
o Work with Microsoft as it develops a common programming model for 
  64-bit Windows NT for the Alpha and IA 64 architectures; 
o Develop a full line of products based on IA 64, also starting with 
  the planned  "Merced" release;  
o Continue aggressive promotion of Alpha as today's leading 64-bit 
  platform for  Digital UNIX, Open VMS and Windows NT; and 
o Enhance the company's business relationship with Intel for existing 
  x86-based products.

"Customers can be confident that whether they make a commitment to 
64-bit Windows NT or Digital UNIX, today or in the future, their 
applications will run regardless of the underlying hardware -- Alpha 
or IA 64," said Palmer.  "And our  OpenVMS customers also have 
assurance that we will continue to support OpenVMS on  Alpha well into 
the next decade. 

Digital is among the few companies that can deliver robust Internet 
business  solutions that comprise leadership technology, high 
performance products, global service  and support and complimentary 
partnerships with industry leading software companies,  which
allows us to solve complex computing problems. 

"These are the attributes that distinguish Digital," said Palmer. 

Digital Equipment Corporation, recognized for product and service 
excellence, is  a leading supplier of high-performance,
Web-based computing solutions which help  enterprises compete in the 
global marketplace.  Digital gives its customers a winning  Internet 
advantage through a comprehensive portfolio of Internet solutions 
based on  award-winning systems, advanced networking infrastructure, 
innovative software, and  industry applications - including those from 
its business partners.  The expertise and experience of Digital 
employees help customers plan, design, implement, manage and  support 
Internet solutions in countries throughout the world.  For the
latest company  information, visit Digital on the World Wide Web at 
http://www.digital.com 
