In article <62ksmg$799@scotsman.ed.ac.uk>, Baron Von Myles writes:
:Well, thanks to all those who responded to my last post. It looks like
:either my VR290 cable or monitor is bust. I managed to get a raster again
:by cracking-open the VR290 and blowing out dust/re-seating connectors, but
:still no text. VCB02 LEDs are on-off-on - "keyboard error" - and all LK201
:LEDs stay on after power-up.
:
:As this is a gifted home system, trying spares is not an option.
:
:So, now I'd like to hook up a terminal. I've read the OVMS and MV FAQs, but
:I'm still a bit fuzzy on some points. Any further assistance would be
:appreciated.
:
:[1]	I have a BC16E-10 cable. One end has what looks like a DB9, though
:	the system BOM states MMJ (I've never seen a MMJ. Is it DB9?).
:	The DB25 end says console. This cable was for connecting the LN03.

  The BC16 is a DECconnect cable.  You apparently have one or two
  DECconnect adapters already connected to this cable, as both of
  the ends are small square plastic MMJ connectors.

  If you have adapters connected, you'll have to include the part
  numbers.  (There is a list of common MMJ adapter part numbers in
  the OpenVMS FAQ.)

  The MMJ is a member of the family of modular phone jacks designed
  a while back, with the locking tab offset to the side to prevent
  the connector from being inadvertently inserted into a phone jack
  and subsequently getting the electronics zapped by a ring signal.

  For the MMJ and DB9 pinouts, please see the OpenVMS FAQ.

:	QUESTION: Is this a console or printer cable? From what I've seen,
:	these are different (some internal jumpers).

  Without knowing which adapters are connected to the MMJ cable,
  it's hard to say...

:[2]	QUESTION: What is the DB25-end pin-out. Standard RS232 or such?

  Yes, it is standard.  You should be able to get it to work with
  four or six wires -- or 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,20 if you're gonzo.  (I'd
  expect 1,2,3,7 would be the close to the minimum needed.)  In any
  case, the DECconnect adapters deal with this for you.

:[3]	Has anyone got a WYSE75 to work as a console? Is a NULL modem
:	required?

  I've switched (for obvious reasons) to DECconnect, as it makes most
  of this stuff -- except for modem signals themselves -- irrelevent.

:	More importantly and irrespective of what terminal type I use,
:	though I now have my manuals I can't find anything about the
:	serial protocol (parity, stop bits, flow control - and do
:	I need to wire-out the DTR, CTS lines).
:	QUESTIONS: What should these be set to?

  The host isn't that fussy, I'd try 4800 or 9600 baud (see the console
  rotary dial switch for the speed setting), eight bits no parity,
  XON/XOFF flow control, 1 stop bit.  Plug it in, and try it -- tap
  <BREAK> a few times and see if you can get the >>> prompt.  I'd not
  try to use baud rates higher than 9600 baud.  (I don't have this
  particular configuration, but I'm using a serial terminal on another
  OpenVMS workstation system.)

:[4]	How robust are the console UARTS (assuming UARTS...)? Easily replaced?

  Donno, I can't remember the last one I've blown.

:Sorry about all these extra questions, but I don't want to experiment lest
:I bust the serial console too by crossing wires and then leaving it connected
:for long periods while I faff about with terminal protocol settings.

  I'd use the DECconnect adapters listed in the OpenVMS FAQ, if I were
  skittish or lazy, or if I had better things to do than soldering and
  running down pinout documentation.

  The FAQ URL:
	ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms

