No doubt. A few snippets from today's SKD...

DIGITAL AND INTEL "JUST SAY NO" TO LAWYERS, SETTLE DISPUTE

Copyright (c) 1997 by Terry C. Shannon

In a widely anticipated development, Digital Equipment Corporation and
Intel Corporation disclosed a settlement of their high-profile lawsuits
and counterclaims. In a press release dated October 27, DEC and Intel
announced that they have agreed to establish �a broad-based business
relationship that will provide significant short- and long-term benefits
to both companies and their customers.�

The terms of the settlement are entirely consistent with the quid pro
quo approach outlined in the last issue of SKD see �The Silly Season,�
SKD V4N20, October 16, 1997). Opting to take the fabless route to
microprocessor production, DEC sold its Fab-6 plant to Intel. DEC also
got the technology cross-licensing agreement it has sought since last
May (see �Digital v. Intel: Andy Gets a Big Surprise,� SKD V4N10, May
15, 1997). Intel gained contractually-legitimized access to Alpha
technology� especially the cache management and branch prediction
capabilities at the heart of the May 12 lawsuit, and both vendors will
benefit from a quick and dignified de-escalation of hostilities. 

A Win-Win Scenario

In a teleconference held earlier today, DEC CEO Bob Palmer and Intel COO
Craig Barrett expressed their satisfaction with the pact. Bob Palmer
made it clear that he remains confident which architecture is �best,� at
least from the standpoint of processing power. �Alpha will maintain
substantial performance advantage in some applications,� said Palmer,
adding that the marketplace will make the ultimate decision.

No Omega for Alpha

According to Palmer, DEC will continue to enhance Alpha through multiple
implementations. The [Alpha] roadmap is unchanged and we remain
confident on execution.� Intel�s acquisition of Fab-6 �doesn�t
fundamentally change our strategy but it gives us greater flexibility
and cost reduction,� claimed the CEO. This statement is consistent with
the remarks DEC VP Jesse Lipcon made on the subject three weeks ago at
the DECUS Europe venue in Copenhagen.

Palmer also said the Intel pact will not affect DEC�s relationship with
Samsung, Mitsubishi, or AMD. Citing the benefits of the fabless
microprocessor model, Palmer noted that Sun Microsystems has been very
successful with this approach.


A Pair of Wild Cards

While today�s settlement was consistent with SKD�s expectations, it
included a couple of surprises. Digital will relinquish control of the
StrongARM architecture, as well as PCI and networking chips, to Intel.
Perhaps more importantly, DEC announced plans to port Digital UNIX to
the IA64 architecture.

�It is our intention that the StrongARM product and the design engineers
associated with that will become part of Intel,� said Mr. Palmer. This
represents an abrupt change from the �StrongARM tactics� that Digital
has employed for the past two years. Palmer also indicated that Intel
will take custody of DEC�s PCI, networking, and graphics chips. 


DEC�s announcement that it will, with the assistance of Intel, port
Digital UNIX to the IA64 architecture caught many observers by surprise.
Although this development was overlooked by many of the analysts who
participated in the teleconference, proliferating Digital UNIX beyond
the Alpha architecture has several important ramifications. Indeed, SKD
believes that the Digital UNIX porting announcement is the most
intriguing aspect of the agreement. 

DUNIX: Not Just for Alpha Anymore


Porting an OS to a foreign hardware architecture is a non-trivial task,
as Digital UNIX (and OpenVMS) developers are fully aware. Nevertheless,
DEC is fully committed to the IA64 port. Indeed, the firm began initial
prototyping prior to the commencement of legal hostilities, and one
might speculate that DUNIX has been successfully prototyped on IA32 to
prove the technical feasibility of an IA64 port. (SKD has reason to
believe that DEC has booted DUNIX on IA32 hardware, but we�re confident
that the 32-bit OS will not be offered as a product). 


Have It Your Way

During the teleconference, Bob Palmer alluded to DEC�s willingness to
license Digital UNIX to the industry. Yeaton went a step further by
stating that DEC not only is willing to license the OS, but will
actively promote its adoption on an a la carte or soups-to-nuts basis.
Potential OEMs will be able to license source code and binaries for the
base OS,   as well as value-added features such as the Advanced File
System �all the way through TruCluster software if you want to target
the enterprise server market.�

Two Birds, One Stone

Today�s agreement addresses two key concerns long expressed by the
1analyst community. Both the Alpha architecture and Digital UNIX exhibit
leadership technological attributes, but both products have been
hamstrung by questionable business models. In the case of Alpha, the
considerable expense of maintaining an organic semiconductor fabrication
capability has led observers to question DEC�s ability to sustain the
architecture through multiple future generations. On the Unix front,
Digital�s target market is limited to the Alpha installed base. By
adopting a fabless manufacturing model and rendering Digital UNIX
compatible with a mainstream, high-volume processor architecture,
Digital has killed two birds with one stone.

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC



