Message-ID: <34558464.581349F2@tonys.ekology.umn.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 285475659
Organization: University of Minnesota
Newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec


Hi

Here are the results of a lattice QCD benchmark, written
mostly in C (Robert Harley wrote the optimized Alpha/RISC
arithmetic routines), on a large working dataset (> 64M).
Of course, this is just ONE particular benchmark, using a
calculation that has no application in the real world...
It is a very FPU and memory intensive calculation on a 
large 4 dimensional spacetime lattice, and it is portable
to MPP and SMP architectures:

(all numbers in MFlops)

16 node T3E-900:         2080
Dual PII-300MHz, SDRAM:   304    (NT)
500MHz Alpha              245    (unix)
PII-300MHz, SDRAM         163    (NT)
PII-300MHz, EDO RAM       150    (NT)

The Intel code uses x86 FPU assembly... the T3E and
Alpha code uses Robert Harley's excellent hand-tuned C,
the T3E uses SHMEM communication... The T3E version was
not particularly tweaked, and could possibly be made faster 
yet. The source code is about 14000 lines.

As I said before, I am not claiming that this is a particularly
good benchmark... it is just an interesting comparison of
completely different architectures, using a single source code
that is substantially similar on all platforms.

Henley



