galagun@gauss.isrc.sandia.gov (Glenn A. Laguna) writes:

>"C. J. Tan" <cjtan@acs.ucalgary.ca> writes:

>> To be more specific, I am looking for a number cruncher machine.  The
>> machine will also be used for development.  Most of the stuff that I write
>> are either in C or C++.  I am picking up Ada, Java and also OpenGL.  Which
>> machine should I go for?  A Sparc or an SGI or an Alpha?  What are price
>> ranges that I will be looking at for these machines?  It'd be best to be a
>> used machine as I can't afford an expensive machine (I am a student).  
>> 
>> I know we don't live in an ideal world, but ideally I'd like a machine
>> that is most trouble-free and I don't need so much constant upgrades.
>> 

>Other thing to consider is reliability and service. While SGI's may have the
>"coolness factor", dealing with them as a company has been a nightmare. Our
>maintenance on an Indigo 2 costs more per year than a good PC, and the
>maintenance is essential because of the high rate of disk failures. SGI's
>development software is quite expensive We can't even get a consistent story
>about what they think we've paid for and we haven't. And we have 120
>SGI's. How's a single user going to deal with this?

I've not had any such problems with our Indigo2s.  With 120 machines (versus
our four Indigo2s and 2 Indigos) you are likely to see more.  However, if
the only reason you are keeping maintaince is to replace disk drives, you
should drop off maintaince and buy the disk drives yourselves.  A good 9GB
Seagate or IBM can be had for $1,100 and has a five year warrenty.

SGI should know what you paid for, but so should you.  Your purchasing
department should have records off all your purchases and copies of each
quote.

An easy way to avoid these questions is through site license.  I don't know
what contracts there are for government, but for education there is Varsity. 
$44,000 would have licensed almost every product for 125 machines.

>For my money, an Alpha running Linux is the way to go.

For simple number crunching, SGIs carry around a lot of extra weight.  An
O200 is a very good deal, though.

For my money, I use the resources of my institution.  I have a hard time
seeing why a student needs a number cruncher.  Unless the project at hand is
recreational, he should be able to access good resources at his university.

Best
-- 
Benjamin Z. Goldsteen



