On Mon, 27 Oct 1997 04:35:49 GMT, Terry Kennedy <terry@spcuna.spc.edu> wrote:
>In comp.sys.dec Matt Bancroft <matt@MEC.Edu> wrote:
>> I ran across a couple of old DECServer 200 8port terminal servers forsale,
>> probably well suited for getting a lab of old Mac Pluses online via dumb
>> terminal service.
>>
>> Is it possible to run the decserver off a linux box or do i need a vax
>> VMS box?
>
>  These only speak LAT (a proprietary DEC protocol). You can boot them from
>various systems (there's a Unix loader available, DEC used to sell one for
>PC's, and I wrote one for RSTS/E) but you need a host that speaks LAT for
>them to do anything useful. I don't know of any free LAT implementations.

Not only does the DECserver 200 not do telnet, It also does not do
bi-directional hardware flow control.  The "terminal" can flow off
the server, but the server cannot flow off the "terminal."  If you
have a modem connected, rather than a terminal, this is IMHO fatal.
XON/XOFF just doesn't cut it and hasn't since about the time the
DS200 was first manufactured.  Best not to waste any money on that
old dinosaur Matt.
 
- Dan



