(Christian J. Bauer) wrote:

 <much snipped out>
Just some "alternate uses" for some of the gear mentioned.
A little unusual perhaps, but if the price is right..... :)
>Some Values as listed in the DDA Trader (October '97):
> VAX 6310 no LIC   300 US$  
We run our email server, web and ftp site on one of these.  Donated.
"No commercial value" was the statement at the time.. 
True enough.  However........:)

> Also the 6xxx Machines are HUGE, HEAVVY 
Can't argue with that, it took six of us and a ute to move it. 
Done that twice now. Not a lot of fun, it's a tight fit through most
normal doorways..
>and requre 3 phase outlets
Well if you are technically inclined, or know someone that is, that's
not real hard to get around. It was 3 phase 415vac when I got hold of
it. CLUE: DEC boxes are overengineered beyond belief, the ratings on
the data plate are what the supply is capable of delivering, but it
would be a heavily optioned box that needed the 5vdc @ 300amps the
supplies are capable of.
> and a climate control.
Well, a room with air conditioning anyway, doesn't have to be as fancy
as DEC specify. If you are comfortable, the machine should be.
(a 2hp room a/c works fine here, and we have 40c+ summers.)
Theres a pic of this setup at
www.stmarks.pp.catholic.edu.au/icon/stmarks/thevax.jpg 
> A 6310 draws about 6A/360V without any drives.
Must have had a LOT of cards in it, ours draws 2A from a 240vac single
phase outlet.  (After I threw out the autotransformer and modded the
300vdc supply a bit) Dead easy if you have a 240ac mains.
It has 64mb, 1 cpu, an ethernet card and a CI connector.

> Also, most RA drives require 3 Phase outlets
Noooo,  the CABINETS have 3 phase connections actually.
Ours RA81 & 82 drives have an IEC 240vac plug like a toaster.
So a domestic power point is adequate to run a couple. They pull just
under 3 amps each when running, maybe a bit more when they are
starting up.
If you have RA7x drives, they fit into a bank of four that also has an
IEC 240ac connection. Again the SA550 cabinet has a 3 phase
connection, but we don't use it.
> and usually are connected to a HSC
Usually, yes, thats what we are using, however a KDB50 card will allow
the VAX to talk directly to 4 SDI drives.
We have a HSC50 that talks to our 5 RA70's and 3 RA72's. 
The HSC50 is also a 3phase supply, again it's not real hard to make it
run off single  phase outlets, it draws about 2.5A from a 240vac
single phase outlet.
Those who have 110vac as domestic supply would have more difficulty,
but it is still possible. 
>These drives are NOT designed to run at home!
Have to agree, but you CAN do it.  I ran this system at my place for
well over a year, (not continuously, I'd own shares in the Electricity
Trust) when we first got it (about 3 years ago) and were trying to
figure out what to do with it.
At that time the boot disk was an RA81. I still have it but the 70
series are smaller and lighter, not to mention quieter..
Naturally, only normal domestic single phase power outlets were
available. Unburdened by the knowledge that it couldn't be done, we
made it go, most of the trouble was with the software.
A  "home" system with a 6xxx box, a KDB50 and 2-4 RA7x drives. (and a
terminal or two of course) could be a fascinating project if you like
that sort of thing.
We use a salvaged (from a scrap metal dealer) IBM PS/30-286
running DOS 6.22 and Telemate as a VT100 console, plus a couple of
VT320's I scored at the same site. They were happy to part with them
for $10 each. the whole lot would pull a bit less than 1800 watts.
Not the sort of thing you would run all day every day perhaps, but not
unreasonable for a few hours a day here and there. 
I still have a stack of RA8x's and a TK78/TU78 Dual 1/2" Tape drive
that we needed a fork lift to move at home, it needs a couple of 5v
supplies replaced. The Tape channel cards are in the HSC.
This one needs a 15a single phase outlet though. (Big air compressor)
(Any unloved H7440's out there?)
One HSC50 minus most of the channel cards.. (I keep it for spares, for
the one at work) so far haven't needed any.
Plus a 1987 vintage VAX 7580 Minus the console hard disk, and console
to VAX cable.  I am led to believe it will run some form of BSD/.
One day maybe......:)
Yes I probably am nuts, what the heck, it was fun...

Cheers


