Jason Patterson wrote:
> 
> Minor miscommunication: I meant that I wouldn't use an SGI as a 
> firewall because of SGI's poor track record with respect to security 
> (lots of buffer overflow bugs etc), not because of the initial 
> out-of-the-box configuration.

The two parts of the phrase amount to exactly the same, for a firewall.
The Firewall setup *doesn't* have these programs waiting for buffer
overflow attacks. E.g. a gauntlet config trashes *everything* in the bin
that it doesn't trust. Amongst others, it has it's own SMTP gateway
(well, we all know how `trustworthy' large programs like sendmail are,
regardless of vendor).

I wouldn't trust any *other* vendor's full-featured config as a firewall
anyway; whether they may have less CERT alerts applying to holes in
their OSes or not, there *are* going to be a lot of them around anyway,
unless you chuck out all the bits the firewall doesn't need nad audit
the rest. And a fully-featured machine will have things like sendmail
floating around, because it lacks functionality if it doesn't.


