>ali@iriquois.eel.ufl.edu (Ali Anghaie) writes:

>For my part, IRIX 6 OS realy better than Solaris. My IRIX 6.4 machines
>had never crashed, and the admin tools are realy simple to use and powerfull.

Only if you have a very small number of users.  My IRIX 6.4 box
currently has (grep :20: /etc/passwd | wc -l) 1368 registered users.
The graphical account management tools are useless with this number of
accounts.  Besides, I'd much rather have my own scripts, so that I
know exactly what is going on.  I find it a lot easier to type:

new.user foo

and then answer a few questions... the script then checks the user
doesn't already have an account under a different ID, creates the
account, on multiple machines if necessary, automatically allocates a
suitable disk quota, gives them a standard set of dot files,
integrates their account with the university's central mail system if
possible, and finally prints out a personalised welcoming letter
telling them their user ID and intitial password, which I just stuff in
an envelope and send off to the appropriate department.

>More over, of admin files are standard and vi-able.

That's true.  I recently started admining a DEC box, and am finding
navigating my way around /etc a pain in the arse.  Why couldn't they
have stuck with a fairly standard SYS V arrangement, such as Debian Linux,
IRIX and Solaris all have?

Tim.



