In article <62q6qi$h0u$1@darla.visi.com>,
Steve Kappel <steve.kappel@iname.com> wrote:
>In article <62nkbd$s0e$1@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>,
>	bobd@araminta.acs.ohio-state.edu (Bob DeBula) writes:
>>In article <344ECAA1.167E@cartsys.com>, Ben Cannon  <ben@cartsys.com> wrote:
>>>Jason Patterson wrote:
>>>
>>>In IRIX 5 days (the current IRIX is 6.4) GCC would not be of much use
>>
>>IRIX 6.4 is the current release of IRIX for *certain* SGI boxes
>>(Origin200, Origin2000, Onyx2, OCTANE). IRIX 6.3 is the current release
>>for O2. IRIX 6.2 is the current release for most (perhaps all?) other
>>SGI boxes with R4000 or greater processors (including the Indy series).
>>R3000 based SGIs can't go beyond IRIX 5.3 (and some of those may not be
>>able to run IRIX 5.3).
>
>I don't know much about IRIX but this seems to be pretty undesirable.
>Is this a short-term situation until they have another "merged
>release" or is this SOP (Standard Operating Procedure) for SGI?

Yes, it is a pretty short term approach, as I understand it. It mainly
came about (again my perception) as a rapid way of getting some fairly
radical new hardware designs into the field quickly. They are supposed
to get a consolidated release into the trenches Real Soon Now. IRIX 6.2
was, for a brief time, such a consolidated release, that ran on all the
fielded current production SGI workstation (disregarding SGI "Cray"s)
machines for a seemingly brief while (until the new machines: O2,
Origin, Octane came out). Of course, new radical hardware in the 
future will probably generate similar situations again at some future
date after the consolidated release (these "one size fits all" releases
seem to be short lived).

The problem is, in large University environments like ours (~400 SGIs
sprinkled about campus of which I am *aware*) we'll have (large numbers
of) machines running IRIX 5.3, IRIX 6.2, IRIX 6.3 and IRIX 6.4 well
into the next millenium even after a consolidated OS release comes out.
We try to provide support for the more reasonable/prevalent releases
(IRIX 5.3, 6.2, 6.3, & 6.4 at the moment).  We still do have dark, dank
corners (not unlike Igor's lab) where machines running 4.0.5, 5.0.X,
5.1, 5.2, and 6.1 still exist and we do get calls with consultation
questions from these folks periodically (that's my Halloween horror
story and I'm sticking to it :-).  I find the machines running IRIX
5.0.X and 5.1 to be the ones that really send chills up my spine when I
take the consultation calls.  :-)

Realistically, we try to keep actual equipment running 5.3, 6.2, and
6.3 at the moment (an Indigo R4000, and Indy, and an O2; bottom of the
line machines each and every one) for support purposes. We can't afford
(or at least can't present budgetary justication for) anything to run
6.4.  I keep trying to convince our SGI reps that we really need an
Octane (bottom of the line, natch) for support purposes and that it'd be
in their own interest to see their way to loaning/gifting us one, but
they don't seem to buy it any more than our internal management and
bean counters do. :-)




