In article <62q6qi$h0u$1@darla.visi.com>,
Steve Kappel <steve.kappel@iname.com> wrote:
>>IRIX 6.4 is the current release of IRIX for *certain* SGI boxes
>>(Origin200, Origin2000, Onyx2, OCTANE). IRIX 6.3 is the current release
>>for O2. IRIX 6.2 is the current release for most (perhaps all?) other
>>SGI boxes with R4000 or greater processors (including the Indy series).
>>R3000 based SGIs can't go beyond IRIX 5.3 (and some of those may not be
>>able to run IRIX 5.3).
>
>I don't know much about IRIX but this seems to be pretty undesirable.
>Is this a short-term situation until they have another "merged
>release" or is this SOP (Standard Operating Procedure) for SGI?

No, it's a temporary situation until the new cross-platform
version (6.5) appears.  I believe that all machines with an R4000 or
better will be able to run 6.5.  The reason for the plethora at the
moment seems to be widely different architectures; IRIX 6.2 was
effectively cross-platform for the machines available at the time.

IRIX 6.3 is O2 specific, and 6.4 is for machines using the newer S2MP
architecture.  It is the first generation of so-called 'Cellular IRIX'
and has a number of features not found in the other versions of 6.x.

I suppose they could have brought out "IRIX 6.2 for Origin" and "IRIX
6.2 for O2" instead, but they'd still be the same as they are now.
From the point of view of the vast majority of programs, the various
flavours of IRIX 6.x are virtually identical (I certainly have common
software disks mounted by machines running all three flavours of 6.x)

From my point of view they are sufficiently similar that I don't have
too many problems, although scheduling is completely different between
IRIX 6.4 and other versions of 6.x; npri(1) has become more or less
obsolete, and they have changed nice so that it actually works.  :-)

Tim.



