Greeting,
The question you pose is a very complex one. The AXP 2100's and now the
4100's are know to be middle of the road systems when comparing unlike
systems like DEC vs. IBM. The 4100 would be a good machine if you were
going from a smaller or similar, say IBM AS400 or RS6000, or even just
replacing the Tamden, but when you get to the heavy iron like a IBM 9xxx
class of process, I would look at DEC's heavy iron like the AXP8400
class of processors, running the 440mhz or the 622mhz with 4 to 6
processors.

Looking at just the specs. of transactions per second and mip rating or
what ever comparision you look at, the large systems from IBM have a
very large thru-put from there I/O devices, and that is where the DEC
machine are lacking. The I/O rates for a unix based box are at max
40mbs, and that is using the new ultra scsi technology. It is very tough
for a DEC platform to compete with a mainframe when a total package like
the one you are replacing, because the of the I/O.

The software package you spoke of, Oracle, is a nice package, but is a
cpu hog and if you whole ideal is to have your company use Oracle, then
going with a AXP is the way to go, you need to decide on the processor
and I/O type. My largest suggestion is for you to speak to a
professional consulating agency that is not biased toward any one
platform.
Regards,
Tim L.



