C. J. Tan wrote:
> 4. Sun Sparcs are not such a good machine. Alpha's are better ...

I strongly disagree with this, especially for software development and 3D
graphics. I use all 3 platforms all day every day for serious software
development work, and the debugger in Sun's C++ Workshop beats the other
platforms by miles for serious work, because of the memory reference tracking,
thread support, and the visual data inspector. But a decent SPARC machine is
out of your price range.

> Java codes are best on Sparc's (both are by Sun).

Yes, Java is currently best supported on SPARC (and x86 of course). But be
aware that Java virtual machines are still in their early days, and
newer/better JIT technology is being rolled out every day. So you should
expect the JIT's from the various companies to continually be leapfrogging
each other for at least the next couple of years. Comparing them in detail
right _now_ is a bit silly (like comparing 2 cooks both half way through
preparing a meal).

> My question is: will an SGI Indigo with R4600 133Mhz be comparable to an
> Alpha 333Mhz??

A 333 MHz Alpha 21164 will blow away a 133 MHz R4600. The 21164 is a current
generation processor, whereas the R4600 is a previous generation processor
(and a low end one at that). Its not a fair competition at all, since the
R4600 is 18 months older (R4600 is early 1994, Alpha21164 is mid 1995). A
21164@333 is about 5 times the speed of an R4600@133.

> At this point, I am truly confused!  However, I have found a few machines
> (used) that are in my price range (not more than US$2500).

It looks like I overestimated your budget by a _long_ way. Sorry about that.

For $2500 it seems to me that the only way you can get a *current* machine is
to buy a 2nd hand Alpha 21164. Even then, I can't see how you can afford to
buy DEC's compilers (even with edu discount), so you'll have to put up with
GNU gcc/g++/g77. This leaves you with the relatively boring Digital UNIX
environment and the GNU development tools with their disappointing Alpha
performance. Either that or WindowsNT. For $2500 I can't see any other options.

A 2nd hand UltraSPARC is too expensive for you, and so is a 2nd hand SGI O2.
That leaves the previous generations from Sun (SPARCstation20) or SGI (Indy),
and no company's previous machines would compete with a current design
(although a quad hyperSPARC SPARCstation20 can match a current Pentium-II for
heavy FP work). Even if you did buy an old SGI Indy you couldn't afford SGI's
compilers (and gcc is not really an option on SGI's).


