> the top one is part number an m7606, then comes a m7608-bs.  then
> there are m7504 and m7546, m3106 and m7555. (these are all packed into
> the top slots, the rest are empty..)

7606 is the processor board
7608 is a 4MB memory board (plus 1MB on the 7606, yeilding 5MB total.)
7546 is a tape controller of some kind (perhaps TK50)
7555 is the ESDI drive controller
I think the 7504 card is an ethernet controller, and the 
3106 is probably some type of async serial controller.

> also, the disk is an rd53, 

As I recall this is a 71MB ESDI disk made by Micropolis.

> turning it on makes the display on the back flash between 9 and f.

Sounds like it might be looking for a console terminal.  Here's 
a listing of what the LED display meanings:
        F       Waiting for DCOK
        E       Waiting for POK
        D       ROM checksum test
        C       Searching for minimum required RAM
        B       Accessing Q-bus
        A       Testing video monitor (if VAXstation)
        9       Identifying console terminal
        8       Language inquiry
        7       Testing RAM
        6       More RAM tests
        5       Testing Q-bus mapping
        4       Testing CPU and registers
        3       Testing interrupts
        2       Searching for boot device
        1       Loading secondary boot
        0       Booting ...

> i'd quite like to get parts to make a serial cable to go between this
> and a pc (or just get a serial cable to do this)...any suggestions on

The Microvax II's 9-pin serial port is wired differently than a
PC's 9-pin serial port.   The pins are:
        1 - Protective ground
        2 - Transmit data
        3 - Receive data
        4 - RTS
        5 - DTR
        6 - DSR
        7 - Signal Ground

To make a simple 9-to-25 cable, connect: 2-2, 3-3, 7-7
Unless I have this NULL, in which case it would be: 2-3, 3-2, 7-7

Here's a couple URLs that might be helpful:
http://www.anglia.ac.uk/~systimk/History/Vaxes.Txt
http://www.neurotica.com/dec-info/

Hope this helps,
anthony

