In comp.sys.dec Ethan Dicks <dicks@math.ohio-state.edu> wrote:
> Unless I've dropped through a hole into a parallel universe, recently, I
> remember seeing a web page about a product that I can no longer find any
> information about.  It's a solid-state (RAM) disk that can be fitted with
> a personality module for SCSI or SMD or even RL01/RL02 compatibility.  I
> spent quite a bit of time searching with AltaVista with *no* hits.

  The Setasi/DDS folks (last seen in Florida, and didn't have a web page)
made an RP-series emulator, but it wasn't a RAM disk, just a smallish SCSI
disk with a Massbus adaptor.

  Quantum got DEC's solid state disk products (ESE's) along with the rest
of the DEC disk/tape lines. They have some info on SCSI ones on their web
page.

  OTOH, I put a Seagate ST31200 ("Hawk") drive on an 11/70 3 years ago,
using a CMD Unibus controller, and it's still in the system cabinet running
with absolutely no maintenance since then. The only thing we did do was re-
place the 11/70 with a QED-95 in the 11/70 box, and then move that QED-95
into an 11/44 box to get a more modern power supply and smaller cabinet.
So even a clunky SCSI disk is better than RM05's.

