No central organization? Um, no.

Linux is a bit different because if you look close enough, you'll
relize that there is a difference between the "OS" and the
distribution. The kernel is centrally administered by Linus
himself. There are plenty of "unofficial" patches, but most people
stick to the official releases blessed by the man himself.  Hence,
when one person says "2.0.30" -- everyone will agree what kernel
revision they're talking about.

The suite of software that you hear about coming with linux is
depedant on the distribution. At the moment, Redat seems to be in the
favorite seat. Good packaging, clean install, and if you buy their
distribution, you get tech support and manuals too. Be on the lookout
for Redhat Unleashed 2nd Ed.  later this year, early next year.

As compared to some of the other high end workstations like SGI's --
apples and oranges. You're never going to see a Reality Engine on a
$2k PC clone. SMP support in IRIX is also pretty spiffy stuff. OTOH,
if the user is planning to do some coding, maybe play doom, and just
have a machine to dink with -- I'd call you a fool for dropping more
than $2k on a box. Does that mean a PC is a toy? No -- like all
hardware, some machines are better suited for some tasks than others..

I think the original poster requested a box that was fast with
floating point. Whether one platform is better than another, I dunno
for sure. Lots of contradictory statements in the thread. But being a
student, I think it's reasonable to say that he/she'll wait an extra
30 minutes for something to run rather than pay another $3k for a
faster box.

I know I would...

-Steve


