Mark L. Wencek (mwencek@kersur.net) wrote:
: This may seem like a non-sensical questions, but...

: Is there anyway to have 2 graphics adapters of
: different types in an AlphaStation400;  the first would
: be "turned-on" for NT Workstation 4.0 usage, the second
: would be "turned-on" DEC Unix/Linux usage under Redhat.

: The problem stems from the fact that I have an Oxygen202
: from Dynamic Pictures which only has drivers for NT, and
: I would like that card "enabled" when the system has
: the env_var "OS_TYPE" set to "NT".  The other card would
: be a DEC ZLxp-E1 (or some other card) which would be enabled
: when the system was to booted into D-Unix or Redhat Linux.
: Enabling/disabling the cards/hardware slots manually would
: be acceptable.

: This is not a question about multi-head configurations,
: but rather something analagous to the way the EISA
: hardware configuration tools allowed selective enabling
: and disabling of cards in particular slots on the EISA
: motherboard.

: Thanks in advance for the help,


This is doable but certainly NOT supported!  Here are the caveats though.

You can only have one VGA-enabled card in the system.  The SRM console will 
come up ONLY on the VGA-enabled card. NT AlphaBios or the ARC console will
come up ONLY on the VGA-enabled card.  Newer PowerStorm 3D30 i.e TGA2
cards have a jumper to disable or enable VGA mode.

If you have Oxygen202 card with VGA enabled and PowerStorm with VGA disabled,
then you will be able to do what you are asking for.  For UNIX you have
to generate a specific system image (i.e. tailored kernel) and you can 
decide which options are included.  Similarly you can set up NT hardware
profile.  You would exclude the *other* card in the appropriate kernel.

When system comes up in the console, it will use the Oxygen202 card
(assuming it has VGA enabled).  When the XServer starts, it will switch
display to the PowerStorm.  You probably will have to set up 
auto-boot as for the period when UNIX switches the driver and until
XServer starts you may not have the display at all.

--
Vikas Sontakke				sontakke@helix.enet.dec.com




