>At this point, I am truly confused!  However, I have found a few machines
>(used) that are in my price range (not more than US$2500).  All of them
>are either SGI's or Alpha's.  My question is: will an SGI Indigo with
>R4600 133Mhz be comparable to an Alpha 333Mhz??  If yes, how can that be?

No such thing as an Indigo (unless it is a lab prototype) with an
R4600; An "Indy R4600", yes, an "Indigo R4600", no. The Indigo series
was available with the R3000, the R4000, and the R4400 (at least as an
upgrade anyway, don't remember if they shipped R4400 based Indigos,
though they probably did).  Per my memory, the Indy came with an R4000,
R4600, or R5000 .

>I am at a university and I have been told that there are some educational
>pricing for some of SGI's software for educational use.  Are there such
>things with the Digital Unix?  

There is a program called "CSLG" offered by DEC. In our case, our
computing organization partially subsidizes that program and we base it
upon approximately 200 subscribers to CSLG.  I don't negotiate the
agreements or participate much in that, but I've gotten the impression
that it might require some volume committment by the educational
institution to participate or to get decent pricing. Trying to buy it
one-off, if your institution doesn't participate in CSLG, might be
expensive or infeasible.

About the SGI Varsity Program, from a previous posting of mine to
"comp.sys.sgi.marketplace" back last March:

=========================================================================
Subject:      Re: Educational pricing?
From:         bobd@araminta.acs.ohio-state.edu (Bob DeBula)
Date:         1997/03/26
Newsgroups:   comp.sys.sgi.marketplace

Er, it kind of depends on which package level of Varsity Program it is
of which you speak.  Participation at the "Developer" level (IDO -- C,
C++, etc.) definitely includes NFS. For definitive information, I
suggest looking at SGI Varsity Program web pages at URL:

        http://www.sgi.com/silicon_campus/varsity_swp.html

The SGI "Higher Education" web page is at URL:

        http://www.sgi.com/Products/highered.html

They used to have three packagings for Varsity Program participation:
The "User Package", the "Developer Package", and the "Power Package".
In actuality, I suspect most folks ended up choosing to participate at
the "Developer Package" level. I note that they don't seem to list the
"User Package" anymore on the web page, so maybe they've discontinued
that. The "Power Package" seems in reality to be an extra cost add-on
to the Developer Package to provide for purchasing multiprocessor
versions of C and FORTRAN (I seem to recall the Power Packages is sold
in 5 packs).

The way SGI packages the Varsity Program is somewhat unique in my
experience though, in that there is a "Right to Use" component and a
"Support" component.  The initial cost (usually on a per machine basis)
for machines at participating qualified educational institutions is
usually the charge for the "Right to Use" component and the current
years' (whenever your institution negotiated it's beginning and end --
ours ends July 1st of any given year) "Support" component. The
"Support" component expires, but the "Right to Use" doesn't, so in
*subsequent* years, all you *should* (as the I understand the program
to work at present) have to buy is the "Support" component. To minimize
confusion, we tend to call these "Varsity Program renewals" locally.
Your institution has to sign a contract/agreement with SGI and they
have to purchase the components in "packs" (I believe the minimum at
the Developers level is a 25 pack). Your institution may well also add
an overhead charge (ours is purchased through our campus bookstore).

If you want current and definitive information, I would suggest
contacting SGI and examining the web resources listed above. I hope
this helps a bit.

More than you ever wanted to know about the Varsity Program, I'm
sure.... :-)

==========================================================================




