Followup-To: comp.arch
Organization: Silicon Graphics Inc., Mountain View, CA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.arch,comp.sys.dec


Nick Maclaren wrote:
> 
> I hope that we are agreed that garbage collection WITHOUT pointer checking
> is a recipe for chaos, sooner or later :-)
> 
Agreed.  But so is explicit deallocation without pointer checking.  In
my experience, more so.  Not only can I introduce memory addressing
errors that don't show up until much later, but I can also introduce
deallocation errors with the same symptoms.  And I often have a harder
time using higher level linked data structures that might get me away
from the dangerous low-level pointer manipulations.

Perhaps a more interesting question is whether test-time pointer
checking techniques that are run only during testing (e.g. Purify, or C
pointer-arithmetic-checking as described for example in my 1996 PLDI
paper) are good enough, or whether you need the expense of full-time
pointer checking.  It probably depends on the circumstances. 

-- 
Hans-Juergen Boehm
