In article <344DB34F.41C6@liua.chem.cuhk.edu.hk>, Zhifeng Liu 
<liu@liua.chem.cuhk.edu.hk> writes:
|>   We have a program compiled successfully by f77 on Alphastations 
|>but couldn't run. The error message says:
|>
|>DECthreads bugcheck (version V3.13-435), terminating execution.
|>Failure initializing the manager thread tcb
|>
|>   The strangest thing is it runs OK on 'OSF1 V3.2 148 alpha', while 
|>not on the newer version 'OSF1 V4.0 564 alpha', although the two
|>machines have similar configuations.
|>
|>   Our program actully doesn't use the thread library. Its
|>size is reasonably large, using around 140Mb memory.  However, we 
|>have around 200Mb on these machines.  We have talked to the DEC people
|>in HK, and they don't have much idea.

Well, this isn't a problem with the compiler.  The run-time library does
use DECthreads, but I had thought this was disabled by default.  Your
local Digital support center should know how to submit a problem report
to engineering.

One thing I might suggest, having seen something vaguely similar in the
past, is to increase your process datasize limit.  Try an "unlimit" command
to start with.  If your program is very large, it may exceed the default
datasize for your system.  The amount of physical memory is not directly
relevant.
