In article <199710202223511709610N@marconi.physik.tu-berlin.de>,
schwarz@physik.tu-berlin.de (Georg Schwarz) writes: 

> I found a 175 MHz Octaine (64 MB RAM) to be considerably faster
> (50-100%) with some floating point/memory thoughput intensive finite
> elements simulation (written mostly in Fortran) than a 200 MHz
> UltraSparc (128 MB RAM). Honestly, I'm quite puzzled why (I tried to use
> the best machine specific optimization on both machines).

As always in this business, it depends on your application.  However, 
I've found with several different tests of my own and other people's 
software that SUNs are often quite slower than DEC or SGI for number 
crunching; I'm talking about machines comparable in price, configuration 
and SPEC ratings.  On the other hand, SUNs can sometimes be faster for 
heavy I/O work.  (Of course, I'm assuming optimum performance for each 
machine here.)  If you're doing number crunching, you also need to 
consider compiler quality, especially Fortran (since it can be best 
optimised and is designed for number crunching), a traditional strength 
of Digital.


