In article <62caih$17mg@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>,
C. J. Tan <cjtan@acs.ucalgary.ca> wrote:
:Which is a better machine for number crunching?  The SGI machines or the
:Sun Sparcs or the DEC Alphas?  Which model in particular?

That depends a lot on the exact behaviour of the code. Is it integer,
single precision, double precision, quad precision, some mix? Is
there a vector program waiting to get out of the code? Does it have
a tight locality of reference? Is the problem suitable for parallelization,
and if so, what parallel programming paradigms is it suitable for?
Does the program require a large I/O bandwidth? A large memory bandwidth?
A large cross-processor memory bandwidth when parallel algorithms are
used? What is the optimum number of processors to execute on, and can
the architecture actually handle that number? Has does the architecture
degrade as additional CPUs are added -- for example if the memory bandwidth
is fixed, then as additional CPUs are added, the bandwidth per CPU will
go down... which might not matter much for your program or might make
a big difference. How much memory does the problem need... how much would
it need if you took a big space/time tradeoff -- would the program run
significantly faster if you had an extra two or three gigabytes to play with?

There is no one architecture that is the best machine for all problems.



