>> does not do unspeakable things to my data storage behind my back.
>
>The difficulty here is that without garbage collection, you may
>accidentally do unspeakable things to the type-correctness of your
>program, and the cost of checking all pointer operations goes up in an
>alarming way.  ...

Um.  That isn't entirely true.  There are two major points:

    1) Yes, doing what I propose in most existing languages is a pretty
fair disaster.  But it IS possible to do the checking efficiently, using
use counts or otherwise, IF you design the language with that in mind,
and keep it in mind.

    2) One of the reasons that garbage collection is faster than what I
want is it does so much less checking!  In particular, a great many user
errors will lead to indefinite storage leaks, which are the utterly foul
failures that I was ranting against.

I hope that we are agreed that garbage collection WITHOUT pointer checking
is a recipe for chaos, sooner or later :-)


