pmontgom@cwi.nl (Peter L. Montgomery) writes:

> In article <y64t6gs45q.fsf@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> 
> Craig Burley <burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> writes:
> 
> >I don't see any rebuttal there.  Care to explain what *low-level*
> >constructs C is offering that Fortran doesn't offer, aside from
> >pointers?  
> 
>          Nobody has been mentioning unsigned arithmetic.
> In many implementations of Fortran (and C), signed and unsigned
> additions and subtractions behave identically, but C
> _guarantees_ what the result will be if it overflows, which
> Fortran does not.

Good call!  I bet this does help low-level stuff.  All Fortran
lets you do in unsigned is what you can accomplish with intrinsics
(say, MIL-STD 1753) or external procedures, which isn't as easily
optimized.

>         C has conditional compilation, which Fortran does not.

I'd say the fact that `cpp' is so widely available, and can thus
provide conditional compilation for Fortran, mitigates this
sufficiently.  In practice, tons of Fortran code is either
written "just fine" without a preprocessor, or one is used.
Though, there are too many different ones.  Hard to see how this
really benefits low-level coding, however.

>         Fortran leads C in its large intrinsic function library.
> C9X will finally let one write a MAX function which evaluates its
> arguments once only and use the INLINE keyword to get good code,
> but the user will need to call different functions depending
> upon the argument types (cf. labs/abs/fabs in C vs. ABS in Fortran).

Hmm.  Still not a big win for low-level coding for Fortran, though.

I wonder if ?: might be an important, but oft-overlooked, win for
low-level coding in C?  You can probably *achieve* the same thing
in Fortran using intrinsics and such, and certainly via statements,
plus Fortran wouldn't "want" to have the sequence-point aspect of
it, but I wonder if a generic CASE(N,E1,E2,...,En) intrinsic that
returns the value of expression E(N) would have made Fortran better
for low-level stuff.  I use ?: a lot in my C code, and usually
don't care if both choices happen to get evaluated, but sometimes
do care that a C compiler might be able to avoid the subexpression
entirely (something harder for a Fortran compiler if the statement
equivalent, i.e. IF/THEN/ELSE, is used, since it means figuring
out temporary variables' lifetimes, etc.).


Okay, so now I think the list of features "classic" K&R C offers
that "classic" FORTRAN 77 doesn't and that can make writing
fairly clean, *fast* low-level code easier than in FORTRAN consists of:

-  Unsigned integer arithmetic

-  Pass by value

-  Structs

-  ?: (*maybe*)

I'll try to keep this list in mind next time I'm tempted to pick
on C again (or, really, rebut the frequent "Fortran is useless"
rants on USENET ;-).
