Jukka Korpela wrote:
> What I'm saying is that from
> the Web authoring viewpoint, there is hardly any need to take
> AltaVista into account _separately_, as opposite to being
> search engine friendly in general.

The whole point of META tags is to support CERTAIN search engines.
AltaVista, for instance, uses "description" and "keywords" tags, as well
as indexing the text, the ALT text (for images), and the TITLE tag.
Other search engines ignore META tags, or include text in COMMENT tags,
or even include text in SCRIPT tags (my favorite!).

For people who are trying to achieve high rankings on search engines, it
is important to know as much as possible about how those specific search
engines evaluate websites. More important, however, is knowing how their
target audience looks for them.

