C. J. Tan wrote:
> To be more specific, I am looking for a number cruncher machine.  The
> machine will also be used for development.  Most of the stuff that I write
> are either in C or C++.  I am picking up Ada, Java and also OpenGL.  Which
> machine should I go for?  A Sparc or an SGI or an Alpha?  What are price
> ranges that I will be looking at for these machines?  It'd be best to be a
> used machine as I can't afford an expensive machine (I am a student).

Well I have all three types here (SPARC's, Alpha's and SGI's), and from what
you describe I think you'd be most happy with a 2nd hand Ultra-2/Creator3D
with dual 250 or 300 MHz processors (or possibly an Ultra-2 clone from Axil,
Integrix or Tatung). Also I'd recommend getting Sun's C++ Workshop and Java
Workshop, or at least trying them out.

The Alpha's are very nice machines, but you'd be hard pressed to get a dual
processor Alpha for less than a 2nd hand Ultra-2, and DEC charge a lot more
for their 3D graphics cards than Sun do (at about $2700 the Creator3D card is
extremely good value for money). Also, 3rd party software availability on the
Alpha's is poor for Digital UNIX and OpenVMS, so things like MAE (Mac
emulator), Sun's Java Workshop, Photoshop, etc aren't available on DU or VMS
(you can make Java workshop work by modifying the scripts like the Linux guys
do, but it's a bit of a hack). Of course you could run WinNT on the Alpha if
you wanted to :-(

As for SGI's, well they definitely have the highest coolness factor, and are
by far the nicest to work with from an end-user perspective (nice desktop
environment, easy admin for novices, lots of bundled tools etc). However it
sounds like you are not in the ballpark for an Octane due to cash
restrictions, and the O2 falls well short of the Ultras and Alphas on CPU
performance, and also on 3D graphics performance compared to the Creator3D
(but the O2 is competitive with DEC's midrange 3D cards, eg 4D40T).


