John Kotches wrote:
> Jason, he's a student.  I doubt he can _afford_ the price of even a
> second hand Ultra-2 or Ultra-2 clone.  I work and I certainly can't
> afford to buy one out of my pockets.

Well I'm a [PhD] student too. Here at my home I own 5 machines: 2 SPARCs, 1
Alpha, 1 SGI, and 1 PowerMac. Admittedly my scholarship helped, but I'm not
rich and I've never inherited a dollar. However, I'm well known for being
able to buy twice as much as anyone else for a given amount of money (call me
a purchasing legend if you want). So I probably see the above systems as less
expensive than you do.


> $2700?  That could be more than his entire machine budget.

Well if that's the case then its going to be pretty hard for him to get a
current model from any vendor, even Intel, assuming you want a decent system
(fast CPU, lots of RAM, big 7200rpm SCSI hard disk, 20" monitor, fast hires
24-bit graphics etc).


>> As for SGI's, well they definitely have the highest coolness factor ...
>
> Yes, and the $$$ are just too high.

That's what I said (for an Octane). But he might quite like a 2nd hand O2,
and they are pretty good value at the moment. If only the O2 could take dual
processors and had faster 3D graphics, it would be an awesome machine.

 
> Depending on what CJ wants to run a reasonably good inexpensive
> alternative would be to pick up on a dual processor P6 or PII processor
> motherboard setup.  Then (heretic that I am), run Solaris x86 on it.

Sure, that's an option. But he did say "number cruncher", which to me implies
that he wants good floating point performance, and I think everyone would
agree that the P-II has dud FP speed compared to any current RISC. A dual 300
MHz P-II is still less than a single 300 MHz UltraSPARC on SPECfp95 (2*8.1 <
1*18.3). Heck, even a SPARC-10 with quad hyperSPARC-125's will beat a dual
PII-300 system, and the SPARC-10 is a 5 year old design with 2+ year old
processors.


> If he's using homegrown code, etc. etc. etc. he can get a pretty rocking
> setup for less even than a used Ultra-2.

But its also going to be much slower at FP and _much_ slower at 3D graphics.
So while he might save a couple of thousand dollars, he gives up a factor of
2 in performance. IMHO the Ultra-2 has more than twice the performance for
only slightly more money, making it the best value for what he wants (which
is why I suggested it).

If the Ultra-2/Creator3D really is too expensive, then I humbly suggest an
Ultra-AX/Creator3D clone instead. The Ultra-AX is a similar design to the
Ultra-2, but only a single processor (still upgradable) and a PCI based I/O
system. In the lower price range a 2nd hand R10k SGI O2 is also very
attractive, except that SGI's compilers are probably too expensive for him
:-(

As an aside, I find it to be a common misconception that high-end PC's are
better value for money than RISC workstations. The real value for money is
found in the LOW END of the PC market. Once you get up into the high end you
find that all those little things that you have to add to your basic
Pentium-II cost a lot more than you think: SCSI card, 7200rpm SCSI hard disk,
fast 24-bit 3D graphics card, 20" multisync monitor, good ethernet card, etc
(not to mention dual processor support).

Even if you buy really well, you end up spending just as much as a comparable
workstation, partially because each individual item is so small that there's
little scope for negotiation, plus things like shipping really hurt with lots
of small items (especially for me because I'm in Australia). I have just been
through this process helping my brother buy a very high end PC for 3D
graphics work, and the value for money is not what you might think.

This is not to say that there aren't bargains in the high-end PC world, there
definitely are. But there are also bargains in the RISC workstation world :-)


> Don't get me wrong, I _love_ Sun's stuff.  But I doubt that C.J. can
> afford the level of machine you're talking here Jason.

I think you took the opinion that because he's a student he wants something
really cheap, whereas I took the opinion that because he was considering an
SGI he must have enough money to get something really good.

Only he can tell us which of these was the case...


