Merced moves a heck of a lot to the compiler and perhaps the OS
so the chip seems in some ways a manual-mode RISC. For all the
talk about its scalability, the proof will be in actual systems
to see how it works on real software. There is work in future Alphas
which shows rather conclusively that automatic instruction scheduling
can be pushed far further than anyone has yet seen on the streets.
Keeping huge numbers of registers out of the macro-architecture
also limits switching overhead, while preserving the speed for
the micro-architecture. When the hype dies down and real silicon
must be exhibited, Merced will deep-six the X86 architecture, but
I suspect Alpha will still be significantly faster. Over the VERY
long term, the cost of new fab lines will affect this, but the
existing fab lines at DEC can be scaled to the next generation sizes
just fine...
...or so I have heard.



