Terry C. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:

: Ah, but that's the assumption I'm not ready to make. Incorporating
: binary translation hardware in Merced--hardware that ensures backward
: compatibility with not one, but two architectures--is likely to burden
: the architecture with excess real estate.

The binary translation hardware is just a filter.  It takes in x86
instructions and spurts out Merced instructions.  Of course there is
some complication because IA-64 programs are bigger than x86 programs
so the filtering will do weird things to the address space.  Hence the
filter will need to distinguish between three requests: "I want the
next instruction", "I want to jump to this address" and "I want the
data at this address".  In other words, addressed will have to be
given two extra tag bits.

If the binary translation hardware is a filter then it has the same
requirements as a cache.  The processor reads through the binary
translation filter and the filter generates its own read requests to
main memory.  Perhaps the binary translation filter will reside on a
second chip in the same way that the L2 cache on a Pentium Pro resides
on a second chip.  This is an expensive proposition and perhaps
explains why Merced has been announced to be a high end only
processor.

-- James



