In article <62fucc$9nh@sun-cc204.lboro.ac.uk>,
James Soutter <cgjks1@lut.ac.uk> wrote:

>The binary translation hardware is just a filter.  It takes in x86
>instructions and spurts out Merced instructions.....
>If the binary translation hardware is a filter then it has the same
>requirements as a cache.

Have you ever done any work on binary translation? I'm not intimately
familiar with the topic, but I have read up on it a bit, and this
view to me seems somewhat naive. Getting good performance on the
Pentium Pro was not just a simple matter of translating i386
instructions into uOPs, for example. It strikes me that your scheme
is fine if you don't mind very slow emulation.

>Perhaps the binary translation filter will reside on a
>second chip in the same way that the L2 cache on a Pentium Pro resides
>on a second chip.

If you're going to go that far, why not go all the way and just
include a Pentium chip on the motherboard? It would probably be
cheaper.

