> >more powerful languages have not?  There are several answers:
> 
> [many good reasons deleted]
> 
> I am surprised that no-one has mentioned what may be the most important
> reason for the success of C: "The C Programming Language" by messrs K & R.
> An excellent book, far better than the language described. A religious text
> almost. After reading it, especially early in a career, one is convinced that
> "C" not only makes sense, but is even "the right way".
...

During the architectural explosion in the mid-80's I was languages
project leader at a one of the numerous small computer manufacturers
that cropped up and died out.

My absolute, unquestionable, MUST-make-this-objective first priority
when I started on the compilers was to have a C cross compiler running
on a VAX but generating code for my employer's instruction set by a
specified date shortly before the first prototype hardware was due to
be available. I undertook that project with NO discussion of the
technical merits or otherwise of C, or of our prospective customers'
language needs - I expected them to use more Fortran than C.

To have a usable system for the amount of money we could raise in
venture capital we had to take advantage of one of the all-time
bargains in computing - a 1980's UNIX source code license. Any other
approach to getting an OS, file system, utilities etc. would have cost
too much and taken too long. Most of the source code, including all
the OS source code that we were going to preserve, was in C.

You could get a UNIX system working with a totally flaky or
nonexistant Fortran, Pascal, or Lisp, but producing a working UNIX
system with all the commands implies possession of something
more-or-less reasonable in the way of a C compiler. I suspect that
this affected writers of programs that needed to run on multiple
systems - if C was the only language that turned out to be reasonably
reliable on all of their target systems, what were they going to code
in?



