Craig Burley <burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> writes:

> A given person can manage only so much complexity at a time.
> If he's spending mental energy coping with a missing feature
> (such as multi-dimensional arrays, or pointers), that's energy
> not being spent on the *application*, meaning he's probably
> going to forget something important, or not have time to test
> some feature, in the application.

When I went to school (quite some time ago) I was taught I should use
the appropriate language for the job. This is still good advice, but
there are factors which tend to prevent one doing this. One is that
there may be a limited range of languages available. In my first job
the choice was PDP 11 assembler or Fortran IV. When I needed pointers
I did it in assembler, the rest in Fortran.

Another factor is incompatibility.  Most largish projects have bits
which could be best written in one language and bits which could best
be written in another language, however, they may not be compatible
and are almost certainly not portably compatible. Digital tried to
address this problem in VMS, with a universal calling convention,
which is good as far as it goes. The problem is that it is difficult
to have a standard/convention which is flexible enough for all future
language developments. There is an even more serious compatibility
problem. Languages such as C++, lisp, java, prolog have extensive run
time support. Whilst it is common for languages such as this to
support calling C or Fortran procedures, doing things the other way
around is impossible in practice and may even be impossible in principle.

Lastly, as you say, as person can manage only so much complexity at a
time. What you don't say is effort spent maintaining expertise in many
languages is effort not spent producing the application.

In short, regrettable though it may be, there are good reasons for
people to pick a reasonably flexible language and stick with it.  It
is so obvious that different languages have different strengths it is
not worth debating. A more interesting discussion would be: How do you
reduce the barriers to using the most appropriate language for the job?

Ian



