Agreed, except for the part about "get far more control, and do more
hand optimization for my machine, with c than Fortran".  I keep saying
this, nobody seems to have put forth any evidence, or even persuasive
counter-arguments, to the contrary: C offers *very* little in terms
of low-level constructs that Fortran doesn't offer.  Richard O'Keefe
pointed out "pointers", and some people jumped all over him for saying
that (because he didn't list a lot more), but he's essentially right,
that's basically all ANSI/K&R C has to offer over FORTRAN 77 (and note
we're comparing a circa-1985-standardized language, ANSI C, to a
circa-1978-standardized one, FORTRAN 77, the latter having even more
historical baggage dating to back when people didn't *know* how to
do language design properly, instead of simply not bothering to learn
about what had gone before...Fortran 90, standardized circa 1991 or
whenever, *has* pointers, overly fancy ones if you ask me, certainly
fancier than any C variant I'm aware of).

And, most Fortran compilers (g77 and f2c probably being the only
exceptions that are at all popular) offer some form of pointers anyway
-- certainly all Fortran 90 compilers do.

Though, adding pointers to Fortran was about as painful and weird
as was adding `restrict' and all the other baggage to C to make
it approach vanilla Fortran performance on similarly expressed code.

In other areas where Fortran lacked the syntactic elegance of C,
adding intrinsics like IAND, IOR, ISHFT, and so on was quite easy
and consistent with normal Fortran expectations, and pretty much
all compilers support these (or they can be added easily by
programmers as external routines, typically at a performance
penalty).

What Fortran doesn't offer is the syntactic *convenience* of
things like `&&' and `||', which is a huge pain in the butt for
some kinds of work (it was for me anyway, back when I used Fortran
and PL/I for my work, as PL/I also lacked these, back then).

But syntactic convenience is not really a performance issue -- not
for wiz kids with nifty editors (like Emacs ;-) anyway.  (Speaking
of that, isn't "wiz" the proper spelling?  I see "whiz" used for
"wizard" sometimes, as in "Weather Whiz" and such, but that makes
me think of "Cheese Whiz", a name I don't believe was intended to
connote wizardly qualities.  Yet while "wiz" makes sense to me as
a noun, when I go to use it as an adjective, I do type the "h"
and then have to edit it out -- I typed "whiz kids" above first,
and it *felt* right, but isn't "wiz kids" correct?  This is a
much more important an issue than which programming language people
should use for multi-million-line numerical-programming projects, IMO.)

The other painful thing about using Fortran for low-level coding
is interfacing to C APIs (just as using C to interface to Fortran
APIs is painful).  Yes, in most Fortrans you can use %VAL, %REF,
%DESCR, and so on to achieve what you want, but that's hardly
a friendly way to go.  But, again, it's a matter of syntactic
convenience, mostly, with a strong dash of portability thrown in
(since the C API might look exactly the same to C code on another
system, whereas Fortran code munged to interface to that API might
not).

But I haven't seen these issues raised in this thread.  The main
issue was multi-dimensionality, and I have yet to see *anyone*
explain why C's flat, single-dimension facility is *superior*
for low-level coding to Fortran's `REAL A(0:N)', which is the
same thing, essentially.  So I *still* don't understand why C
is *better* as a low-level translation of code originally,
and properly, written in Fortran, than is Fortran.
