geoffrobx@stmarksx.ppx.catholicx.edux.aux (Geoff Roberts) writes:

> On 15 Oct 1997 15:08:09 -0700, Kevin Oberman <oberman@es.net> wrote:
> 
> >Please note that it is NOT an 802.3 repeater and should be used with
> >Ethernet V2 transceivers, not 802.3 ones. It is claimed to work with
> >802.3 transceivers with SQE Test enabled, but I have not had good luck
> >with this.
> Does this mean they will or won't carry TCP/IP between, say, PC's
> correctly? I am not familiar with the differences between V2 and 802.3
> / 802.2 transceivers..

They will. The differences between 802.3 and Ethernet V2 are all at
the physical layer and 802.3 was carefully designed to inter-operate
with Ethernet V2.  The only piece of equipment that typically cares is
a repeater. 802.3 repeaters want 802.3 transceivers (SQE Test
disabled) while Ethernet V2 repeaters want Ethernet V2 transceivers.

The only MAC layer difference is the optional
inclusion of a length field in the header in place of the protocol
type. Since IP normally does not use this option, no problem. In any
case, a LAN using Ethernet V2 hardware can still carry 802.3 frames.

There are several slight differences between Ethernet V2 and 802.3,
the biggest is test of collision detection commonly known as
"heartbeat". In 802.3 it is called "SQE Test" and may be
disabled. Ethernet V2 uses a similar, but slightly different signal
which may not be disabled. This is what causes repeaters to have
problems. 
 
> I have a couple of these, they appear to also have what look like
> fiber optic connectors as well as the AUI ports.  I am trying to think
> of something useful to do with them, but haven't thought of anything
> so far.  We have only one VAX and don't use Decnet at all. (ipx or ip)

DEREPs came in two flavors. One has two AUI connections while the other
has one AUI and one FOIRL port. It is essentially the same, but has a
FO transceiver built into the unit. (DEREP-FA, if memory serves.)
FOIRL is compatible with modern fiber Ethernet (10Base-FL).
