> simg@netcom.com (SimGraphics) writes:

> > All right, I'll try.
> >
> > Think of implementing run time library for two languages,
> > using two choices of languages.
> >
> > 1) C runtime implemented in C
> > 2) C runtime implemented in Fortran
> > 3) Fortran runtime implemented in C
> > 4) Fortran runtime implemented in Fortran
> >
> > My ordering, from easiest to hardest: 1,3,4,2.

Fortran compilers have been written in Fortran, and the
worst operating system stuff is usually written in assembly
language anyway.

I'd like to plug, again, my current favorite bit of research,
which is the SPIN kernel done at U Washington.  This is an OS
written in a language that has both safe and unsafe variants,
and carefully defined rules for how safe and unsafe code
interact.  What this means is that user code can (using a
"trusted compiler", the weak link in this system) be loaded
into the kernel.  In practice, this is used to enhance existing
OS services while avoiding the high cost of an untrusted
user-to-kernel transfer of control, and the benchmarks they
get are great (with a single exception that is merely equal,
uniformly better than commercial OSes running on the same
hardware).  Note that the language used (Modula-3) suffers
from having a merely ok compiler, plus all the overhead
associated with checked pointer casts and array bounds checks,
as well as garbage collection.  These low-level performance
handicaps were overcome by a better design made possible
by certain language features.

Just another reminder not to miss the forest for the trees.
No amount of C-hacking expertise can get you the security
benefits of a safe language with such good performance.

-- 
David Chase, chase@world.std.com



