Ian Dall <Ian.Dall@dsto.defence.gov.au> writes:
>There is an even more serious compatibility
>problem. Languages such as C++, lisp, java, prolog have extensive run
>time support. Whilst it is common for languages such as this to
>support calling C or Fortran procedures, doing things the other way
>around is impossible in practice and may even be impossible in principle.

C or Fortran calling Prolog or Lisp is by no means impossible in practice.
It is perfectly normal.  (How do you think the X window interface in a
commercial system works?  X callbacks call C functions which then call
the appropriate Prolog predicate or Lisp function.)

Or take Poplog, where *one* address space simultaneously supports
Prolog, Pop11, Common Lisp, ML, and whatever C or Fortran you care
to link in, with any language easily able to call any other.

>Lastly, as you say, as person can manage only so much complexity at a
>time. What you don't say is effort spent maintaining expertise in many
>languages is effort not spent producing the application.

What effort?

Sounds a lot like "we're too busy trying to catch up with our missed
deadlines to bother with any of this software engineering c---."

>In short, regrettable though it may be, there are good reasons for
>people to pick a reasonably flexible language and stick with it.

There are, but the reasons you have cited are not among them.

