simg@netcom.com (SimGraphics) writes:

> In article <y6en5m9p9z.fsf@tweedledumb.cygnus.com>,
> Craig Burley  <burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> wrote:
> >this, nobody seems to have put forth any evidence, or even persuasive
> >counter-arguments, to the contrary: C offers *very* little in terms
> >of low-level constructs that Fortran doesn't offer.
> 
> All right, I'll try.
> 
> Think of implementing run time library for two languages,
> using two choices of languages.
> 
> 1) C runtime implemented in C
> 2) C runtime implemented in Fortran
> 3) Fortran runtime implemented in C
> 4) Fortran runtime implemented in Fortran
> 
> My ordering, from easiest to hardest: 1,3,4,2.

I don't see any rebuttal there.  Care to explain what *low-level*
constructs C is offering that Fortran doesn't offer, aside from
pointers?  I've already *said* that C's notational convenience
makes it easier to type in the code, but that's not particularly
relevant to getting maximum performance out of existing or new
architectures.  (And I've already pointed out that entire *operating
systems*, implemented on machines that C couldn't cope with
properly in fact, have been implemented largely in FORTRAN 66,
so I doubt a C runtime implemented in Fortran would be particularly
challenging, when it comes to gaining the same performance, as
compared to one implemented in C.  Can you say the same thing
about a Fortran runtime that includes popular numeric-library
routines?  I *know* you can't, the aliasing problems alone would
require you to use C extensions that are only being discussed and,
sometimes, implemented *today*.)
