Richard A. O'Keefe wrote:

> No, the point is that sophisticated compiler analysis (read: not just
> interprocedural data flow analysis but cross *translation unit* data
> flow analysis) is *required*.

However, such analysis is becoming increasingly commonly available these
days from most serious compiler vendors. I used to consider
interprocedural analysis as an exotic and expensive frill, but no
longer.  This is not to excuse the language design shortcomings, but to
say that they are no longer as great a drawback as they used to be..

> C is wrong because it cannot optimise code that _is_ clean very
>   well without going to a hell of a lot of work to find out that it
>   is clean.
> Fortran is wrong because it _does_ optimise code on the _assumption_
>   that it is clean.
> What we really want are languages for which a compiler _can_ exploit
> the absence of aliasing, side effects, &c whenever it is safe to,
> needing only linear cost analysis of the source code to determine
> when it is safe.  The Larch annotations for C strike me as a move
> in the right direction.

Mmm. I'm torn on that front. I agree that such annotations are very
useful. However, as a C purist, I would be satisfied with a small set of
qualifiers that provided enough hints to the optimizer to exploit
significant opportunities without being perfectly optimal.

"const" and "volatile" (and the much-discussed "noalias" or its bastard
child "restrict") are steps in the right direction. I'd also vote for a
hint that a function had no memory side effects (something that, even
with source, is often hard to determine).  The most aggressive
optimizations that *relied* on these keywords would have to be an
optional level (because of the curse of legacy code), but at least they
would be available.

