kma@doorknob.cs.brown.edu (Keith Adams) wrote:

> "The effort spent maintaining expertise in many languages." It takes an 
> investment of time and energy to learn any programming language. To become
> productively competent in a programming language takes considerably more time
> and energy. And once you've made this initial investment, you must make the
> continued effort of staying current as the language and the tools that support
> it change.

I program regularly in C, Perl, and assembly language.  Two of these languages are
nearly static.  Perl doesn't change so rapidly that I can't keep up easily.  I would not
care to rewrite anything I did in C with either Perl or assembly, nor would I want to
replace the assembly with C or Perl code, and so on for the perl stuff.

So I submit that for the mix of programming that I do regularly, I'm more productive
because I use multiple languages than I'd be if I only used one of the three.

If my job required me to start writing code for parallel machines tomorrow, I'd spend
a few weeks getting acquianted with another language before I used any of the three
languages above for a big project.  Ditto if I had to start a large project full of
numerical analysis.

Yes, it takes effort to gain proficiency in a new tool.  But using the wrong tool can
require even more effort.

Kelly




