Yes, and I regard this whole mess as a tragedy of the first water.  The
point is that there are TWO aspects here:

    1) Are ALL pointer operations checked?

    2) Is there a semi-automatic way of recovering dead space?

The confusion that caused the Maclaren-O'Keefe explosion was due to a
misunderstanding over this.  I was stating that I did not like the second
and had never seen a good one - but I was NOT stating that I did not want
the first!  What I actually want is a language where all pointer uses are
checked, and I get an error when I get it wrong, but where the system
does not do unspeakable things to my data storage behind my back.

I have a fair amount of experience in attempting to debug problems in
large programs (not mine) with unchecked pointers AND garbage collection,
and it has almost always been a complete disaster.  The effect of an error
is that it is rarely detected near where it occurs, and the garbage
collector has destroyed all the evidence by the time the program falls
over.  I have several times had to resolve this problem in cases where the
authors of the code admitted defeat, and it took AGES.

Hence my position.  If don't really WANT garbage collection, but I will
put up with it (for most programs) ONLY if all pointer operations are
checked.  If that is not so, I don't want to know.


