In article <62caih$17mg@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>, "C. J. Tan"
<cjtan@acs.ucalgary.ca> writes: 

> Which is a better machine for number crunching?  The SGI machines or the
> Sun Sparcs or the DEC Alphas?  Which model in particular?

There was a LONG thread about this a while back in at least one 
newsgroup.  Do a search for `Deutscher', `girlfriend' `crunch*' and 
you'll probably find it.  (The thread began by Stefan Deutscher saying 
that his girlfriend wanted to give him a number cruncher for $70000 
(that's right, seventy thousand dollars) and really took off from there.

It really depends on your budget and what you are doing.  I'm very happy
with an ALPHA (running VMS, but they run other operating systems as
well).  It also depends on the language in that the quality of the 
compiler is also an issue.  For Fortran I don't think you can beat DEC; 
their other compilers are certainly good but I don't have much personal 
experience with them (though C and C++ are used on my machine and I've 
never had any problems).

If you want to spend a lot of money, you can probably get a test machine 
from each vendor on loan for a week and run YOUR OWN programmes on them 
and make your own comparisons.  Assuming that you have adequate and 
equal knowledge about how to get the most out of one machine, that is 
really the only fair comparison.  Also, depending on what you are doing, 
there might be differences in the way things scale if you have more than 
one job running at a time, especially comparing multiprocessor to single 
processor machines (the above vendors all have multiprocessor machines; 
you didn't mention IBM and HP and Intel---they might be worth looking 
into).

Rumour has it that the next generation ALPHA processors will appear 
sometime next year (I don't know about other vendors) so depending on 
your budget and time scale, the fact that the present generation MIGHT 
become substantially cheaper around that time (it has happened in the 
past) might be something to take into account.

If you're developing a new application and are not planning on using 
Fortran95, reconsider.  It is in general the best language for number 
crunching, and the close interaction between Fortran and HPF could be 
something to keep in mind if you run your code on a parallel machine.
Digital has F90/STANDARD=F95 which is a good compiler.


