> If I invested the amount of money I give to SSA every year, I'd be a
> millionaire by now (possibly several times over). Instead, I'll get back
> what I put it--with something like a 2% return. That's not a very good
> example.

Not that this has anything to do with any of the newsgroups it's
posted to, but what the hell....

You are either very old, overestimating your investing skills, or
overestimating the amount you have put into SS.  If you absolutely
maxed out contributions to Social Security for the past 20 years,
counting both employee and employer contributions, and invested it to
get 10% return every year, you'd STILL have less than $250,000.  You
might say you could get 12%, but you'd still be nowhere near a
millionaire.  Much more than 12% is pretty unreasonable return to
assume, and probably colored by the fact that the past few years have
been giving huge returns...  (12% is pretty typical for a stock-heavy
portfolio in the past 20 years, and 10% is pretty typical for a more
balanced portfolio).

Now maybe if you've been contributing for 40 years and could get a 15%
return every year, then maybe, just maybe you'd be near a
millionaire.  But even that's not a sure thing...

