I see DEC selling its ALPHA to Intel the same way I see DEC selling RDB
to Oracle. To put it bluntly: capitulate to competition and get some 
short term bucks in exchange for dropping out of a market in which DEC 
didn't think it had a chance.

The timing of the announcement/speculation is what bugs me. Digital
isn't exactly regaining its former prominence. It has put all its own
eggs in its Alpha chip and now *wants* to sell Alpha ? ? ? All it would
have left is its service and Microsoft consulting.

If Intel were to fully adopt Alpha and make a go at it, I could see some
advantage. But according to the WSJ speculation, Intel would get Alpha
and only be obligated to manufacture it for a certain period, no
obligation to market it. So DEC wouldn't be selling Alpha to make Alpha
succesful. So why would DEC sell Alpha ?
 
If a company the size of Compaq was able to make a serious attempt at
buying Digital, then surely Intel would be able to get Digital if it
wanted to.

When a small guy sues a big guy with a serious and valid complaint,
the big guy spends his way out of the public lawsuit. Palmer sued 
Intel and my fear is that Intel would rather take out some petty cash
and
buy DEC to make the suit (and DEC) irrelevant. 

If Digital were to be unable to prevent Intel from buying DEC, then ts
options would be to shed its offending product: Alpha. 

> Given that we don't even yet know whether there is a deal,
> this all seems a little hasty.

Correct. It is all speculation at this point. But many such speculations
in the past have proven to be not so far fetched after all.

If I were a DEC shareholder, I would much prefer to see DEC capitulate
and
accept a buyout from Intel compared to DEC selling off its last limb and 
continue as an also ran.

Whether Digital is right or wrong with its patent infrigement suit is
not the issue. The issue is how DEC would deal with Intel wanting to buy
DEC to 
stop the lawsuit.



