guru@newindia.com wrote:
> I had the site in the Altavista's index and it wsa well searcheable
> for a long time (more tehan a  year) Upon redesigning, and
> resubmitting a couple of days back, we found no signs of the site.
> Even the earlier pages disappeared. The pages were fetched while
> adding the URLs but  they never turned up in the search, whatever we
> tried to search. Eventually we discovered all, (even the earlier, well
> ranked) pages missing. We have even complied to their anti-spam policy
> in page redesign etc

I was able to find your pages on AltaVista.

I think the "problem" you are experiencing is related to your attempt to
influence the spider by using META tags. There are two things I think
you are doing wrong.

First, your page has the following META tags in it:

   <META HTTP-EQUIV="Content-Type" CONTENT="text/html;
charset=iso-8859-1">
   <META NAME="Author" CONTENT="Rajeev Tandon (MPS Digital Labs)
guru@newindia.com">
   <META NAME="Keywords" contents="
genuine leather bags, ladies bags, fashion bags, portfolio bags, soft
leather briefcases, computer bags, notebook bags,
genuine leather belts, soft leather belts, brided leather belts, 
leather saddlery, horse riding, saddles, saddlery, reins, harness,
bridle, halters, reins, saddle pads, halfter, zugel, leder sattle,
zaume,
exporters, exports, kanpur, india, manufacturers, industries, industry,
joint venture, business, uttar pradesh
">
<META NAME="Description" CONTENT="Manufacturer and exporter of Leather
Saddlery, Leather Bags, Leather Belts, Computer Bags. In-house tannery.
Genuine Leather.">

The example given on the AltaVista help page looks like this:

<META  name="description" 
content="We specialize in grooming pink poodles.">
<META  name="keywords" content="pet grooming, 
Palo Alto, dog">

The first thing I recommend is watching your capitalization. Just to be
safe, match their capitalization EXACTLY. So use "description" instead
of "Description" and "keywords" instead of "Keywords".

Second, I suggest you NOT start your keywords with a carriage return.
Safest is to place them all on one line, even though their example has a
carriage return in it. Also keep this in mind:

"AltaVista will index the description and keywords up to a limit of
1,024 characters." (from the AltaVista help page).

Good luck!

