gcc2_compiled.:
___gnu_compiled_c:
.text
        .align  0
        .global _b
_b:
        @ args = 0, pretend = 0, frame = 0
        @ frame_needed = 1, current_function_anonymous_args = 0
        mov     ip, sp
        stmfd   sp!, {fp, ip, lr, pc}
        mov     r0, #0
        sub     fp, ip, #4
        ldmea   fp, {fp, sp, pc}


At its core this whole discussion is not really a FORTRAN versus C
issue.  Its a question of "How do we come up with a language that
allows us to represent what is really going on in such a way that it
is easy for the programmer to specify (correctly!) and possible for
the compiler to make effective use of it."

Fortran "solves" this problem by having a restricted type system which
makes it inappropriate for some problems (e.g. writing operating
systems and device drivers). C's type system is simply pathetic, C++
is worse, and Java's is only marginally better than C's.

From the other point of view the functional programming people
understand type systems properly but have yet (in my experience) to
provide a language which allows nasty things like device drivers to be
written.

[ Consider 'void reschedule(void)' Fortran might optimise such a thing
out, which might be very bad for your operating system.... ]

Oh, and this language has to be both non-proprietary and have huge
industry support.  It may well be that we have to go from here to
there in steps, and that Java is the first such.

