You've got to be careful here.  Blocking seems to be very hard,
though I would not be surprised if someone's compiler somewhere
did it.  There are algorithms that are intrinsically unblockable,
so I am told my the true experts in the field.  An example of this
is QR factorization using Householder transformations -- you have
to use Givens rotations instead.  No compiler is going to do that
for you.

The advantage of Fortran here is that by-hand cache blocking is
probably sufficient, and the compiler will behave relatively
well with that.  With C, it is likely that you will need to
carry out more extensive by-hand transformations to get the
same performance.

Also, your gradual student had better watch out for what might
euphemistically be termed "targeted optimizations", undiplomatically 
known as benchmark cheating.  Avoid industry-standard benchmarks.

> What I'm wondering, and here I go way beyond any expertise I might
> possibly claim, is whether (a) there are patterns of memory use in
> functional languages that hardware designers might exploit, without
> adverse effect on more conventional languages?  and (b) whether the
> hardware designers see any point in worrying about this?

(a) maybe and (b) not much, though I did loan what I considered
my interesting GC papers to a hardware designer at one time.
I think he was interested in doing no harm, as opposed to actually
helping.

For the time being, I think it makes sense to concentrate on making
functional language implementations resemble Fortran under the hood.
For instance, you could write a garbage collector to take advantage
of the ability to load-w/o-faulting (load first, ask questions about
null pointers later) and also perhaps to prefetch (certainly, in a
copying GC, it would make sense to prefetch/zero cache lines that you
knew you were about to write).

-- 
David Chase, chase@world.std.com



