Shankar Unni <shankar@powertelglobal.com> writes:

>Richard A. O'Keefe wrote:

>> >       bool = !(!f() | (!g() & !h()));
>> 
>> In a word, NO.  The C version absolutely requires all of the function
>> calls to be performed.  

>First of all, this is true for "&" and "|", but not "&&" or "||". I'm
>sure you knew that.  (Does Fortran allow .AND. of INTEGER variables, or
>only LOGICAL variables? If the latter, then "&" is a bad analog anyway).

Yes, I know that.  One of the points here is precisely that Fortran
refuses to distinguish between & and &&.  Another point is that Fortran
applies exactly the same rule to *all* operators:  if the compiler
can determine that the *result* of some subcomputation is irrelevant
to the value of the whole, it can delete that subcomputation entirely.

Another point is that the C operators & and | were introduced in this example
in a failed attempt to express the Fortran .AND.  and .OR. operators.
(Hence all the bangs, to make sure the operators only received 0/1 arguments.)
What they do on general integer values is irrelevant to the argument.

>Secondly, even for "&" (or "*", as in the 0*f() example) , it can elide
>the call if the effect is the same whether it had or had not called the
>function. I.e. if the compiler *knows* that the function has no side
>effects (either by deduction, or by programmer intervention), it can
>skip the call.

But the compiler *can't* know that in many practical cases.
(With a richer type system, e.g. with the LCL specifications that the
LC-lint checker can enforce, where you _can_ pin down the possible
side effects of a function you haven't seen yet, a C compiler _could_
know this, but of course the language isn't quite C any more.)

The point here is that a Fortran compiler can *always* do this;
it is *always* legal for a Fortran compiler to treat *any* function
call as if it had no side effects

> Thus, in

>  static int foo() { return 10; }
>  ...
>  for (i = 1; i <= 1000; i++) {
>     (void) 0*f();
>  }

>the compiler is allowed to boil down the entire loop into nothing.

Sure.  But just you try and move foo() into another file.

>Other such optimizations are also allowed: if the function had been

>  static int foo() { return globalvariable = 10; }

>then the entire loop can be boiled down into

>  globalvariable = 10;

Again, so what?  Fortran compilers have always been allowed to do
inlining too.  Nobody is arguing that inlining is a bad idea or that
compilers should not or cannot optimise code they have *full information*
about.


>(The point is that yes, you can create contrived examples in which C is
>not allowed to optimize what Fortran is, but sophisticated compiler
>analysis can make up for a lot of these problems. Not a panacea, but
>it's better than nothing.)

No, the point is that sophisticated compiler analysis (read: not just
interprocedural data flow analysis but cross *translation unit* data
flow analysis) is *required*.

Lest anybody mistake my attitude, let me state again that here I
think Fortran and C are *both* wrong.

C is wrong because it cannot optimise code that _is_ clean very
  well without going to a hell of a lot of work to find out that it
  is clean.

Fortran is wrong because it _does_ optimise code on the _assumption_
  that it is clean.

What we really want are languages for which a compiler _can_ exploit
the absence of aliasing, side effects, &c whenever it is safe to,
needing only linear cost analysis of the source code to determine
when it is safe.  The Larch annotations for C strike me as a move
in the right direction.  Not only is there the potential of getting
better optimisation if the compiler could know this stuff, LClint
makes a _great_ debugging tool.

