Kurt Schweitzer <kurts@sunriseconsulting.com> writes:

> The first thing I recommend is watching your capitalization. Just to be
> safe, match their capitalization EXACTLY. So use "description" instead
> of "Description" and "keywords" instead of "Keywords".

Why should one match to the capitalization in the examples given by
the designers of one particular search engine? Perhaps analogously
to "designing for Netscape"? :-)

As Dmitry Kirzanov describes in his excellent discussion of search
engines from Web authoring point of view, at
  http://www.webreference.com/dlab/books/html-pre/43-0.html
there is little we can really _know_ about the behavior of search
engines.

But I'd expect any decent search engine to treat values of the NAME attribute
of a META element as _case-insensitive_. If search engines don't work
that way, there is little we can do unless at least the most popular
engines explicitly reveal how they want the cases to be.

> Second, I suggest you NOT start your keywords with a carriage return.
> Safest is to place them all on one line, even though their example has a
> carriage return in it. Also keep this in mind:

Similar remarks apply here. If search engines parse HTML miserably,
they might as well have some arbitrary line length limit.

> "AltaVista will index the description and keywords up to a limit of
> 1,024 characters." (from the AltaVista help page).

Is that a problem? The example discussed wasn't anywhere near that
limit, and it looked perfectly good use of META elements to me.

Naturally one should not write irrelevant keywords or repeat keywords
(search engines might really punish you for trying to get hits that way).
And the description should be reasonably short so that it makes sense
in a typical search report. And these natural requirements should
normally imply that an arbitrary, although quite understandable, limit
of 1,024 characters can hardly be a problem to you.

It is a public secret that AltaVista has features which effectively
restrict its data base to about 600 - 700 documents _per server_.
It is also a public secret that there is something odd in the
searching by AltaVista. On the other hand, AltaVista has very nice
features, and it is still popular. What I'm saying is that from
the Web authoring viewpoint, there is hardly any need to take
AltaVista into account _separately_, as opposite to being
search engine friendly in general.

