Tsk.

Norcroft ARM C compiles the equivalent:

   extern __pure int f(int a);

   int b(int a)
   {
       return 0 * f(a);
   }

to

		      b
    000000  e3a00000          MOV      a1,#0
    000004  e1a0f00e          MOV      pc,lr
  
I thought gcc was supposed to be catching up with our compiler? ;-)


Back to thread, a point to make is that neither of these programs are
ANSI C any more, and what's being discussed is ANSI C vs. ISO (?)
Fortran.

Or, as you rightly point out "how do we make a language that ...",
which is what Nick Maclaren has changed the subject to, essentially.

>Fortran "solves" this problem by having a restricted type system which
>makes it inappropriate for some problems (e.g. writing operating
>systems and device drivers).

I think what Fortran solves in this argument is that it guarantees much
less than C. E.g.:

  void copy(char *p, char *q, int n)
  {
    while (n--) *p++ = *q++;
  }

C defines the behaviour should p and q overlap.

  return 0 * f();

C defines that f is called.

  return f() || g();

C defines that g is not called, if f returns non-zero, and that f is called
before g in any case.

Fortran, from what I have read, makes none of these guarantees, which
leaves more flexibility in the compiler to target the application at
a given processor.

Nick Maclaren's gripe appears to be virtual functions, of the type used
often in C (X11, e.g.), C++ and Java. (Java's a bit of a red-herring, as
JIT compiled code might be able to remove the virtual function calls, and
a JVM isn't really executing the Java bytecode - it's a prefetching from
the datacache problem, which is there with any programming style.)

A strong type system certainly helps the compiler - but it isn't all the
problem. (Though it might help with inter-procedural optimisations, which
would solve some of the examples above - e.g. the compiler discovers for
itself that 'f' is __pure.)

Mike.



