David Cathey wrote:
> 
> Terry Shannon wrote:
> >
> > Selling Alpha lock stock and barrel for $1.5B doesn't make good sense.
> > As you pointed out, the Alpha run rate is over $1B per year, probably
> > closer to $2B.
> 
>         And the FAB mentioned in the article cost about $1.5B
> to build, too.
> 

During the Fab-6 groundbreaking ceremonies, DEC said the fab would cost
about $525M. Subsequent DEC releases put the price at $425M and $450M.
(Given the cost of union labor in Massachusetts, I am at a loss to
explain the underrun!)

Of course, ~$500M gets you a fab and a Class 4 clean room, not all the
x-ray photolithography equipment, etc, needed to actually produce chips.

Over its lifespan, Fab-6 could easily cost $1.5B. The real problem is
the NEXT fab, which undoubtedly will cost much, much, more than its
predecessor. Fab-6 should sustain DEC through EV8 and perhaps EV9, but
DEC's big Y2K issue is beginning the design and near-term construction
of a Fab-6 followon.

