In article <1997Oct14.153332.1@eisner>, comarow@eisner.decus.org (Bob Comarow) writes:
>In article <3442E005.33D9@videotron.ca>, jf mezei <"[non-spam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca> writes:
>> 
>> I cannot understand why shareholders are not revolting at this news
>> (whether rumour or not) and asking for an immediate ousting of whoever
>> is responsible for such a plan.
>> 
>
>Stockholders rarely get upset the day their stock goes up 4.5 points.
>

I found that rise in stock price to be completely mysterious.  Ok, it
makes sense for a NORMAL company's stock to rise when there is a good 
chance that there will be a large cash infusion, but it doesn't make much
sense for DEC's stock to rise since:

1.  The stock in question doesn't pay dividends, so there is no chance 
    whatsoever that the money will be passed on to investors directly.

and

2.  DEC's downword spiral is not the result of a lack of cash, but rather
    the consequences of poor management.  They've had piles of cash on hand
    before, and have had other large cash infusions recently (from the sale
    of RDB, and the disk division) and, near as I can tell, these at best
    only served to slow the decline slightly. 

So what other reason's for a stock run up could there be?

3.  Speculation that Intel would buy DEC outright.  That would probably 
    increase the real value of the stock, since Intel is much better at
    making money than is DEC.

4.  Speculation that there might be a management change, since early rumors
    had this "sale" as coming from the board level, ie, an indication that
    current management was being removed from (part of) the loop.

Regards,

