reid@psi.ch (Dr Ivan D Reid, muSR Facility) writes:

> 	Hmm, weird timing.  I still have a file dated 17-OCT-1989, i.e.
> not quite 8 years ago, from when the 
> 	"W O R M S    A G A I N S T    N U C L E A R    K I L L E R S"
> managed to release it in a system I had some control over.  As far as I
> recall, it came down to an account having the password the same as the
> username.  I remember it took me some hours to convince the systems people
> that there was a problem...  The programme looks like it went to all sorts
> of tricks to find holes in system security, but I think someone who
> analysed it more closely than I came to the conclusion that there was a
> programming bug that prevented most of the nasty stuff from being executed.

While this was an interesting event, it did not use the Internet. The
WANK worm was propagated entirely via DECnet. It spread widely on the
NASA SPAN network and, somewhat less widely on the DOE HEPnet (which
was connected to SPAN). It also (in a different form) infected
Digital's EZnet.

I was the one who did the analysis (along with John McMahon at
NASA-Goddard and Mark Kaletka of the DOE FermiLab. A recent book, "The
Underground" by Suelette Dreyfus on the worm paints a credible case
for its source being Australia, although original law enforcement
efforts only traced it as far back as France.

While not deliberately destructive, it was highly annoying and
included a virus, written in DCL like the worm itself.

It hacked into system by looking for accounts that lacked a password
(this code was broken) and accounts with the same username and
password. It also tried a set of "standard passwords" from a
dictionary and modified itself as it found working passwords. It did a
variety of non-destructive, but annoying things.

A subsequent re-release of the code fixed some bugs and aimed directly
at the huge DECNET non-privileged account, long a gaping hole in
DECnet systems. This worm finally induced Digital to fix the problem.

The BBC recently filmed a documentary on the worm which should appear
in the UK on the program "Mysteries". I'm the shifty eyed one.
