Richard Black <rjb@tanna.dcs.gla.ac.uk> writes:

> [ Consider 'void reschedule(void)' Fortran might optimise such a thing
> out, which might be very bad for your operating system.... ]

No, it won't.  Fortran *subroutines* are viewed as containing
must-be-executed imperative steps, so since `reschedule' would be
a subroutine, it *would* be called at the specified point.  (You
know, we used to write operating systems in Fortran, back before
C became popular!  :)

It's Fortran *functions* that are viewed, to some degree (and, frankly,
even the Fortran community and the standards committee itself are
widely divided on the extent of this!), as more "functional", or
mathematical, in a traditional sense.  C doesn't have this concept;
a C "function" is really like a Fortran subroutine with a convenient
extra "argument" to return a variable.

(The debate in the Fortran community over this issue is pretty much
bounded by complete agreement that "R = 0 * S()" need not call S()
and that "CALL FOO" *must* call FOO.  In the sense of the omnipresent
"as if" rule, this means their side effects must be performed in the
order indicated by their definitions.  The debate is over things
like whether "R = S()" need call S when that R is not used or is used
once as in "T = 0 * R".  The FORTRAN 77 and Fortran 90 standards seem
to say, especially when all the text and examples are taken into account,
that S *must* be called in this case, but some members of the standards
committee appear to be claiming either that the F90 standard is not
really requiring this, or that future standards won't require it, or
both.)

Please, everyone keep in mind in these discussions, that there *is*
no tradeoff between Fortran, with its "high-level constructs", and
C with its "low-level constructs".

The tradeoff is between Fortran, which *has* (some) high-level
constructs, and C, which does not.  *Both* languages have the
pertinent low-level constructs: Fortran allows writing code that
views memory/arrays as linear just as C does, Fortran allows writing
what C has as `&&' and `||', and so on.  In some of these cases,
Fortran is more verbose (too so for my tastes and the kind of software
I write, but I'm not writing numerical software), but C offers
*no way* for a programmer to express multi-dimensional arrays,
Fortran's (and English's ;-) .AND. and .OR., and so on.
