Paul D. DeRocco wrote:
> 
> Richard A. O'Keefe wrote:
> >
> > Another point is that Fortran
> > applies exactly the same rule to *all* operators:  if the compiler
> > can determine that the *result* of some subcomputation is irrelevant
> > to the value of the whole, it can delete that subcomputation entirely.
> 
> This is certainly true of C++, whose current draft standard
> states that the observable effects of running the program must
> be the same as if the program were executed as described in the
> standard. It's commonly called the "as if" rule.
> <snip>

For better or worse, Fortran is more liberal in its as if rule. If the
value returned by a FUNCTION (what O'Keefe implies by *result* above)
can be shown to be the same under the assumptions that its arguments and
global variables are not modified and any internal state of the FUNCTION
does not alter the value returned, then the subcomputation can be
deleted entirely. The difference from the vast majority of other
languages is in the above assumptions which result in local analysis
being sufficient for the as if rule for more than standard library
procedures. This means that internal state of FUNCTIONs is intended for
efficiency of computation purposes only. It also means that
modifications of arguments and global variables by FUNCTIONs are well
defined, if at all, only for error flags or similar constructs. However,
it is difficult for a compiler to detect intent and report erroneous
useages of constructs.

