> of real-world machine behaviour.
> 
> The bottom line (as I, not being a computer scientist, see it):
> The design of the standard C language is less suited to optimization
> by compiler for modern mainstream processor architectures than some
> other languages.  FORTRAN looks to be one of those other languages,
> from what most people have pointed out in this thread.
> 
> You may be a mighty software god who can do all that optimization at
> the C source level, but I think most people don't want to have to cope
> with that kind of lossage. And I suspect you might feel the same way
> if you had to enhance or port the resultant code.

Did I ever say otherwise? Of course you are right.

But for me, writing more efficient code can reduce running time
from 4 months to 1 month, even if I am using a 300 Gflop machine.



