Richard A. O'Keefe wrote:
> 
> geest3+@pitt.edu (Gregg E Economou) writes:
> >You miss the point that while yes, they may be physically multidimensional,
> >and even organisationally multidimensional, and even architecturally
> >multidimensional, they are *logically* linear, and accessed in a linear
> >fashion. Yes, there are strange exceptions out there, but the majority
> >of memories are linear int the majorities or architectures and the
> >majority of machines ever designed, made, or sold.
> 
> With everybody accusing everyone else of missing *the* point,
> perhaps it is time to make *a* point.
> 
> Today, if you want to write high-performance code on stock hardware,
> you have to view your plain ordinary so-called 'linear' address space
> as three dimensional.
> 
> For example, I am writing this on an UltraSPARC 2.
> 
> Level 1 data cache:             (The L1 code cache is the same,
>     16 kbytes                    I _think_)
>     32 bytes per line
>      1-way association
> 
> Level 2 cache:                  (Unified, I _think_)
>    512 kbytes
>     64 bytes per line
>      1-way association
> 
> VM
>      2 Gbytes
>      8 kbytes per page
> 
> Physical memory
>    512 Mbytes

Yes, you have made a point. 

You have made the point that memory is not uniform,
you have not made the point that memory is multi-dimensional. Perhaps I
should discuss exactly what I meant when I mentioned "multi-dimensionality".
When I use the word "4 dimensional", for example, I usually refer to
a situation where there are certain symmetries present, not matter which
direction (or axis) you are pointing along. (I should point out,
proudly, that I have had no formal computer training of any kind, ever,
so I may not use the same terms that you use in that 
Ivory Tower of yours over there). In the context of memory,
I naturally assumed that any sensible person would realize that the
symmetry of interest would be "speed of access". What you have described
does NOT have this symmetry, and therefore I cannot fathom why you
would claim that it is multi-dimensional. I can only assume you are using
some broader abstract concept of the term, which does not really fit 
well with my scientific concepts. The fact that you have various
components of the memory system is meaningless! OF COURSE, you are
going to have to take into account the fact that there are registers,
various levels of cache, local memory, shared memory, virtual memory....
Only a poor programmer would not take this into account! However, all this
proves is that you have a 1+1+1+...+1 dimensional structure to your
memory, and 1+1 is NOT equal to 2. You can still make most efficient
use of the memory through the use of one dimensional structures,
whilst optimizing data layout and use in such a way as to maximize
usage of the cache, main memory, etc.

Ok, now let me discuss where MY use of the word "dimensional". Say
for example, you are working with a 3D or 4D "array", representing
space or space-time. The actual symmetries of spacetime are
reflected in the fact that you would do best with a memory system
in which access is equally fast no matter which axis you are
doing access along. (If you want specifics, imagine some kind
of a N-dimensional 3rd order derivative operator, requiring
information from neigboring points in the 2N directions).
When I said "memory is not multi-dimensional", I meant "memory
access is not equally fast along all axes of a multi-dimensional
array". Of course it is not!! Some points are in the cache, 
some may be stored in registers from some previous calculation,
some are in main memory, some may be on another node in some
MPP supercomputer. I know that high performance fortran actually
usually does an excellent job of optimizing the calculations,
but my personal experience is that I can usually do better
by really thinking hard, and coming up with an algorithm that
realizes that the various ONE-DIMENSIONAL components of the 
memory system are utilized in an optimal manner. And as I said
elsewhere, most people don't need to bother wasting their time
on this kind of optimization, but for me, doing so can reduce
a simulation from one month down to one week, on a 300 Gflop
supercomputer that I have very limited resources on. 

It is amazing that almost everyone jumped on my statement that
memory is not multi-dimensional with absurds comments about the
"multi-dimensional structure of memory hardware". I suggest you
all replace "dimensional" with "component" in the above phrase,
to which I reply:

                     DUH



