THE SILLY SEASON 

Copyright (c) 1997 by Terry C. Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC

Summer may be over, but news accounts gleaned during the past week
reveal that the Silly Season is upon us. In news of the bizarre, the
lice trade has become a booming business in Romania; an Egyptian peasant
woman sacrificed her life to save her donkey when she threw herself
between it and three men preparing to kill the animal for raiding their
fields; and the US National Park Service is spending $330K to build a
"two-holer" outhouse at the Delaware Water Gap. But tales of lucrative
lice, the Taj Mahal of privies, and animal-rights gone awry are eclipsed
by the latest development in the Continuing Saga of Digital v. Intel.

In our last exciting episode, Intel filed a countersuit against Digital,
which came as no surprise. But the Monday October 6 issue of the Wall
Street Journal carried a rather surprising story: DEC planned to sell
the Alpha architecture, and all rights and privileges pursuant thereto,
to Intel. In exchange, the report claimed, Digital would pocket
$1.5B-about two weeks of Intel's net profits.

Suffice it to say that this story generated much grist for the rumour
mill, and an equal number of questions at the DECUS Europe get-together
in Copenhagen, Denmark. Given the Current Unpleasantness between Digital
and Intel, VP Jesse Lipcon couldn't comment extensively on the rumoured
selloff of one of DEC's few remaining family jewels.

Lipcon did explicitly and emphatically state that Digital remains firmly
committed to the Alpha architecture; that two more generations are on
the drawing board; and that Alpha customers can be assured of continued
access to industry-leading technology. (A terse corporate directive that
SKD examined rendered it clear that Lipcon could go no further).

Subsequent articles published by Reuters and the San Jose Mercury News
backpedaled on the WSJ's "Digital's gonna sell Alpha lock, stock, and
barrel" take. Briefly, the articles claimed that Intel would pay Digital
$650M for the firm's still-underutilized Fab-6 plant, plus up to $200M
for rights to the technology behind the Alpha chip. The articles further
claimed that the proposed settlement would ensure that DEC becomes a
strong supporter of Merced, ostensibly because the firm no longer would
have to stress Alpha as an Intel alternative.

While still speculative in nature, these articles appeared more credible
to SKD. The outright sale of the Alpha franchise seems irrational, given
DEC's investment in the Alpha program, and the architecture's
performance superiority. But DEC would have no problem licensing Alpha
to Intel-in fact, Intel refused DEC's licensing overtures in 1991.

Digital might or might not be interested in unloading its semiconductor
foundry in Hudson, MA, but Fab-6 no longer imposes the financial drain
it did several years ago. If Intel is experiencing capacity constraints
and DEC wants to go the fabless route, a sale might make sense. 

As for de-emphasizing Alpha as an Intel alternative, Digital already has
adopted a dual-architecture strategy wherein the Alpha and Intel
architectures do not compete head-to-head. While the 21164PC might
change things at the low end, the projected performance of Merced and
circa 1999 Alpha technology renders it likely that DEC can maintain its
performance differential, thereby ensuring roles for both Alpha and
Merced in the future.

Obviously, neither SKD nor any of the other usual journalistic suspects
have many details on the DEC/Intel talks, but some likely conclusions
can be drawn by applying a quid pro quo formula.

What might DEC be looking for? As we reported quite some time ago (see
"Digital v. Intel: Andy Gets a Big Surprise," SKD V4N10, May 15, 1997),
a technology cross-licensing agreement is among the Maynard firm's key
agenda items. Digital would clearly benefit from guaranteed early access
to Merced technology, as well as guaranteed early access to Intel's
extensive pre-Merced 32-bit roadmap. DEC would like to see some money
come its way from Intel,  too. Finally, a rapid return to status quo
ante bellum would preempt a potentially devastating competitive
knockoff: "Given the uncertainties surrounding DEC's access to Intel
technology, is the continued purchase of Intel-inside systems from DEC a
responsible business decision?"

What might Intel be looking for? A quick and dignified de-escalation of
hostilities would be a Good Thing for both parties. Moreover,
contractually-legitimized access to Alpha technology-especially the
cache management and branch prediction capabilities at the heart of the
May 12 lawsuit-would benefit Intel.

Perhaps more important in the long run to Intel is DEC's binary code
translation prowess. Recall that when Intel and newfound partner HP in
June 1994 announced the P7 development program, the companies claimed
that their RISC effort was aimed at producing an entirely new 64-bit
architecture that would maintain backward binary capability with both
the x86 and PA-RISC architectures.

This is much easier said than done, and binary translation seems the
best way to tackle this very complex problem. And nobody does binary
code translation better than DEC. Stay tuned: SKD believes that the
settlement will take a quid pro quo approach to conflict resolution.

This commentary originally appeared in the October 13, 1997 issue of
Shannon Knows DEC, a subscription-based DEC-market newsletter published
in Ashland, Massachusetts, USA. For information
on subscribing, email shannon@world.std.com. Representative articles may
viewed at http://www.hrgresearch.com or downloaded in PDF format from
www.astrumsoftware.com

Cheers,

Terry Shannon



