Thank you for the honor of having my understanding denigrated by you.
I really do appreciate being placed in such distinguished company.

Take a look at
http://www.fujitsumicro.com/products/memory/memory_pdf/mb81410c.pdf,
the data sheet for an arbitrarily selected DRAM chip with fast page
mode. On page 2 there is a block diagram showing the actual bit array,
"4,194,304 Bit Storage Cell", as a rectangular block with the Row
Decoder feeding in to the left hand side and the Column Decoder
feeding in to the top. Even if you are unfamiliar with hardware block
diagrams, this one is fairly simple and clear. At its center, this
DRAM is a 512x512 array of 16 bit items.

If you don't think this has any performance significance, consider the
following quote from near the bottom of page 4: "The fast page mode of
operation provides faster memory access and lower power dissipation.
The fast page mode is implemented by keeping the same row address and
strobing in successive column addresses."

Someone or something is going to take your linear address space model
and map it down to a memory bank select, a row address, and a column
address. The row and column addresses will be passed consecutively to
the address pins of the DRAM chips in the bank. 

The mapping between a program memory array and a flat address space,
at its very simplest, is equivalent to the mapping between a Fortran
multi-dimensional array and a flat address space. In general purpose
computers one rarely meets the simplest case - the mapping between
data address spaces in a program and computer memory is usually
substantially more complicated than the Fortran multi-dimensional
array mapping.



