I wrote:
: Another point is that Fortran
: applies exactly the same rule to *all* operators:  if the compiler
: can determine that the *result* of some subcomputation is irrelevant
: to the value of the whole, it can delete that subcomputation entirely.

"Paul D. DeRocco" <pderocco@ix.netcom.crud.com> replied:
>This is certainly true of C++, whose current draft standard
>states that the observable effects of running the program must
>be the same as if the program were executed as described in the
>standard. It's commonly called the "as if" rule.

You have drawn a false conclusion.  C++ (like C) is entitled to
delete a subcomputation if the *EFFECTS* would be the same.
Fortran is entitled to delete a subcomputation if the *VALUE*
isn't needed, *REGARDLESS OF ANY EFFECTS*.

To take a fairly extreme case, consider
	extern int system(char const *);
	int i;
	i = 0*system("rm *");
A C or C++ compiler is unable to delete the call to system(), because
it has an *effect* which would not take place if the call were deleted.
Whereas, give "the same thing"
	INTEGER SYSTEM
	EXTERNAL SYSTEM
	INTEGER I
	I = 0*SYSTEM('rm *')
to a Fortran compiler, and it is entitled to delete the call to SYSTEM,
because the *value* is irrelevant, and the fact that major effects will
no longer take place isn't allowed to matter.

I do wish people would read more carefully.
-- 
