> > hardware, but it really is there and it really can make a performance
> > difference.   
> 
> Does the OS know about it? If not, it is totally irrelevant, and
> you have missed the whole point. Next, you are going to say that
> because the chips themselves are arranged across on the simm/dimm
> models, which are arranged sequentially, that the memory is arranged
> three dimensionally. You obviously have no clue as to what my
> point it. The platforms I use have one dimensional flat memory
> spaces, and if I cannot access the mythical underlying structure
> you hint at, what is the point? What is your point, anyway?

The question is not whether or not the OS knows about it, its whether
you as the programmer know about it.  In fact almost every thing
inside a computer has either two or more dimensions.  If you actually
ever looked at a memory sub-system, you would find that its is made of
of a block oriented 3D pyramid stucture, with hard drives being the
base and the register being the tip.  Actually dram itself depends
heavily of the actual thing accessing it to know about its internal
structure in order to get good memory performance.  And AFAIK, one
dimensional flat memory spaces die out a long time ago.  What we have
today is multi-dimensional multi-segmented memory conglomorations.
Now as a programer you can treat them as 1DFMS and piss away
performance, but personally if you did, I wouldn't run anything you
would write.

aaron spink
not speaking for dec



