Steve Tate wrote:
> A while back (this is probably 5 or 6 years ago) I was trying to get
> as fast an FFT as possible, so I was experimenting with split-radix
> FFT.  Being a C programmer first, I implemented this in C and spent
> quite a bit of time getting everything as efficient as possible.  When
> I was done I decided, on a lark, to implement the same general
> algorithm in a very straight-forward fashion using FORTRAN.  I spent a
> couple of days timing and tuning the C code, and about an hour on the
> FORTRAN code.  And the FORTRAN code was probably 5% FASTER.  That
> surprised me quite a bit.  Now it could very well be that I was using
> gcc as a C compiler, which isn't the greatest compiler in the world,
> and I was using a propriety very optimized FORTRAN compiler.  So maybe
> a better C compiler would give me that 5% of runtime speed back.  But
> what would give me that 2 days of C tuning time back?

A while back (3 years ago, IIRC), someone in my university donated a
bottle of champagner (which was turned into a chinese meal later) for
the first student who exceeds 30 MFLOPS on our 50 MHz PA-RISCs on
500x500 matrix multiplication (using the standard O(n�) algorithm). The
naive fortran version got around 10 MFLOPS (compiled with +O3, where the
compiler replaced the multiplication with a call to mat_mult()), the
naive C version got around 0.2 MFLOPS (because it was heavily swapping,
on machines with more memory, it got around 1 MFLOPS).

After a short discussion, whether this is possible, and why champagner,
I decided to do it, and I got just above 30 MFLOPS, using GCC. Using
HP's C didn't help (it was slightly worse); I examined the assembler
output, there was nothing to improve.

Well, it was hand-blocked for registers and memory, hand-layoutet so
that it got the best out of GCC's weak scheduler... and it took me two
days (certainly not full 9-5 days, that was on university).

It would have taken longer to generalize the approach (either by writing
a general matmul function, or by writing a generator for specialized
matmul functions), and certainly, this result is _not_
language-dependent (I could have get it out of Fortran, too). Any
language with a code generator that allows me to write code that matches
the machine instructions and allows me to choose my memory layout does.

I often use C, especially GCC, as portable assembler. GCC's built in
operations map to many instructions (including indirect branches, which
are missing in ANSI C), and when it doesn't, a bug report often helps
great. Writing fast software is complicated, it requires deep knowledge
of the machine you use, and 99.9% of the programmers will get better
code for numerical programs if they use the better compiler (thus
Fortran in most cases).

For writing larger applications, I don't use C, because it doesn't allow
the design methods I like to tune these applications. I use Forth.

