My first expose to assembly was the 68k, so I'm kind of partial to it :) 
That said, I don't know anyone who didn't like programming it.  It always
was like the hypothetical processors in textbooks - simple, powerful, and
useable.  You could understand it and do useful stuff with it even if you
never touched assembly before in your life.  I once wrote a routine for a
Mac program i was working on that took a point input, and checked to see
if it was in a list of rectangles (provided by a pointer).  Did that after
playing with 68k assembly for a week.

Interesting thing is when you drop into Macsbug, if the machine was
emulating 68k at the time, it comes up with the 68k registers, etc, and if
it's powerpc, it comes up with the powerpc stuff instead.  Neato..

