Let's take a course in macroeconomics.

Option A:   Individual pays for medical school, using loans, work study,
whatever. Upon graduation, they have to pay back the loans, resulting in
higher medical costs.

Option B:   Government pays for medical school. They raise taxes on _all_
products and services (or income, which amounts to the same thing) to pay
for it.

A is a lot less intrusive.

A is more likely to result in economical use of resources because the
consumer is the one paying.

A  is more efficient because an individual has less overhead than the
government.

A is more fair in that the people who use the services pay for them.

The problem is that you think that the government's money comes from
nowhere. It comes from the same people who would pay the doctor--except
that you turn over control of your life to the government.

> 
> >
> >Again, how does your proposal for a "free" higher education improve on
