boo <boo@tonys.ecology.umn.edu> writes:

 
> One other thing.... the important thing really is cache memory,
> not DRAM. Even if you have several cache lines, they still
> are cache LINES. Do you see what I am saying? If you can show
> me some dimensionality of the cache (or other) memory that
> I can actually take advantage of at the assembly level, then
> I would love to hear about it. Seriously, I would, because I 
> would use it, so please enlighten me. Some of the arrays I have
> played with in F90 have been 6 or 7 dimensional, so this is 
> quite important.
> 

Well, since you mentioned assembly...

A lot of future enhandments to architectures are going to be in part
comprised of things like prefetch buffers and streaming buffers.
Basically EXPLICITLY user controlled caches.  Another thing, the DRAM
does matter!  If I'm going to organize an array of data, I want the
layout in such a way that I can achieve maximum bandwidth from the
memory system.  Most compilers don't support this yet... They will.

Programming in a flat memory abstraction is fine.  But languages and
compilers that can exploit increasingly complex memory systems will,
in the end, give the best performance.  Because of the structure of
the fortan language it is easier to adapt a lot of these memory
intensive optimization to it than C.

aaron spink
