boo wrote:
...
> No, you don't understand me. I want to do the nitty-gritty
> myself. As I said, computer memory is NOT multi-dimensional,
> and I do not trust compilers to do a proper job. Perhaps someday
> compilers will do the job properly, but until then, I want
> the control myself. The thought of CS snobs like you having a
> hand in the process is scary.
> 
> And I don't care about what someone did before I was born.

Why do you say "computer memory is NOT multi-dimensional"? Every
computer memory structure that I have looked at in enough detail for
analysis of its performance has been multi-dimensional. In the case of
a large server, memory may be an array of modules each of which is an
array of banks where each bank is implemented by an array of memory
chips, each of which provides a two dimensional array of data bits.

Some levels of this may be missing in smaller systems, but it is
difficult to avoid having multiple memory chips, or having separate
row and column addresses in talking to each chip. This may have
performance impact. DRAM chips often have a mode in which repeated
access to different columns but the same row can be done significantly
faster than if the row address were changing.

In a virtual address system, there is a layer of array access between
you and the hardware in the form of the virtual-to-real translation.
Virtual memory at its simplest is a two dimensional array indexed by
page number and offset within page.

Cache hierarchies are also multi-dimensional structures, in which
there may be arrays of lines that are divided into arrays of sublines,
which are themselves arrays of bytes. Fitting well with the sizes and
organization of these units may be crucial to the performance of
memory intensive programs.

Even in a real address OS with no caches, any view of a flat
one-dimensional computer memory is just an abstraction that hides much
of the nitty-gritty.

[boo, I don't know whether you are old enough to care about this -
please ignore if early 70's are before your learning cut-off date.]

Even before memory chips, the first memory design that I looked at had
arrays of square units, each of which was a two dimensional array with
wires strung in both directions. Each bit was stored in a small
magnetic rod at an intersection of the vertical and horizontal wires.



