On 11 Oct 1997 08:52:18 GMT, hisys@rmi.net (HI Systems) wrote:

>In article <343640a9.1784105@news.micro-net.com>,
>Aaron Bilger <syserror@micro-net.com> wrote:
>
>>  Real
>>monopolies, where you have no choice and must deal with one company at the rates
>>they demand, seem to be the realm of the government.  For example, electric,
>>water, or cable companies granted by government the right to be the only company
>>of their kind in an area.  Government laws work against free market.
>
>Actually, utilities are a good example AGAINST your point; you need to
>read more history, Aaron.  They formmed effective local monopolies
>*before* government got involved. 

Of course the first company into a new industry is going to have a period of
effectively being the only supplier until others have time to form and develop
and compete.  This principle is forced even in realms of intellectual property
rather than real property such as the laying of water pipes, by way of patent
laws.

>Theoretically, serveral companies could
>string power or bury water pipes in your neighborhood, but when this was
>legal, it *rarely* happened, because of the nature of the technology and
>infrastructure investment.  You might say that the entrenched company (pun
>acknowledged) had unusually high ground from which to fight off
>incursions. 

*grin*  

On what average order of time are you claiming between the first company
developing its infrastructure and between either a) other companies competing in
the same area or b) government further entrenching the monopoly?

>This dynamic was also true of telephone, and most of the US wound up being
>covered by one giant company, which despite being a regulated monopoly was
>reasonably efficient.  But not as good as competition might create.  So a
>few years ago it was recognized that with the new programmable electronic
>switches, alternative long distance companies were not techincally
>feasible.  

Did you say the opposite of what you intended?

>In a sense, yes, government laws work against a free market; it is only
>because of government laws that the slavery market is in such bad shape

Free trade is based on recognizing people as rational free individuals who can
justifiably interact with voluntary trade but not by initiating physical force
or fraud.  From this, slavery is clearly unjustified; its existence in the early
U.S. certainly departed from free trade based upon individual rights, and that
departure was bound by government law, including the U.S. Constitution.

>today.  Sometimes society decides a given market is not a good thing, and
>shuts it down or limits it.  Or even in a well respected market,
>government may "intervene" to keep it functioning well.  For example,
>shutting down frauds in the stock exchange - so that the stock exchange
>can work *better* as a market, than it would if it were completely "free"
>of intervention and anything goes was the rule.

Again, stemming from individual rights, fraud is in violation of rights and
opposed to free market.  Organizations, even up to the scale we would term a
government, are justified in combatting fraud.  However, the actions of
government which fall into such a justifiable realm are indeed small, and most
actions on its part are by initiating force against those who have not been
violent or fraudulent themselves.

>Look at the stock market.  Up until the 1930's, the economy had been stuck
>and a very firm cycle of boom and bust every couple of decades.  The 1929
>crash was just one of many; it assumes such proportions for us now because
>it was the LAST of the series.

It is true that throughout the 1800s there were cycles of bull markets and
recessions, but nothing near the magnitude of the crash in 1929.  The last had
the unique factors of occurring after the Federal Reserve Board was formed in
1913, which controlled interests rates, allowed banks to lend past their real
capabilities.  By maintaining artificially low interest rates throughout the
1920s, this government institution encouraged overinvestment in ventures not
sound enough to be backed were banks relying on their own funds for lending, and
this overreliance on bad investments and lending funds which weren't really
available came to a crashing halt in 1929 when people suddenly realized 'the
emperor has no clothes'.

>These boom/bust cycles are natural oscillations.  During booms there is a
>lot of money to be made, and investors build production capacity quickly,
>until supply outstrips demand - then there are many failures and things
>get bad.  Eventually, it recovers and another boom results.  This has
>nothing to do with government. 

True, natural cycles would result, but it certainly helps to have a suspension
of normal regulators on the economy, such as the Fed and artificial interest
rates, to allow as extreme a low as the great depression.

>It would be a shame to let ideology blind us to the richness of our
>combined experiences over the past century.  It has been an extraordinary
>lab in which to compare ivory tower theories with actual results.

The ideal is a system recognizing peoples' rights as rational individuals, to
trade but not to coerce.  I am confident that people aren't so hopeless as to
make such a laissez-faire system impracticable, and don't think they need
coercion of government to keep them in line, 'tune their engine' or whatever
other analogies.  If, however, enough people are so intrinsically violent or
irrational as to make laissez-faire an 'ivory tower' philosophy and necessitate
a forceful violent state to oversee them, what then?  If you thought you were
dealing with a world where people needed a system embracing coercion, why stop
at trying to get a 'controlled market economy' rather than simply scrambling to
become the agent of coercion rather than its subject?  Without treating people
as rational and capable of voluntary interaction rather than force, why would
you want anything less than to head a tyranny?  (and these are not rhetorical
debate tools, I sincerely want to see what philosophy and ethics you can have
underlying your position)


Aaron




