> > Is Digital really selling all rights to the chip itself, or just the
> 
> I think that this article, of all of the press is probably the closest
> to the truth.
> 
> Intel have been accused of stealing technology. Buying the rights to use
> said technology will relieve part of that suit. Buying said technology
> in total, would  an effective admission of guilt and would be a dramatic
> indication of rampant stupidity on their behalf, as that process would
> be only be effective if they bought the Alpha Technology and then shut
> it down (anything less would be an admission of guilt).
> 
> In my opinion, what we see here is stupid speculation around the first
> part of reasonable negotiations.
> 
> Selling Alpha Technology for a one off payment of $1.5billion would get
> Bob Palmer sacked from the board of Digital. Digital Spent over $1B
> getting alpha going, and while it doesn't have the market share in raw
> chip numbers, it has a quantum level of performance and functionality
> over its competitors, certainly worth more than $1.5B (I wouldn't be
> surprised if DEC sold more than $1B worth of Alphas a year...)
> 
> I would put the total sale of Alpha at a Price tag of closer to
> $30-40B,  the sort of investment that Intel would only make if were to
> abandon the X86 line totally. (Yes!)
> 
> Me, I'll wait for Mr Matco's cut on things...
> 

Well, Chuck and I were at DECUS Europe in Copenhagen to field-test a
beta one release of MATCO Knows DEC. As such, we missed all the
brouhaha. My take is similar to yours: both firms could use a technology
cross-licensing agreement so such a settlement makes good sense.

Selling Alpha lock stock and barrel for $1.5B doesn't make good sense.
As you pointed out, the Alpha run rate is over $1B per year, probably
closer to $2B.

Note that Intel had the opportunity to license the Alpha architecture
back in 1991 and chose not to. This, and Intel's comments during the
initial Merced "announcement" many, many, many, months ago ("P7 will
be much better than Alpha...") fly in the face of an acquisition.

Now, Intel would certainly benefit from legitimate access to DEC's cache
management and branch prediction expertise, and DEC just might benefit
from access to Intel technology, especially if the former firm might be
thinking about adding additional Intel compatibility to the Alpha
architecture.

We live in interesting times.

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC



