Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> 
> In article <343D64F1.167E@lanl.gov>, William Clodius <wclodius@lanl.gov> writes:
> > Fortran currently also lacks a standard defined preprocessor. This may,
> > or may not, be viewed as a problem for Fortran. The received wisdom on
> > preprocessors is that they can help performance and portability, but
> > their effect on code maintenance and legibility may not be worth the
> > price.
> 
> As for performance I have only seen the C preprocessor to hinder
> compilation speeds (presumably run-time performance is not the
> issue since some combination of libraries and inlining can make
> code from sophisticated compilation equal to that from any sort
> of preprocessing).
> 
> But as for compilation speed, taking a full set of VMS interface
> definitions ("Starlet") and using them from C slows down the
> compilation _considerably_ compared to compilers like Bliss,
> Pascal and Ada which make use of precompiled libraries.  DEC
> releases a new version of VMS only once a year or so, why should
> I recompile the interface definitions every time I compile ?
> 
> As I understand it, the reliance on preprocessors is actually
> required by the definition of the C language and it is impossible
> to implement a C compiler which uses precompiled interface definitions
> because one is supposed to be able to add definitions before those
> which are "include"ed and have those added definitions affect the
> "include"ed ones.
> 
> Larry Kilgallen

On PCs pre-compiled header files have been a standard feature for
some time (driven primarily by the requirement to include
Windows header file when Windows 3.0 took off).

The compilers keep a database of precompiled header information
for each source file in a project. If you change the include files
or add definitions ahead of the include files then this is detected
and the precompiled header information is regenerated by the
compiler/preprocessor.

So it's not impossible and it is workable.

-------------------------
Andrew Cruickshank.




