In comp.sys.dec Ben Myers <benmyers@ma.ultranet.com> wrote:
> Does anyone have technical info about the DEC RF35/RZ35 hard disk
> drive?  The DEC websites don't, or at least I can't find anything.
> The drive is labelled "RF35/RZ35" To be specific:

  DEC got out of the disk drive business, and as is usual when DEC stops
doing something, all information related to that item is removed. This
drive was discontinued on 7/1/94.

> 1. The drive uses the same HDA in its two configurations?  Does the
> RF35 use a different printed circuit board than the RZ35????  Or is it
> the same?

  It's the same physical HDA. The factory format data is different.

  The circuit boards are different. The RF series are DSSI and the RZ series
are SCSI. DSSI (Digital's Strange Storage Interconnect 8-) is electrically
similar to single-ended narrow slow SCSI, but the protocol is _completely_
different.

> 2. What are the jumper settings for the jumper bank opposite the SCSI
> and power connectors?
>
> 3. The drive has a 5-prong "special DEC" power connector.  What are
> the voltages?

  If you have the 5-pin power connector, I think you have the RF version of
this disk. Unless you have a DSSI system (in which case you'd have the match-
ing power connector) I don't think it'll be very useful for you.

  BTW, this is an 850MB disk drive, so even if it was SCSI, unless you had
a _lot_ of them, it probably wouldn't be worth your time to set it up.

