> I didn't like the Z-80, actually.  Even more special cases than the Intel
> 8080 and later 8086.  I mean, it worked fine, but I didn't consider it
> clean.

The nice thing about the Z80 was the quite large number of registers
that could be used interchangebly (except for the accumulator which
was still essential for real calculations).  And you could even simply
take a pair and use it like you had a single 16bit unit.  And you even
had two copies of the whole register set that could be exchanged with
a single instruction IIRC.  (Or was this for interrupt handling ?
Maybe it's time to dig out my copy of Rodney Zacks, if I still have it)
What I know about the x86-architecture reminds me somewhat on the 6502.
Very few registers with a special purpose so you juggle around the data
a lot inside the CPU.  But even then the 6502 had a partially orthogonal
instruction set...
But then I don't pretend to be an expert on x86-architecture.  I once
borrowed a book about intel assembly and gave up in the middle of the
first chapter because it was so unbelievably ugly.

Greetings,
