>proprietary.  

I agree that MS is probably no better or worse than the next guy within
the computer industry; at the same time, MS is in a much better position
to screw a lot more stuff up in many more areas.  I hate to see any company
with that degree of influence over the entire industry.  Such behaviour is
almost understandable (even forgivable) in small start-ups, fighting for
their lives (which Netscape was at one time), but I'd like to think that
a company with the size, influence, and guaranteed future of MS would hold
themselves to higher standards of behaviour.  MS seems not unlike a bratty
12-year old kid suddenly thrust into a position of great power and responsi-
bility; rather than rise to the occasion, and set a positive example for all, 
they channel their power and influence towards satisfying their basest
desires.  What MS needs to do, is to grow up, and start conducting themselves
in a mature and constructive manner.  Those with the greatest power have
the greatest responsibility, and so far MS has been a complete, utter, and
total failure at taking those responsibilities seriously.  They really won't
be satisfied until they have created a monopoly in every conceivable segment
of the industry.  They want to be the Standard Oil or AT&T of the '90s.  I
can already hear people saying, "Yeah, but all companies yearn to become
monopolies; MS is just trying to be as successful as possible."  The reason
this argument doesn't hold water with me is, MS is actually in a position
to do just that.  It's fine for a business to try and be as successful as
possible, but there are lines that should not be crossed, and MS is very
close to those lines.

My viewpoint is complicated by the fact that I also don't think the government
really has the right (or even the means) to step in, shake things up, and
magically create a competitive market again.  I can really only hope that
MS fails on their own, in a free market.

>And we will in the future.  I can't see *any* way for BG to stamp out FreeBSD 
>and Linux, can you?

No, of course not.  At the same time, should MS succeed in wiping out the
commercial Unix industry, I have a sinking feeling that interest in these
OS's will evaporate.  They will be perceived as technical throwbacks, even
though this is not the case at all. :(

  Regards,
    Mike




