Vladimir Petroyevsky wrote:
> 
> On Fri, 10 Oct 1997 09:16:11 -0400, Curtis Crowson
> <curtis_crowson@removeme.emory.org> wrote:
> 
> >Joe Ragosta wrote:
> >
> >> My point was that higher education is a luxury like a fast car, fine wine,
> >> vacations in the Riviera, etc.
> >
> >Higher education is becoming less of a luxury because of the information
> >age. The U.S. would be much better off in the long run if higher
> >education was nearly free.

We CANNOT afford to treat education, whether at lower or higher level,
as a luxury.

Let me tell you a little story.  Back in Irish History, at a time when
we weren't allowed vote, be literate, own land, practice relgion of our
choosing by the English Government, the Irish people understood the
basic need for education.

You could have been walking along a small country road, or perhaps
stumbled upon some small cottage.  The sight there would have amazed
you. In that mud filled ditch, or in that ramshackle house, you would
have found children.....perhaps barefooted, cold and hungry, but
children all the same.....Children at school....Being taught perhaps by
a local who had schooled abroad, or perhaps who had themselves been
taught in a school such as this.  His wages paid in food, clothing or
whatever locals could afford.  Constantly in danger of being captured
and executed for teaching these "muck savages", as we were known by the
Brittish.  Yet there it was, the seeds of education, of intelligence,
being sown under the nose of the greatest Empire of all.  Children
learning to read, write, learning mathematics, history.....  These were
what became known as "The Hedge Schools".  

The children educated in the hedge schools were destined to emigrate,
since they wouldn't be given equal opportunity to start or run
businesses.  They would go abroad, to places such as England, mainland
Europe or perhaps Australia but predominately the United States.
Education in hand, and the clothes on their backs, those that did
survive "The coffin ships" played a major role in the creation of U.S.
political structure, not to mention the building of it's industries and
infrastructure.

You see, education is no luxury to us.  We expect it to be treated like
our right to religious belief, our right to free speech and free
assembly or our right to vote.  As an INALIENABLE right.  We have the
RIGHT to improve ourselves, because we understand how fundamental
education is, whether school or college, to our sense of achievement and
dignity as well as to our nations development.  You see, even 150 years
ago, as a peasant nation of "muck savages", we understood this.  For
what is a human who isn't allowed develop his own intellect to his own
level?  Sounds awfully like a slave to me.  And what sort of a system is
it who denies it's people access to that most basic right and dignity? 
Certainly not democracy.

Now, I'm not saying that education automatically infers intelligence on
people, or that they become better people as a result.  It does however
give to those who wish to go further the skills they require.  There are
many idiots who are for example accountants(no offense to any
accountants here, just an example, can apply to any other profession)who
make vast sums of money.  Does it matter if they are stupid?  If the
society has given them the skills to do this job, doesn't it benefit
from their taxes, which can be used to assist someone else through
higher education? And being "stupid" people, haven't they gone beyond a
life on welfare? Having a higher standard of education not only helps
economically, it gives a nation a greater sense of self, improving even
the lowliest in society who take advantage of it.  After all, isn't
Ireland known as "The Land of Saints and Scholars"?  It's called that
with good reason.  One of the highest standards of education in the
world, a highly skilled and motivated workforce.  Look around you at the
Irish descended people you know.  Chances are somewhere in the past
their ancestors moved there to make use of an education that they
weren't allowed use at home, or if they are more recent arrivals, to
find work for skills they have but couldn't practice back home. 
Wouldn't you say they've enriched YOUR country?

Higher education is not free here in Ireland, and lower education does
have it's costs, but most people can gain access to it.  I'm not saying
the Government must hold everyone's hand through college and pay their
way, but they MUST make it easy for people to gain access to this most
basic of human rights.

Comparing a good education to a porsche or a nice bordeau '72 seems
pretty silly in this light I would say...

Inexpensive education should be YOUR right.  If it's not, there IS
something wrong.

Regards,





James.............



