Maynard Handley <handleym@apple.com> wrote:

> Heck, if universal education is such
> a fantastic scheme for turning out productive adults, why is unemployment
> in France so high?

Well, here is the first of a few fatal mistakes. You can't judge
everything's value based on economic results. Education does not equal
low unemployment. It equals educated citizens. It equals citizens who
can find their own country on a globe (unlike 30 percent of US high
school graduates in the 80s, according to a few reports I read), who can
(and do) read books and do basic math, and who can speak at least one
foreign language relatively well, and who care enough to vote.

The reason France has high unemployment is because the voting citizens
overwhelmingly demand high living standards for everybody. While in the
US the work week is being lengthened, the minimum wage stagnates well
below the poverty level, and retirement is being mysteriously pushed
back or pulled out from under you, in France (and in most european
countries), they are fighting to IMPROVE the lives of their citizens.
Shorter work weeks, earlier retirement, higher wages, more vacation,
better medical insurance. It all translates into less time spent chasing
after The Almighty Dollar and more time living. And speaking of living,
at last count, French citizens live 10 years longer than their American
counterparts.

Yeah, they have more unemployment than in the US. They also have fewer
McDonalds and Ryan's "all-you-can-stuff -down-your-gullet-for-6.99"
steakhouses. Go figure.

>They have free tertiary education. Maybe it's because
> the country is run by pin-heads like you whose solution to any problem is
> to come up with a slogan then claim the problem is solved.

AS opposed to the good ol' US of A, who is run by pork barrelled, money
grubbing, wanna-be venture capitalists, who's sole aim in geting elected
is for the fat kickbacks and free dinners from the Tobacco Lobbies. Oh
yeah, it is the land of the free, the cradle of democracy.



