I've tried to read both sides of the argument in this thread so
far. As far as I can tell, the argument is that, whereas it is
possible to do multi-dimensional arrays in C, it's not possible to
deal with them efficiently, if you write the 'natural' code.

In fact, my reading of it - and I have to admit it's not something I'd
considered before - is that the problem is largely to do with arrays
in general, rather than multidimensional.

The problem with using a construct such as array[i*(x*y) + j*y + k]
(via, say, a macro), compared to array[i][j][k] is the latter is
undefined if i, j, or k is out of range, whereas the former isn't. (It
may be in your application, but the compiler can't assume this.) The
compiler could use that information to improve the dataflow through
your program. Without it you have to jump through hoops, in C, to get
a similar result. Hoops which probably vary from machine to machine.

The other part of the argument probably centers around the way the
various Fortran advocates[1] seem to know what they're talking about,
whilst being insulted and derided by their opponents. I don't know
about the intel and dec groups, but it's a bit out of place on
comp.arch.

Mike[2].

[1] Actually, I suspect many of them are just experienced programmers.

[2] Never written a large numerical processing program in his life; C
programmer; managed to avoid Fortran completely for the past 6 1/2
years.



