Your original comment was that "as far as I understood, computer
memory is not two dimensional", and then you go off on this latest
posting and talk about how you're interested in computers that are
used by many people.  Well the computers that are used by millions of
people DO have two dimensional memory, which you seem unaware of.
DRAM (of any kind) is two dimensional: locations have a row address
and a column address.  This is hidden, for the most part, by other
hardware, but it really is there and it really can make a performance
difference.   Not that this relates directly to the current language
war...

The whole thread here of FORTRAN vs. C is a common religious war, but
let me throw in my 2 cents and risk being called a "crap C programmer"
by people (which would definitely not be true, incidentally).

A while back (this is probably 5 or 6 years ago) I was trying to get
as fast an FFT as possible, so I was experimenting with split-radix
FFT.  Being a C programmer first, I implemented this in C and spent
quite a bit of time getting everything as efficient as possible.  When
I was done I decided, on a lark, to implement the same general
algorithm in a very straight-forward fashion using FORTRAN.  I spent a
couple of days timing and tuning the C code, and about an hour on the
FORTRAN code.  And the FORTRAN code was probably 5% FASTER.  That
surprised me quite a bit.  Now it could very well be that I was using
gcc as a C compiler, which isn't the greatest compiler in the world,
and I was using a propriety very optimized FORTRAN compiler.  So maybe
a better C compiler would give me that 5% of runtime speed back.  But
what would give me that 2 days of C tuning time back?

Bottom line is this.  People arguing that "C is always better" are
being silly.  People arguing that "FORTRAN is always better" are being
silly.  Each has its place.  If I were writing straight numerical code
with very nice predictable access patterns, I'd use FORTRAN and have
the compiler optimize the hell out of it.  If I were doing a
non-numerical problem and had to implement some advanced linked data
structure (for example, an AVL tree or a skip list), I'd use C.

Don't delude yourself into thinking everything looks like a nail just
because you want to use your hammer all the time (a mangling of a
popular cliche there, but what the hell...).

