michael.williams@arm-sponge.com (Michael Williams) writes:

> In article <3440F805.C98BB347@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>,
> boo  <boo@tonys.ecology.umn.edu> wrote:
> >I did not say there was ONE efficient way. If you are just looking
> >for ONE efficient way, then perhaps you should stick with Fortran.
> >As I said before, Fortran is for the lazy, the feeble-minded,
> >the incompetent, and for those who aren't doing anything beyond
> >simple-minded number crunching.
> 
> I've tried to read both sides of the argument in this thread so
> far. As far as I can tell, the argument is that, whereas it is
> possible to do multi-dimensional arrays in C, it's not possible to
> deal with them efficiently, if you write the 'natural' code.

It's *possible* for a compiler to deal with them efficiently.  It's
rare for a compiler to *actually* do so in practice.  Programmers
with sufficient time and/or interest can always "lower" their code
in C -- or Fortran, a fact which nobody seems to have bothered with,
since Fortran already lets you just type it in the "natural" way and
let the compiler do the work for you.

For example, a Fortran compiler in beta test that is nevertheless
widely used, GNU Fortran (g77), in some cases generates substantially
better code if you replace array declarations and references like
"REAL A(M,N)...A(I,J)" with "lowered" ones like "REAL A(0:M*N)...
A((J-1)*M+(I-1))" (or whatever the equivalence would be).

The reason is that the back end it uses is C-oriented, and it has
been pretty well optimized to produce good code for "lowered" C code
to do some of these things, whereas g77, given the higher-level Fortran
constructs (which cannot be expressed in C, ANSI C, or C++), implements
them via back-end mechanisms that are not as well optimized.  (Although
this is being worked on....)

However, some g77 users have discovered that turning on an old
macro definition in the g77 sources causes it to convert the
higher-level Fortran constructs directly into the lower-level C-like
form when it comes to calling on the back end -- and this *also* results
in generating (the same, or similar) faster code.

In other words, while using C *requires* you to "lower" your code
to get anything like decent efficiency *and* use a pretty sophisticated
optimizing compiler to "discern" what you're really trying to do, using
Fortran *allows* you to leave your code in its "original", higher form.

Then, if your Fortran compiler produces great code from it, wonderful;
if not, you can then lower your Fortran code just like you can with C
(though a C preprocessor is not standard with Fortran, you can use
statement functions where you'd use C macros for this) *or* you can
just modify your compiler -- the latter being far easier to do when
it's a Fortran compiler than a C compiler, as illustrated by the g77
example.

Since g77 is in beta test and has only recently had performance issues
come under some scrutiny in terms of actual improvements to the
compiler, I suspect few production Fortran compilers *require* lowering
of Fortran code to get substantially better performance, except perhaps
in rare cases.

Of course, once you "lower" your code -- either by changing your Fortran
code, or by writing your application in C -- you've lost some *other*
features, like ease of debugging, and catching out-of-bounds references
(especially the kind that are out of the bounds of their dimension,
where the complete array-offset calculation is *not* out of bounds --
which is nevertheless a programming error).

A similar example of C *requiring* lowering because it doesn't provide
linguistic features other languages (like Fortran) have is illustrated
by the following Fortran snippet:

  IMPLICIT LOGICAL(A-Z)
  BOOL = F() .AND. (G() .OR. H())

C has no way to express the above.  It *requires* you to lower it, to,
for example,

  bool = f() & (g() | h())

or:

  bool = f() && (g() || h())

C programmers typically claim this "low-level" nature of their language
*allows* them to write "faster" code for a particular machine, but,
in practice, that isn't true in cases like the above, because even
Fortran allows such a thing.  The Fortran versions of the above "lowered"
code are

  BOOL1 = F()
  BOOL2 = G()
  BOOL3 = H()
  BOOL = BOOL1 .AND. (BOOL2 .OR. BOOL3)

and

  IF (F()) THEN
    IF (G()) THEN
      BOOL = .TRUE.
    ELSE IF (H()) THEN
      BOOL = .TRUE.
    ELSE
      BOOL = .FALSE.
    END IF
  ELSE
    BOOL = .FALSE.
  END IF

(respectively).

In both cases clunkier, but in both cases *semantically equivalent*
when it comes to code generation.  That is, aside from other,
unrelated semantic differences between C and Fortran, the compilers
have pretty much the exact same degrees of freedom when it
comes to generating fast code.  E.g. in the first lowered versions,
each compiler *must* ensure that all three functions are called
(and thus their side effects performed), whereas in the second
lowered versions, each compiler must ensure that the functions
are called in order and only depending on the results from the
previous calls, again, due to side effects.

However, the *original* Fortran code is (assuming it's correct)
most faithful in expressing the original problem.  As soon as you
write it in C, you've lost information regarding how flexibly the
implementation can be in calculating the answer, but while it's
in Fortran, the implementation can even do things like run-time
determination of which of F(), G(), and H() will take longest to
run and call them in any order, or call them in parallel, or call
only one or two of them, or whatever -- stuff a C compiler can do
*only* if it does far more global analysis than the Fortran compiler
typically needs to do.  (Although, just as C programmers often overstep
what the standard lets them do, meaning compilers must sometimes avoid
doing otherwise-okay optimizations, the same is true for Fortran.
E.g. a C compiler could, but users might otherwise complain if it
does, optimize "*p1 ^= *p2 ^= *p3 ^= *p4;" in a way that "breaks"
if p1==p3 and/or p2==p4.  But, similarly, Fortran users might complain
if "BOOL = F() .AND. G()" doesn't actually perform the side effects
of F() when G() evaluates to .FALSE..  In both cases, the users are
requiring the compiler to implement a more strict form of their
respective language, something real-world compiler writers must
take into account.)

In summary, C often *requires* you to write *lowered* code instead
of expressing what you mean directly, whereas languages like Fortran
merely *allow* this.

In fact, I was talking with someone at Dakota Scientific Systems (sp?)
a few months ago.  They take (normally Fortran) code doing real
number-crunching and optimize the heck out of it for a particular
platform/chipset/whatever.  E.g. stuff like BLAS, IIRC.

Anyway, what *really* surprised me was that the guy explained that
they do *not* simply take the compiler's output and optimize the
assembly -- instead, they optimize the *original Fortran*, leaving
the optimized code in Fortran!  That is, they move assignment
statements around, and, I'd guess, "lower" the code when necessary
(probably rare), to get the *compiler* to produce the native code.
And, apparently, they get at or near industry-best results this way.

Proof, or persuasive evidence, that pretty much any lowering C requires
you to do, Fortran *allows* you to do, especially in the realm of
numerical computing.

There's little doubt in my mind that C offers great notational
convenience, though.  I'm much happier, for now, writing compilers in
C than Fortran or, say, PL/I, *because* it has `&&' and `||', among
other things.  Yes, sometimes I'd like to use the (missing)
higher-level constructs and let the compiler decide, but in the
kind of code I write (non-numerical), it doesn't usually matter --
the performance relates more to algorithm selection and overall
design than to low-level implementation issues.

But, in my experience, the numerical types who use Fortran *already*
have their algorithm issues worked out, because they're engineers,
not programmers, and because they *have* to.  E.g. it doesn't bother
me much if my compiler is twice as slow as it could otherwise be
on my home PC, at least, not compared to how much these numerical
types are bothered when their latest number-cruncher is 2% slower than
it could otherwise be, when that 2% costs them $10,000 per week due
to it being run on a supercomputer on which they're buying time.

C programmers just don't usually have as much vested interest in
squeezing out that last 2% or so -- after all, their users can just
wait for the next Pentium to come out anyway, while Fortran programmers
include a pretty substantial group that depends on today's fastest
and most expensive computers, and a newer, faster computer only
means they can stuff more data into, or make more accurate
calculations in, their programs, instead of using the next generation
to cover up their programming inefficiencies.

(My take on the difference between an engineer and a programmer:
an engineer takes real-world complexities into account when designing
his system, and thus tends to produce a simple, robust system; a
programmer ignores real-world complexities so he can design an
unnecessarily complicated system.  For example, if Microsoft designed
the Golden Gate Bridge, it'd allow traffic in only one direction at
any given time; if two cars got on at the same time in opposite
