Paul Rubin <phr@netcom.com> writes:

> Craig Burley <burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com> writes:
> > 
> >   IMPLICIT LOGICAL(A-Z)
> >   BOOL = F() .AND. (G() .OR. H())
> > 
> Is this C statement equivalent to the Fortran?
> 
> 	bool = !(!f() | (!g() & !h()));
> 
> Translation: sets bool to 1 (.true.) if f() is nonzero (.false.)
> and g() or h() is nonzero; makes sure all the functions are called,
> but they could be called in any order.

No, forget about the int/bool distinction, that's not what I was
getting at.

The Fortran statement does not *mean* "[make] sure all the functions
are called".  That's why Fortran is "higher-level" in this case,
because it offers a construct that cannot be expressed in C, whereas
it does offer (verbose, but still clear) ways to specify the lower-level
constructs C specifies.  This example is probably not important in
most cases when it comes to generating optimal code, but it does
illustrate the concept, being discussed here, that higher-level
constructs have important advantages over lower-level ones.  And,
Fortran offers many higher-level constructs (functions, boolean
operators, multi-dimensional arrays, adjustable arrays, and so on)
that are inexpressible in C.  I think even C's pointers can be
expressed in FORTRAN 77, using a translation method that would be quite
painful to live with (and thus worthless as a language to write code in),
but there *are* no translations of some common, and frequently used,
Fortran constructs available in the C language.  Fortunately, there
are typically several choices of *implementations* of such constructs
that are available in C, which is why products like f2c (which
*converts*, rather than translates, FORTRAN 77 to K&R C) can exist.
