Paul Rubin wrote:
> <snip>
> Is this C statement equivalent to the Fortran?
> My Fortran is very rusty.... :-)
> 
>         bool = !(!f() | (!g() & !h()));
> 
> Translation: sets bool to 1 (.true.) if f() is nonzero (.false.)
> and g() or h() is nonzero; makes sure all the functions are called,
> but they could be called in any order.

No it is not equivalent. The Fortran compiler can choose any of the
available shortcircuiting paths and hence need not execute more than one
of the functions. However unlike C you have not explicitly specified a
shortcircuiting path and have no standard conforming means of knowing
which (if any) shortcircuiting path it took. In other similar code it
need not execute any of the functions, if they had been previously
executed and the semantics of Fortran implies that the value returned
would not change. I.e., no intervening subroutine call had occurred.

