In article <343C1030.4A86F6D6@tonys.ecology.umn.edu>,
boo  <boo@tonys.ecology.umn.edu> wrote:
>Shankar Unni wrote:
>
>> But if I'm doing numerical analysis or linear regressions, I'll pick
>> Fortran almost any day. 
>
>Hahahah! linear regressions!! I can do those on my watch calculator!!
>Hey, that is some high level stuff you are doing there, thank God
>you have Fortran to help you out! I agree, with advanced (heh) stuff
>like that, you need to have a specialized numerical language.

If you think that you can handle serious numerical analysis on your
watch calculator, you have no understanding of current issues in
high end technical computing.  Data set sizes often run to Gigabytes, and
compute times on high end servers (that run in excess of 10Gflops) runs to
days or weeks.  The problem sizes will continue to grow as compute power per
dollar grows to allow larger problems to be attacked.

The problems are not just of academic interest.  Most every major
manufacturing company uses computer simulation before they start "bending
iron" (actually its seldom iron these days).  Technical computing
applications include: simulation of the whole airplane to improve fuel
economy and quietness, crash simulation of cars to improve occupant
surviability, flow analysis of air filters for better durability and
effectiveness, seismic data analysis to improve drilling success rates,
modeling of chemical interactions to reduce time to market for new
treatments for illnesses, and many others.

The overwhelming majority of existing code in these market areas is in
Fortran and C.  New development is moving to C++ and Fortran90.  In most of
these areas, the common method of programming is to the computationally
interesting part of the code in Fortran and the human interface part of the
code in C.  That method works because each language has areas where it is
strongest.  Some developers chose to forgo the advantages of using both
languages in order to have only one development language, but they also pay
a penalty, either in performance if they use only C or ease of development
of elaborate data structures if they use only Fortran.

All high-end vendors that I know of offer reasonable (and semi-portable)
ways to mix Fortran and C.  For example, Fortran arrays are column-major and
C arrays are row-major.  You mix them by being sure to reverse the order of
array indices when going from one language to the other.  If you want
portable code, you avoid passing certain kinds of data structures between
the Fortran and C portions of your (large) application.  Character strings
in particular tend to have non-uniform representations between vendors.
But vectors of float (real*4) and double (real*8) tend to work the same on
almost all vendors between C and Fortran.

All this "my language is best because it is all I know" discussion is not
very enlightening.  Let's get back on the "language features which will
aid/hender future architectures generate fast applications" topic.

- Patrick McGehearty
patrick@convex.hp.rsn



