Fortran currently also lacks a standard defined preprocessor. This may,
or may not, be viewed as a problem for Fortran. The received wisdom on
preprocessors is that they can help performance and portability, but
their effect on code maintenance and legibility may not be worth the
price. For example, Denis Ritchie at the HOPL II conference (as quoted
in the latest issue of SIGPLAN) had several criticisms of cpp.

Common substitutes for this lack are cpp (Fortran syntax unaware), m4
(Fortran syntax unaware), CoCo (a conditional compilation code (no
macros) with Fortran like syntax that will probably be part of F200x),
and fpp (a Fortran syntax aware variant of cpp that was proposed as an
alternative to Coco by Sun as standardizing current practice).

