>The real question is why has C caught on in such a big way when other
>more powerful languages have not?  There are several answers:

[many good reasons deleted]

I am surprised that no-one has mentioned what may be the most important
reason for the success of C: "The C Programming Language" by messrs K & R.
An excellent book, far better than the language described. A religious text
almost. After reading it, especially early in a career, one is convinced that
"C" not only makes sense, but is even "the right way".

Think about how thinks might have turned out had this book never existed.

The above is a bit tongue in cheek, but twenty years in computing has lead
me to believe that software success owes far more to cultural factors than to
technical merit. This applies to "users" and "corporate IS management" as the
success of MS-DOS, Windows, and now NT amply illustrates. It seems to apply
to programmers or "software engineers" perhaps even more strongly.

The C vs anything else debate often illustrates this. There are a few
central elements to this argument that are common whenever it appears. The
interesting thing is that the same assertions continue to be made persistantly
even in the face evidence to the contrary, and even when they explicitly
contradict each other.

 - C is "close to the machine" and somehow, this is "efficient" or good.

   Even after proof that say Fortran is more efficient for some problems,
   or that Modula or Ada, or lisp is more powerful, or that the whole problem
   could have been solved with a 20 line script, the C proponent will reply
   (often paraphrasing K&R) that C is both more efficient and more powerful
   "since it lets you get close to the machine".
   This assertion continues to be made even after contrary facts are
   demonstrated. This can go on to absurd lengths where some unholy concoction
   of inline assembler and preprocessor abuse on one machine (so sadly no
   longer made) is claimed to be faster than the "same" program in fortran.

 - C is "portable"

   C is certainly common, but even within the confines of the ANSI standard
   there is wide scope for major portability problems. In the database
   world, the major players all have entire departments with 100+ or so
   persons that do nothing except "port" each new release of what is
   essential one very large C program.
   Often the portability argument will somehow co-exist with the "close to
   the machine" argument, even alongside the claim that "if you want real
   performance, you need to read all the hardware manuals and rewrite for
   each machine"

 - C is a simple language with simple syntax.

   This is simply not true. A look at either the grammars, or the language
   reports will reveal many simpler languages. C may be simpler than ADA or
   PL1 or most especially C++, but compared to say Oberon or Modula-3, or
   even Fortran it is not a simple language.
   More strongly, it does not have simple syntax. Forth, Smalltalk, Self,
   the lisp familyi, these have simple syntax. Among the Algol family, some
   of the Wirth languages, especially Oberon have much simpler syntax than C.
   The assertion (that C is simple) is often comtemporanious (but hardly
   supported) with statements that the asserter has frequent reference to
   more than one C book and that the questioner "must not understand the
   fine points" of C.

 - C is terse and elegant.

   For any C program there is a much shorter and more elegant solution in
   some other language. As simple proof, consider a language "just like C"
   but with garbage collection. Most C programs would be far shorter and
   more elegant if written that way. For a really tough test of terseness,
   pick a random perl script and write it in C.  This argument may be
   related to the "simple language" argument, except that is seems to be
   motivated by a subconscious aversion to typing keywords. This is
   almost  explicit in K&R where "{" and "}" are equated to "BEGIN" and
   "END".

 - C lets me do what I want. You can't write operating systems in "other".

   This of course begs the question of how common "writing an operating
   system" is, and ignores several perfectly good operating systems
   not written in C.
   The relevance of this point is also hard to fathom since it is usually
   raised in opposition to the assertion "for some problems C is not the
   best choice".
   The counter argument is that C makes it really easy to do what you
   do not want. Somehow this is felt to be adaquately countered by assertions
   about "competent C programmers" vs "crap programmers". The inconsistancy
   with the "simple language" claim is rarely explored.


Enough of the war, now to the unsupported opinions:

Why is C successful, and what lies ahead?

  In a word, it is mostly "fashion".

  C was initially successful because it had a great book, available
  implementations (especially, available to universities), was easy to host
  on smaller hardware, and wasn't much worse than the common alternatives of
  the time.

  C survives because it is ubiquitous, it will do most jobs eventually, and
  many people (who should know better) are unaware of the alternatives or
  are stuck with it for some reason.

  C has fanatic followers because it appeals to lazyness with its "terse"
  syntax, to Walter Mitty fantasy with "I could write an OS with this",
  and to the need for machismo comparison with its arcane intricacy "a real
  C programmer would just use macros for that". Each SEGV becomes a bonding
  ritual with the C brethren akin to an extended fraternity hazing.

  C++ is successful at the moment, mostly because there are hordes of C
  programmers eager to "progress" to the next level. Since it is unlikely
  that most programmers will ever be able to work at an economicly acceptable
  rate in C++ due to its overwhelming complexity. It also almost completely
  lacking the actual virtues of C.

  Java is likely to be a sucess, perhaps in a dialect form that omits some of
  the tougher parts of the implementation. This will occur not only because it
  it is better than C, or C++, but also because it, brilliantly, looks a lot
  like both. The hordes of not quite successful C++ programmers and their
  managers are going to be casting about for some way out of the swamp.
  Java will be there with all the familiar appearance and without most of
  the hidden minefields.



Who the heck am I anyway?

  I have been programming for a living for almost 20 years. I learned C on a
  PDP-11, where it really did make some sense, and have worked mainly with C
  for the past 15 years. For the last 10 years I have worked at one or
  another of the major SQL database companies on the core server engine(s).

  An SQL server engine is typically a large C program, between 500,000 and 2m
  lines of code. These engines are about as close to "writing an OS" or
  "writing a compiler" as possible. They implement the SQL language processor,
  a file system (usually built on raw partitions), a networking protocol (on
  top of tcp or other transport), and what looks like an OS kernel (asynch
  I/O, threading, sychronization, memory management for threads, etc.)

  These programs ported to dozens of platforms, mostly unix flavored, but also
  NT and others.  They are written, maintained, ported, and enhanced by
  engineering departments consisting of a few hundred persons. The code is
  expected to be useable for up to a decade. New versions are expected no
  more than two years apart.

  Performance (both time and space, both compute and I/O) and reliability are
  considered critical. Vast effort is spent on testing, somewhat less on
  benchmarking, but somehow, performance is easier than reliability.

My opinions about C and software development come from my experience as a
practitioner and as an observer of the DBMS engineering organizations as well
the odd bit of reading here and there.

