In article <61kjsd$i4r$1@goanna.cs.rmit.edu.au> ok@goanna.cs.rmit.edu.au (Richard A. O'Keefe) writes:
   (Oh yeah, for anyone who doesn't know, ESPL was another early SIL
   with *much* better pointer facilities than C has ever had, including
   pointers that could be tied to specific areas and the ability to allocate
   in stack frames; not just in your own stack frame as in alloca(), but also
   the ability to allocate in your _caller's_ stack frame, an idea which has
   been reinvented under the name 'region analysis', if I've got that right).

In systems based on region analysis programmers don't explicitly
allocate in any particular stack "frame", instead they just allocate
as usual and the compiler works out in which stack frame each
allocation should live (and this isn't restricted to the current or
previous stack "frame"). The stack "frames" are generally called
regions (they obviously aren't of a fixed size), so the whole memory
management system looks like a stack of regions.
-- 
