Well, strings are right when what you have are strings: that is,
a collection of characters typed in by a user, or a report being
prepared for output.  PL/I was the easiest of the languages I worked
with for serious projects.  What I learned from PL/I is that there's
a big difference between "natural" and "obvious".  To the PL/I designers,
it was natural that "a = b + c;" meant, when all three variables were
strings, to convert b and c to numbers (by assuming they were encoded
in decimal), add them, then convert the result to a string (again,
decimal).  That's natural to some people, not to others, so it isn't
obvious; C++ proves this by making the "other" obvious choice, where
the same statement means an entirely different thing (concatenate the
two strings).  But I think we've had this discussion before, on
comp.compilers, a few months back.

I agree that using strings where trees and such are appropriate is
"wrong".

