In article <61j84a$soo$1@dismay.ucs.indiana.edu>, bdwheele@indiana.edu (Brian Wheeler) writes:

:The fault light is on, and the console passes all tests (from 7 to 3) and 
:then I get a >>> prompt.  When I press the fault button 'WRITE PROT', 'A' and
:'B' flash.  using 'b' to boot the OS doesn't work.  Do I have a bad drive
:(I hope not)?  Any tips?

  The flashing indicators represent a drive fault code.  Press the
  <FAULT> button once more, and see if the flashing lights and the
  fault indicator light are all extinguished, eventually the <READY>
  light should illuminate, it will tend to flicker when the drive is
  accessed, and when both no port has been selected and when the drive
  runs a self-test cycle.

  Then cycle the <RUN> button out and back in again, and see if the
  drive faults out again.

  If it does, you either don't have it connected properly, or there
  is a drive fault.  (I don't have the drive fault code translation
  tables handy.)

  Use >>> B DUAn, where `n' is the unit number of the drive.  This
  assumes the KDA50 controller is the first disk controller in the
  Q-bus, and this assumes the Q-bus is correctly configured.  If
  this is not the first controller -- such as on a system with an
  RQDX3 controller -- then use B >>> DUBn, where `B' indicates the
  second controller and `n' is the unit number...

