Larry Kilgallen (kilgallen@eisner.decus.org) wrote:
: As I understand it, the reliance on preprocessors is actually
: required by the definition of the C language and it is impossible
: to implement a C compiler which uses precompiled interface definitions
: because one is supposed to be able to add definitions before those
: which are "include"ed and have those added definitions affect the
: "include"ed ones.

Many compiler vendors offer solutions to this problem. (Metrowerks
CodeWarrior and Microsofts Visual C++ to name two.) There are varying
levels of "transparency" involved. As with many things (alias analyisis,
building fast x86 implementations) the solution is difficult, but far from
impossible.

A related problem that often comes up in porting large apps is the
semantics of textual inclusion make the "interface dependencies" of C
programs relatively difficult to determine. And the situation gets worse in
development environments where precompiled header information is passed in
on the command line because the C source code then often lacks any mention
of the headers it depends on. Then there's the "mother include file"
syndrome where every source file starts with an include of a single header
which includes every header than might ever be used in the program.

But salvation is close at hand. I understand Merced will have a "compile C
program" instruction. Its only fair given the CDC STAR "compile Fortran
program" instruction. (Or was it TI ASC? SYMBOL? One of those :-))

