In article <343bcd1d.9621114@news.micro-net.com>,
Aaron Bilger <syserror@micro-net.com> wrote:
>On Fri, 03 Oct 1997 22:20:20 GMT, dosixdos@super.zippo.com (Dosidos) wrote:
>
>>On 3 Oct 97 15:25:23 GMT, wilson@dbit.com (John Wilson) wrote:
>>
>>>But predatory marketing techniques like the IE giveaway DEFEAT capitalism;

Too broad a judgement.  Monopolistic practices sometimes damage the
health of a market system, which is one of the benefits (and costs) of
capticalism.  The IE giveaway may more may not turn out so badly, tho.

>>BS, Government intervention is what defeats capitalism. 

Again, way too broad.  That's like saying cutting people with knives kills
them, lumping surgeons and street thugs together.  There are examples
where government intervention hurt market systems, and examples where it
helped.  If government intervention ALWAYS harmed capitalism, Japan would
still be a third world country, and a poor one at that.  If it always
helped, the USSR would still be a superpower.

The problem with this subject is that some people need absolutes to
believe in and are unwilling to consider the shades of gray (and hues of
color) which characterize the real world.  There are many examples of real
world intervention into markets by governments, and any objective observer
interested in learning rather than in pushing some psychologically
satisfying oversimplification will find that some worked well, some did
not.  This leaves us with the harder question of figuring out in advance
which is likely to be which; some prefer the simpler ideological approach
of painting all with the same brush and substituting fervor for reason.

I do NOT mean to say this applies to the previous poster!  I'm just
speaking of the pitfalls we are walking among if this subject comes up.

>Government has no justification screwing with honest business transactions, even
>company buyouts.  Government is the ultimate coercive monopoly, power to
>arbitrarily take your money and freedom, declare that you give them X or they'll
>lock you up; no company can even come close to that.

Sorta true, but a limited view.  All legal contracts are in the end based
on invoking the coercive power of the state (no more or less than taxes
are). If you don't pay your mortgage, somebody will physically force you
to move out if all gentler means fail.  If you resist with force, you will
be met with force, all the way up to fatal "coercion".  This is just as
much the underpinnings of "honest business transactions" as it is
government - in the end, any degree of force will be used if neccessary.

The point of the system is that rather than let every organization use
force to enforce it's own interpretation of a contract, we give a
government a near monopoly on the strongest forms of such coercion, and
make the private parties go through a process with less biased ajudication
and protections against excessive force.  So First National isn't allowed
to just hire thugs to kill you and take your house if they think you've
violated your contract (in their biased opinions); they have to go through
procedures which include some checks and balances.

For an example of the altenative, reference organized crime, which is a
type of business which must enforce it's own "contracts" because it cannot
call upone government to do so for them.  Don't think First National
wouldn't wind up eventually being similar but for government.

The monopoly holder on these strongest coercions is then responsible to
the voters, a crucial restriction on government but not on First National 
(nor the Mafia).

Basically, there are different feedback/control systems in place for
government and for private enterprise, because they fill different
societal niches with different dyanamics.  Government is both more
empowered and more restricted than business - in different areas.

If there were no government, empowered by the whole of the commonwealth
(the people governed mas or menos) with a near monopoly on major coercion,
and accountable thereto, then companies WOULD in fact have/take that
power.  That is, if there were no county courts and sheriff to evict the
non paying homebuyer, then private enterprise WOULD hire thugs to be AT
LEAST as coercive as government is now, with many fewer protections and
restrictions than democratic government provides.

And whoever had the most effective "enforcers" would win constract
disputes, regardless of techincal merit.  Care to argue your
interpretation of the protection "contract" you have with the local crime
boss, given any ambiguity or unilateral changes?

In this light, the government (near) monopoly on coercion isn't any
outrage, it's a DAMN good thing!

I say near monopoly, because private security guards actually can be very,
very coercive, down to using discretionary lethal force (can't be much
stronger than that). And I have friends who were beat up and thrown out of
a concert for verbally *questioning* the over-reaction of a concert
security promoter abusing a strangers - coercion doesn't get much more
naked than that, and it had nothing to do with government - except that
without government, it would be much worse and less restricted.  The
"private security forces" called in to break strikes in the 30's were a
good glimpse of how coercive private enterprise would be absent a
democratically constrained government - not a pretty picture. 

I'd have to disagree that no company can come close to being as coercive;
many have, when government was ineffective in preventing them from doing
so.  They've killed and beaten and forced people out of homes.  That this
is not routines any more in developed countries with strong democratic
governments is not because companies differ so much, but because of those
same governments.

So government doesn't have a real monopoly on hard coercion, we've
just chosen for good reasons and based on real world experience to give it
the bigget and most crucial chunk. nowadays.

Is this far enough afield, yet?
    Zhahai




