>Being unable to get a car because you can't pay for it is disgusting, too.

That's one way to look at it, especially if you think of an education
as a luxury consumable, benefitting only the lucky recipient.

There is another way, tho.  Education is pretty critical for most folks to
succeed.  The more widely it's available, the closer our society comes to
being a meritocracy, where advancement, status, and remuneration are tied
to ability and contribution (and thus effort).

The more an education is limited mainly to the children of the alraedy
wealthy, the more the society shifts towards an oligarchy or plutocracy,
where advancement and reward are tied to a good choice of parents - and
much good talent is wasted, with folks who could have been sculptors or
architects instead fixing cars.

Of course, this isn't a black and white thing, it's a continuum.  For
example, a society might be structured so that a poor kid in the 99.99
percentile of ability and initiative has about the same chance of success
as a well to do kid in the 25th percentile.  In such a society, it would
still be true that "anybody can get ahead, if they have enough talent
and gumption".  But "enough" may be slightly or wildly uneven in
distribution.

Some believe that a 80th percentile kid (in ability and initiative) should
have a good chance of success, whether of rich or poor parents.  This is 
NOT just some socialist idea!  It is believed by some fervent capitalists,
because it mobilizes the full potential of the society better, and creates
more overall wealth - that rising tide which floats all ships, rich and
poor.

The "differential access to education by parental wealth" model is
actually precapitalist, a feudal residue of the aristrocracy which the
capitalist merchant class fought for social power (and largely won).
In that light, it's seen by some capitalists as a hoary residue which
impedes the efficiency of the market for talent and ability.

You may disagree.  But I'd hardly call the above argument for an efficient
and fair meritocracy equivalent to giving everbody a free car, jet, and
Hawaiin mansion!  The social justifications and ROI are vastly different.

