>
>No one _owes_ you an education. Coming from a large family with little
>money, I had to pay for my own education (B.S., M.S., Ph.D.). Anyone who
>really wants one can get it.

This sounds more motivated by personal, um, scar tissue than by calm
and rational consideration (in the John Stuart Mill tradition) of social
policies.  "It was hard for me, it should be hard for them now that I've
got mine - regardless of what might be best for society".

I also got my education the hard way, without family support.  Luckily, my
talents were of a high percentile sort, so I at least got loans (repaid
in full), as well as student assistant jobs and such.  And I went
to a tax subsidized public university.  I presume you went to private 
schools and paid 100% of your costs. 

However, I also recognize that the public university system was
proportionately cheaper in those days, even accounting for general
inflation; it would be harder today for somebody in the position I was in
then.  Doesn't mean it can't be done, with *enough* talent and drive.  But
I don't feel like sneering would be very appropriate.  I worked AND I got
some breaks.  It would take nothing away from me (and perhaps lay a better
economic groundwork for when it's my turn to retire) :-) if young folks
today got *at least* as good a chance at a good education as I did, if not
better - even if they are only moderately talented (not extremely and
rarely) and have poor parents.

Note that we are NOT talking about "easy graduation for incompetents"
here.  Kids still need to do the work.  This is about wider access, after
which merit comes to the foreground, rather than inheritance.

I would consider that an investment in our society's competitiveness, not 
unlike R&D, rather than a luxury giveaway.

Your maya may vary.
    Zhahai




