In article <614b4u$o5h@rac6.wam.umd.edu>,
Zsolt Szabo <zszabo@wam.umd.edu> wrote:
>>Is fair competition defined as to protect the little guy from the big
>>guy? If it is then Government should have stepped in when Home Depot
>>came to my state and closed every hardware department store in our
>>communities.

Is that all you have?  Around here, Home Depot competes with Builders
Squares, Hugh Woods, Eagle Hardware, Sutherlands, and at least one other
large chain.  There is lots of competition and price matching.   This has
been more a case of a class of stores displacing another class, rather
than a monopoly eliminating competition.

I don't see that happeing with MS...

>So when was the last time that Home Depot gave away their products for
>FREE to force the smaller stores out of business?

If they were that dominant (which they are not), and they could affored to
do so (they can't), and they could make money on their broader product
line while losing it one the one product the competitor survives on, well
they might try.  But none of this is the case.

Part of this, by the way, involves the inherent difference between
information and materials.  The incremental costs of duplicating
information is essentially unrelated to the value of the information, and
typically trivially low by comparison (and shrinking).  How much does it
cost Microsoft to allow 1,000,000 people do download IE?  Not much.
Building and giving away 1,000,000 lawnmowers is quite different - you not
only lose potential income, each outgoing unit *costs* you a bunch.
Microsofts cost is mainly the development, which is about the same whether
10 or 10 million people download it.

>Realistically, there is no alternative to MS these days; it is
>simply too inconvenient for the average person to buy anything else.

Fairly true.

Not sure what to do about it that wouldn't be worse, tho.

>from SGI for another $2000!"), the reality of the situation is that MS has
>a complete and utter market dominance and monopoly. If you want to 
>convince yourself of this, advise some computer-illiterate friends to buy
>a system other than a Wintel PC (and explain the consequences) and see how
>many will choose the other system. 

There is indeed a strong bandwagon effect here, a positive feedback loop
wherein the larger MS's market share gets, the harder it becomes for
anybody else to challenge them, so it grows more.

Perhpas a better test tho: try getting a venture capitalist to fund a
realistic effort to found a new company to take away business from
Microsoft in one of their key markets.  Expect to raise at least $100
Million.  If this can be raised, the the collective wisdom of the market
is that MS is vulnerable.  If not, then the judgment is that MS has a
solid lock - AND that too is self-reinforcing, because nobody else can get
such funding either.

I've oversimplified, of course.  But the ability to raise money to begin a
competitor is one measure of the strength of a monopoly, and the
availablity of competitive alternative should they abuse their position.

If AMD and Cyrix fail, I don't think there will be any other US
challengers to Intel for the PC marketplace.  The entry cost will be too
steep, the chances of success too narrow.  And Intel will have such an
accumulated war chest that it could lose money for years to bankrupt a
competitor, then gain it back.  Perhaps one of the Japanese or Korean
Megacorps would have deep enough pockets to outlast Intel, but even that
would be questionable.

Microsoft doesn't even have that competition, except in some application
areas.  Their Microsfot Home series bombed.  There are alternative to
their office suite, and even to their development tools.  For now.  They
may use their OS advantage to slowly close those doors too.  We'll see.

   Zhahai






