Speaking of that, the 6809 missed a great opportunity.

It had four instructions to load an effective address into a 16 bit
regiser  Three were named LEAx, where x is the register abbreviation.
The fourth loaded the program counter, and could have followed the pattern
and been called LEAP, but they went with the synonym JMP instead.  

Imagine, if they had, some future computer historian with an
incomplete record might theorized that LEAP preceded JUMP...
Like the mythology about Grace Hopper and the origin of the word "bug"...

(There was indeed an insect taped to the log after being extracted from a
relay, but the usage of bug for a flaw precedes computers, so it was a
clever and lasting pun rather than a new term).
