>A free market means no government screwing with people's choice to buy, sell, or
>trade as they please.  If there is only one supplier in a market, you're free to
>start a competing product.

A functioning free market requires that there be effective actual
competition, in order to keep the benefit/price ratio high.

Government interference can prevent competition, or not, depending on the
specifics.  Monopolies can also prevent compeitions, even in the absense
of government.

>It would be absurd to try to claim Microsoft or
>Intel have no competition. 

I think the real question is: Will there be enough *effective* competition
to keep prices in line, 10 years from now.  Some markets have an virtual
monopoly, even tho there are minor alternatives, too limited to
effectively rein in the market leader.  I don't think Linux will go away,
but it may never compete effectively enough to affect Microsoft.

>  Real
>monopolies, where you have no choice and must deal with one company at the rates
>they demand, seem to be the realm of the government.  For example, electric,
>water, or cable companies granted by government the right to be the only company
>of their kind in an area.  Government laws work against free market.

Actually, utilities are a good example AGAINST your point; you need to
read more history, Aaron.  They formmed effective local monopolies
*before* government got involved.  Theoretically, serveral companies could
string power or bury water pipes in your neighborhood, but when this was
legal, it *rarely* happened, because of the nature of the technology and
infrastructure investment.  You might say that the entrenched company (pun
acknowledged) had unusually high ground from which to fight off
incursions.  By contrast as trucking company can very easily expand its
turf, and another one has little "high ground" from which to defend - they
have to compete head on.

So utiliites were gouging people, and the free market wasn't working.
*In response to this* reality there was a rethinking around the turn of
the century.  It was recognized that some services were "natural
monopolies", that is, their inherent dynamics tended to make them
monopolies, like it or not.  The government couldn't force competition.
So, reluctantly, the ideas was to create "regulated monopolies".  This was
NOT as good a a working free market, but it was better than effective
monopolies without regulation.  In fact, it was SO much better, that
nearly every state created a public utility commision and regulated some
utilities as regulated monopolies - because it worked better in the real
world.

This dynamic was also true of telephone, and most of the US wound up being
covered by one giant company, which despite being a regulated monopoly was
reasonably efficient.  But not as good as competition might create.  So a
few years ago it was recognized that with the new programmable electronic
switches, alternative long distance companies were not techincally
feasible.  So Bell was broken up, and Long Distance was opened to
competition, which indeed became fierce - and effective.  It was a good
decision, and it worked pretty well.  But part of the reason why is that
the government set up some rules that created a "level playing ground" to
encourage competition.  For example, your local telco has to treat all the
competing LD companies equally, and let each consumer choose among them.
Without such government regulation, the local telcos might have killed the
competition for LD by favoring the one that gave them the best deals, not
allowing the consumer any choice - or many other failure modes.

More recently, it has become (or started to become) feasible to compete
for local telephone service.  PUCs around the country have opened their
areas to competition; the monopoly is broken.  Again, they have rules
aimed at preventing monopolistic abuses.  It's too early to tell, but this
will probably be a good thing too.

A form of deregulating electrity is also happening.  This is somewhat
harder, because they have to use the same wires for distribution.

Anyway, what we see here is a case of "free market" meltdown, followed by
workable (if not optimal) government supervision for decades, followed by
cautious and successful adaptation to changing technology allowing
competition again.

Basically, markets are great at some things, but have failure modes.
Governments handle some things better, others worse.  An intelligent
and adaptable hybrid has worked better than either in pure form - tho
still imperfectly.

It takes a pretty blind faith to percieve markets as always better than
government (or vice versa), given the historical record.  It's also a well
demolished myth that government intervention always harms market
economies (tho it can do so).

In a sense, yes, government laws work against a free market; it is only
because of government laws that the slavery market is in such bad shape
today.  Sometimes society decides a given market is not a good thing, and
shuts it down or limits it.  Or even in a well respected market,
government may "intervene" to keep it functioning well.  For example,
shutting down frauds in the stock exchange - so that the stock exchange
can work *better* as a market, than it would if it were completely "free"
of intervention and anything goes was the rule.

Sometimes governments go overboard (tho when is a matter of personal
judgement rather than a simple fact).  But they can go underboard, too.

Look at the stock market.  Up until the 1930's, the economy had been stuck
and a very firm cycle of boom and bust every couple of decades.  The 1929
crash was just one of many; it assumes such proportions for us now because
it was the LAST of the series.  Because government stepped in, began
tweaking things.  It's worked for quite a long while - we've had
recessions and growth periods, but no more catastrophic crashes.  And the
market is thriving overall, with sustained growth.  Did the government
intervention harm the stock market?  You'll not find many experienced
people who think so.

These boom/bust cycles are natural oscillations.  During booms there is a
lot of money to be made, and investors build production capacity quickly,
until supply outstrips demand - then there are many failures and things
get bad.  Eventually, it recovers and another boom results.  This has
nothing to do with government.  Similar dynamics can happen in population
dyanmics in a natural ecology, even.  The problem is, the busts are very
hard on people, and on the economy.  People are hurt, talent is wasted,
investments are lost, good projects die for bad reasons.

A free market is something like an unregulated internal combustion engine.
Powerful, valuable.  But likely to run away and damage itself; emitting
pollution; inefficient in many ways.  You can make it even worse, by
regulating it badly (bad laws).  But you can also add microprocessor
controls, fuel injection, catalytic converters, etc - and make a MORE
efficient and less wasteful device than the completely raw original.
Even tho it's still an internal compustion engine fundamentally, not an
electric or steam engine.

A partially regulated market is similar.  If done well, it works better
than a "free" one, with fewer stalls and bent piston rods.  (If done
poorly, it is worse).  This isn't just an ivory tower theory - it's the
real world, tested by experience.

Virtually all markets are "unfree" to some degree; there is something
which cannot be traded, or there is some practice which is prohibited, or
there are some incentives for "desired" goals (like efficiency, stability,
honesty, environmental protection, better lives for humans).  The engine
has been harnessed to a drive shaft and a vehicle, and thus has purposes
beyond playing with itself.

The Eastern European bureaucrats made the mistake of thinking they could
elminate markets, and substitute a fully centrally planned economy.  To be
honest, that had some successes.  It was a near miracle that the USSR was
able to expand its economy fast enough to fend of Germany in WW II.  But
it also has many critical failures; and it requiresd totalitarian control
of the political system as well as the economic system.  Lots of
suffering.

Actually, its failures were not much worse than early, "more pure"
capitalism.  But pure capitalism had been abandoned by ALL western nations
long before, supplanted by a more successful hybrid of social welfare
capitalism.  It was still an internal combustion engine, but with
controls and pollution regulations, etc.  And this hybrid worked better
than both early capitalism, and later communism.

It would be a shame to let ideology blind us to the richness of our
combined experiences over the past century.  It has been an extraordinary
lab in which to compare ivory tower theories with actual results.
    Regards, Zhahai




