The national 32000 series wasn't bad, just too late to market.

And the Motorola 6809 was pretty clean - but it too was too late for its
window, and too slow (lots of microcode for its CISC instruction set).
It disappeared leaving only OS-9 to remember it by.

The 68000 was certainly nicer than the 8086!

I didn't like the Z-80, actually.  Even more special cases than the Intel
8080 and later 8086.  I mean, it worked fine, but I didn't consider it
clean.

And, bucking popular opinion,while the 16 bit 8086 instruction set is
klugy, the 32 bit native set really isn't bad at all - except that the FP
stack was retained, sigh.  And of course, nowadays we like having more
registers.  Hindsight.

I'm hoping the P7 will clean things up.  But it may turn out to be a very
complex device, unfit for carbon based programmers.
