In article <61g30t$o2n@news-rocq.inria.fr>,
Robert Harley <harley@pauillac.inria.fr> wrote:
>
>I think your problem is that C is too flexible for you.  It doesn't
>wrongly assume that there is one way of laying out and accessing
>arrays that is appropriate for all purposes.  For instance you can
>choose a flat array, 'float *a', or a hierarchy like 'float ***a'.

Speaking as someone who is writting a math library in C (in his free time,
but I'm actively grappling with these exact issues), also as someone
who hardly knows Fortran, this _SUCKS_.  You are forced to either:
1) Only accept predefined sized matricies- generally not an option
2) Every time you access the array either type in the whole address 
   equation or use a macro (hideous and it tends to make the code hard to
   read).
3) In addition to allocating and managing the matrix, also allocate and
   manage arrays of pointers into the matrix (with resulting overhead and
   additional complexity).  This also kills any locality of reference
   you're attempting to maintain.  This thread started with the hw engineers
   complaining that C does too much pointer chasing already.
4) Depend upon non-portable compiler-specific constructs.

The problem I have found with C's "flexibility" is that most of the time
it amounts to "Hey, not only can you reinvent the wheel, you have too!
But if, for some braindamaged reason, you want to reinvent the wheel as
a _square_ instead of the generally accepted circle, it lets you do that
as well!"

There are situations where you need to play pointer games (my day job is
writing device drivers).  But it's hard to call being forced to play
pointer games an "advantage".

I don't speak for Bit 3.
Brian

