It isn't... but the code I wrote still looked like C,
with a huge macro library that I mastered quickly 
enough. The CDPEAC macro package expanded into SPARC
asm blocks with specific memory accesses that 
controlled the four VUs. Yes, I know this is all 
dead now, but so what... It really is not a big
deal to learn a new architecture and new optimization
techniques, and to adapt all the time. It used to be
that Fortran was the only way to achieve performance
on the high end vector and MPP supercomputers, but
thankfully that is not the case anymore. I felt I
had more flexibility with C than the Fortran, and
I know that is my personal choice. I still believe
that (with the help of expert C programmers like
Robert Harley, whose routines are being used in
the fastest lattice QCD code running on the T3E)
I can beat Fortran every time. Fortran-like code is 
usually mandatory on seriously custom MPP harware,
unfortunately.

