architecture I am working on, in fact I enjoy it. I do
appreciate that many people have better things to
do with their time. It doesn't take long for me to read
several manuals, and attack the problem using the 
optimal method. As I said before, I occasionally chose
Fortran in the past.... back to the example of the extinct
CM5, I learnt C* and CMF, and mastered both. C* (which is
a parallel version of C, quite beautiful in fact)
produced terribly inefficient binaries compared with 
the CMF compiler. Thank you, Sweet Jesus, for the fact that
it is possible to NOT use Fortran these days! If a situation
should arise in which Fortran really is the better choice,
I would hesitate and then go for it. I don't think it
is going to happen, though.

> (otherwise, you were not using the machine efficiently, I
> don't want to think about what weird accounting, padding,
> and coding tricks this may have induced over time.)

I did things that were simply illegal in CDPEAC, but they
worked, they screamed, and they produced identical results
to my CMFortran equivalent. Except faster, of course, and
that is the bottom line for me. If someone else was going
to pick up the code, I would say I failed dismally!



