> William Clodius wrote:

> > So you are saying that David Chase, [resume omitted] writes lousy C code
> > because he can't get within 10% of the performance of Fortran. There
> > are several possibilities here, not all
> > mutually exclusive

You have to be careful there.  First, I try not to argue from
authority.  Second, I know people with PhDs that I would not
necessarily trust to write C; skill at writing C does not
necessarily correlate with anything.  I do think it was pretty
good code that I wrote, at least in terms of performance.

Also, I am emphatically NOT one of the primary authors
of the Boehm-Weiser collector.  Early user, yes, enthusiastic
fan, yes, author, no.  Hans and I worked on the optimizer-
versus-your-garbage-collector problem in the distant past,
trying to come up with some way to trick a C compiler into
certainly not destroying the properties of the generated
code that were needed by the collector, while still allowing
it to generate decent code.

The point, which apparently has not yet sunk in, is that
indeed, C gets you very close to the machine, and on
modern machines, if you want to get best performance,
this means you need to know all sorts of silly stuff,
and hand-apply atrocious transformations that will not
necessarily enhance performance on the next machine.  The
tricks that were taught 5 years ago no longer apply, and the
tricks that will work best 5 years from now will probably
differ from the best tricks today.  The guys at Tera think
they have a solution to this problem, and I hope they are
right (and I still want to know why they decided to build
the damn thing about of GaAs, as if they weren't taking on
enough risk already.)

boo wrote:

> About 5 years ago, I wrote a large simulation in CM Fortran for
> the CM5 (and you had one of those at lanl, so I think you follow
> what I am saying...), and was quite impressed with the performance.
> I then wrote the crucial routines in C with CDPEAC (macros
> which expand to vector unit assembly codes), and produced something
> that thrashed the THEORETICAL PEAK EFFICIENCY POSSIBLE of CM Fortran at
> that time, according to their manuals.

CDPEAC is defined in what section of the C standard?  You
do know that they're not making those vector units anymore,
don't you, and that they're one of the reasons (but not the
only reason) why TMC is no longer in the hardware business?
Custom hardware sucks.  

(Why do you think we were trying to see how fast
stock hardware could be used to multiply matrices,
given the best possible code we knew how to
generate for a typical numerical problem?)

Note to the non-CM users:  a CM-5 contained a collection
of Sparc chips speaking to memory through a pair of
memory controllers, where each memory controlled
was "enhanced" with (sort of) a pair of vector processors.
Thus, you get 1 Sparc + 4 vector units.  Each VU
executes pipelined memory-to-memory operations, with
a throughput of one per tick.  The clock rate of the whole
blob is typically lower than the current Sparc workstation
clock rate; I don't know if this is the limit of the 
VU performance, or if it was done to make the entire machine
reliable enough to use.  The Cypress chip blobs were clocked
at 25Mhz, the SuperSparc blobs were clocked at 40 Mhz.
(I think this is correct; there were different terms
for the blobs depending upon whether you were counting
Sparc chips, memory controllers, or apparent vector units).

CMF at least, you can translate pretty easily into HPF, and
get it compiled onto an SP-2 or whatever else you choose.
If I remember correctly, the current versions of the PowerPC
ought to give the about the same performance as one of those
Sparc+VU combinations.  40 Mhz * 4 multiply adds/tick gives
you 320 Mflops speed-of-light, versus one multiply-add per tick
on a 300+ Mhz PPC, assuming you can keep the damn thing fed
operands, which is the entire point of the pipelining and
blocking nonsense.  The experiments we ran indicated that
we were unlikely to get more than 2/3 of the speed-of-light
on general-purpose hardware, and this was wildly optimistic.
Compilers and Fortran "optimizing preprocessors" available
at the time did a pretty poor job.  On the other hand, peak
performance from the CM was also pretty rare; I think that 1/6th
peak was considered to be "good enough" at one national lab
(otherwise, you were not using the machine efficiently, I
don't want to think about what weird accounting, padding,
and coding tricks this may have induced over time.)

