This is not condescending?

C's major performance fault is that as defined, and
especially as used, a compiler does not have
a great deal of freedom to move loads and stores around.
C programmers may be very careful to avoid "unnecessary" 
loads and stores, but nowadays, latency is the big problem;
performance is reduced if you do your loads "too late", or
if a conditional branch depends on a result that you computed
"too late".  A "too late" load is typically a bigger performance
hit than a "not necessary" load.  Fortran has many fewer
problems with this, because the aliasing rules make life
much easier for compilers.  The contiguously-allocated
multi-dimensional arrays help with this very much, too,
because it is easier to infer that A(i,w,x) addresses
different storage than A(i+1,y,z) than it is to infer
that

   pa+i*n*p+w*p+x != pa+(i+1)*n*p+y*p+z

Modern hardware goes to some trouble to reduce these costs
in the local sense, by predicting branches and starting
loads early, but this only gets you so much.  Parallelization
can get you superlinear speedup (as in, when I worked at 
Sun, a happy customer observed superlinear speedup on one
of their dusty decks on a 16-processor machine) on a very
good day.  Has anyone anywhere ever taken a dusty C deck,
dropped it on a multiprocessor, asked the compiler to
parallelize it, and obtained even a linear speedup?

Sun's Fortran compiler, back in 1994, would take care of loop
interchange for better cache use.  The dependence analysis
finds many "vector" operations, and these are specially
code-generated as software pipelines to get the best
possible performance.   You can get this sometimes for C
(it helps if the loop contains no stores to globally-accessible
variables) but you'll get it more often for Fortran.
Fortran performance is also pulling ahead on the newer
processors -- friends (former colleagues) told me that
the then-new version of the compilers improved Fortran
performance by 160% over the older compilers, but C and
C++, not nearly so much (I do not recall the figure).
The superoptimizer (converts control flow to very tricky
data flow) is looking better and better on these new
machines, but the combinatorial costs of searching ever-longer
instruction sequences limits usefulness (and the conditional
instructions added to many new processors also expands the
search space, though they also increase the chances of
hitting with a short sequence).

A decent combination of cache blocking, register blocking,
and software pipelining can get you a 20x speedup over
unoptimized code (if I recall, the large-matrix multiply
unoptimized ran at something like 1.5 Mflops).  C compilers,
lacking the restrict keyword, cannot do that (everyone who
knows what the restrict keyword is and what it does, raise
your hand.)  You can do this in C -- I did this in C,
apparently ineptly, since a Fortran program beat me -- but
it is incredibly tedious work, and the peak performance
is not portable (a conservative coding will improve
performance by quite a lot on most machines).
Cache blocking parameters depend on the cache size,
associativity, and line size.
Register blocking parameters depend on how many
registers you have, and registers used for blocking
are stolen from registers used for pipelining.
Pipelining depends on the latency of the loads and CPU
operations, and on where the operands are expected to come
from (dcache? ecache? main memory?  contiguous addresses that
can be prefetched or fetched in a burst?)

And, again, this is slow and tedious work; I normally write
something like 100 lines of debugged code (C, Java, x86 assembly
language) per day over long periods of time (I can count the
lines of code that I have written in the last nine months),
but writing this stuff, my rate falls down to something
under 10 lines per day.  Someone writing similar code in
i860 assembly language at Dana/Ardent/Stardent quoted me
a figure of one line of code per day, for a general case
version of the code (this one someone working on pipelined
implementations of vectorized transcendental functions).

If you were designing a new programming language ( to get back
to the original thread ) it would be a good idea to figure out
either how to make it easy to do alias analysis, or what "rules"
should come wired into the language that are both easy for
programmers to understand and obey, and useful for the compiler
to exploit.  Fortran 77's rules are well outside the bounds of
what I'd call elegant, but they work, and the optimization benefits
are substantial.

Another possibility is to make it as easy as possible
to discover that a program will terminate -- though this is
general-case intractable, there's a large class of interesting
programs for which this can be determined (for instance, all
loops are "for" loops, or all recursion is governed by a
strictly decreasing parameter).  Or, for instance, there is
some datatype that captures the notion "directed acyclic graph",
so that you know that any path traversal will terminate.

I'd also look into some way to make it more tractable to write
programs which use dataflow instead of control flow.  C* went
quite some distance in that direction, and it was a relatively
easy way to get decent performance out of a Connection Machine,
but people always seemed to want more, and I think they tended
to get it with CMF(ortran) or explicit message-passing.  (And,
as "boo" writes, some people programmed the vector units in their
assembly language.  Yuck.)

-- 
David Chase, chase@world.std.com



