Yes, someone who thoroughly understands the target architecture should be
able to beat (or at least get close to) what a FORTRAN compiler's optimizer 
can do, if they are programming in a lower-level language like C (or, if
you want to take this argument to its logical conclusion, assembly!).

IMO, that argument misses the real point: Programmer-hours are expensive.
IMO the real "crap programmer" is the one who chooses to spend lots of time
hand-optimizing their code, when a different design or tool choice earlier 
in the process (including but not limited to language selection) would have 
given adequate (if not exactly equivalent) performance, saved them the 
trouble, and allowed them to work on the next project that much earlier.

I _strongly_ prefer the C programming language to Fortran, but for 
programming straightforward (no fancy I/O and rudimentary UI) quick and 
dirty numerical apps I often use Fortran. The reason is simple: I know 
that doing so will give me the most performance for a minimum level of
of effort invested.

