Bernd Paysan <paysan@no.informatik.spam.tu-muenchen.thanks.de> writes:

>If you compare language evolution
>with biologic evolution, we are just in the midth of the carbonic
>explosion of forms. Most languages known today won't survive on the long
>run, but there will be one small and unregarded with an early form of a
>backbone, and this one will take over later.

And the year 2000 problem is the giant meteor that will kill off the
dinosaurs? ;-)

Actually, I think the metaphorical meteor will be something else. As
has been noted earlier in this thread, tehre is a limit to how much
high-level languages can give you: You still have to tell the computer
exactly what you want done, and there is no way this description can
be arbitrarily shortened.

However, a lot of programming time today is taken with tracking down
bugs. Languages that will make this process easier/faster is bound to
win in the long run. One way is to remove sources of common bugs by
restricting the language. Common bugs in C are caused by the use of
integers to represent booleans. This makes mistakes like "if (x=y)"
pass the compiler, when a better type system would detect that an
integer was used in place of a boolean. This is only a simple example,
which b.t.w. many C complers will detect and give a warning (not an
error) about. Other things that a good language with a good comipler
can outlaw or catch are non-exhaustive tests, undefined variables,
out-of-bounds access and many more. We are probably only in the
beginning of an evolution towards languages/compilers that catch
inconsistent behaviours.

The trend today seems to try to reduce the programming effort by
providing HUGE libraries and hope that these will cover the majority
of what programs need. This can make some programs be quite short, but
if what you want isn't in the library, then you are out of luck.
Furthermore, this trend makes it take a long time to use a language
well, as most of the effort is taken by learning the libraries. Even
experienced programmers spend a major fraction of the programming time
reading manuals or searching in libraries. I think this trend will die
under its own weight. Libraries are not bad in themselves, but they
shouldn't dominate.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)



