I have had to implement multidimensional dynamic arrays in C, and sorry,
it does suck. When a language does not support a common construct well
enough to allow an optimizer to do something intellegent with it, the
language has a serious problem. Bliss has a much better mechanism for
doing fancy array or structure implementations, in the sense that it
allows easy generation of structures like arrays, even in cases such as
symetric triangular arrays, without the wasted space C requires while
still allowing an optimizer to operate. In some senses, Bliss allows
object oriented design and implementation without the mess of C++ (which
at best is a kludge on top of a hack).

C falls under the catagory of if all you have is a hammer, everything is
a nail. Since most universities only teach C now, we can expect a
generation of "experts" who work in the "real world" to spend the next
twenty years rediscovering 1960s level programming issues and solutions.


