On Mon, 06 Oct 1997 18:17:52 -0700, Maynard Handley <handleym@apple.com> wrote:
>You know, this reminds me of what I read in _Wired_ last night. (This was
>a _Wired_ from about 6 months ago that I was reading for lack of anything
>else at hand). In it this guy was whining about how the FCC was going to
>allow the broadcast industry to "get away" with not broadcasting HDTV and
>said author really really wanted HDTV. 
>Isn't this just classic _Wired_ hypocrisy? To complain about how the
>government is telling people to run their lives, until it turns out the
>government WON'T tell a certain group of people you care about how they
>should run their lives.

Um.  You haven't kept up with the HDTV debate have you?  Here's the 
overly abreviated version:

1) All concerned decide they want to do HDTV.  After much time, pain,
   and money they finally get a standard, sort of.
   
2) Broadcasters whine that they need time to transition to the
   new technology.
   
3) FCC agrees and offers to give each station a new channel allocation 
   expressly for HDTV.  RF spectrum of that magnitude is no small gift.
   The catch is that, after (I think) 2005, the stations must give back
   their old allocation.  The bulk of their viewers should have
   transitioned to HDTV by that time.
   
4) Broadcasters whine that it isn't enough time and successfully derail
   attempts to place *any* limits on the time they can hold onto their
   old channel allocations.
   
5) FCC gives in, effectively giving every TV station an extra channel
   allocation indefinately.
   
6) Broadcasters decide that they would rather use the new spectrum and
   technology to broadcast multiple standard definition channels,
   competing with, and effectively duplicating, existing cable service.
   Consumers get no new service.  The money the goverment spent to
   help develop HDTV and wasted and the broadcasters are still holding
   on to two channels apiece.
   
It's thing to ask the government to stay out of your business.  It's
quite another to ask for help from the government.  Get it.  And then
decide the government should have no say in how this "help" is used. 
*That's* the hipocricy.

Fortunately, in the HDTV debate, there is another party who has yet to
speak: consumers.   I think the broadcasters are going to find that
consumers are not willing to shell out the dollars for HDTV sets just to
see more broadcast channels at the old resolution.  Thus, unless they
broadcast real HDTV, the extra channel allocation is worthless.



