Richard A. O'Keefe wrote:
> 
> William Clodius <wclodius@lanl.gov> writes:
> <snip>
> >It lacks stream based I/O.
> 
> I am not quite certain what you mean by this.
> If you mean the kind of thing that is found in C,
> then I have a printed copy of the specification for stream-based IO in
> Fortran somewhere in this office.
> <snip>

This is low on the list of priorities for F200x, but still on the list
of priorities. In principle Fortran I/O remains record based. For files
that do not have a fixed record size, (or for which backspace does not
make sense, i.e., standar I/O to a terminal) the execution of any given
read or write statement cannot assume that it will not be followed by a
backspace command. As a result the file must retain some construct that
indicates the size of the record. For non-record based systems, e.g.,
UNIX this is (invariably) implemented by having an integer at the start
and end of the record indicating the size of the record. For writing a
file to be read by other systems this is a minor problem, but for
reading files produced by other processors, e.g., C, the absence of the
starting integer in the file causes no ends of problems. It is also a
problem for interfacing to hardware, e.g., instumentation, although an
appropriate Fortran specified interface that does not rely on READs or
WRITEs can be an acceptable, if sometimes non-intuitive, substitute.
Most vendors I believe offer this as an extension.

