Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> 
> William Clodius <wclodius@lanl.gov> writes:
> 
> > F90 also lacks pointers to procedures.
> 
> Is that true? Then it isn't a superset of F77 anymore. Or is the way you can
> pass the address of a function or subroutine in F77 not a pointer to a
> procedure?
> <snip>

F90 has this capability. This is typically implemented as a pointer to a
procedure and can be used to emulate some of the capabilities that you
want from pointers to procedures, but it is not an explicit pointer to a
procedure. In priciple in F90 terms you want something like

INTERFACE
   FUNCTION A(x)
      REAL :: A, x
   END FUNCTION A
   FUNCTION B(x)
      REAL :: A, x
   END FUNCTION B
END INTERFACE
INTERFACE(A), POINTER ::C ! define a pointer that accepts real arguments
! and returns real results

IF ... THEN
   C => A
ELSE
   C => B ! All subsequent invocations of C actually invoke B
ENDIF
...
y = C(z) ! Invokes A or B

This is equivalent to C pointers to procedures, and is roughly what is
planned for F200x. In the most general case, you want procedures to
return pointers to procedures as values, i.e., a form of closures. This
is not currently planned.

