In article <61g4i9$u82$1@news.ro.com>, reinhs@sh1.ro.com (Senter Reinhardt) writes:
:Hi all, just curious what anyone might have heard on this...
:We all no for rendering that Alpha's are a solid two times faster
:than Intel, but lately I've started to see some very low prices
:on Dual Processor Intel systems coming out. Since software like
:Lightwave is multithreaded, a dual processor seems to compete very
:well with my nice fast Alpha system. 

  Symmetric multiprocessor (SMP) Alpha systems have been available for
  a number of years now, and a number of these systems are supported
  under OpenVMS, UNIX, Windows NT, and other Alpha operating systems.
  The upper-end Alpha SMP systems support up to 12 processors.

  As for future plans and specifics, I (obviously) cannot comment.

	--

  SMP is not a performance panacea -- given a choice between a
  processor with a rating of 1 `unit of performance', and a dual
  processor SMP system with two 0.5 `unit of performance' processors,
  the uniprocessor is almost certain to have better performance.  And
  it has better single-stream performance.  And SMP performance tends
  not to scale particularly linearly beyond a design-specific number of
  processors -- with an SMP system, adding processors beyond a certain
  number does not help performance.  (I won't get into a discussion of
  how the OpenVMS `Galaxy' work helps here.)

  Also see `Amdahl's Law'.

