Mark Gibson wrote:
> <snip>
> 
> Wow, I haven't used FORTRAN much in at least ten years.  (F77 was new
> when I last did any real work with FORTRAN.)  All this fuss over
> which is better, C or FORTRAN, has made me curious.  Is there a low-priced,
> high quality implementation of the most modern version of FORTRAN? 

Depends on your definition of low priced/high quality. For most people
the answer is no, for some with deep pockets or a restricted range of
emphasis for quality, the answer is yes.

A full F90 highly optimizing implementation is almost invariably
relatively high cost: $300 for a stuent Wintel, $600 professional
Wintel, about twice that for UNIX. With full toolsets, i.e., debugers,
editors, integrated environment, special libraries, another factor of
two or more in price can be introduced.

There are low optimizing compilers, I believe Salford is one, in the
$100 range. There are F90 subset compilers (i.e., they are not supersets
of F77) from Lahey (ELF) and Imagine1 (F), available for free or the
$100 range. The subset compilers are primarilly intended for the
educational market, and will often not be appropriate for professionals.

> Does
> Gnu produce such a thing?

No although G77 does implement some of F90 (e.g. SELECT CASE) it does
not implement modules, pointers, array arithmetic, etc.

> 
> Also, what native support for large-integer arithmetic do the latest
> versions of FORTRAN provide?  Will they allow me to crunch 128-bit
> integers efficiently without writing my own arithmetic routines?

Processor dependent, but relatively easy to use if available. I believe
some vendors for 64 bit machines offer this capability, several of them
offer 128 bit REALs.
