Robert Harley wrote:

> I think your problem is that C is too flexible for you.  It doesn't
> wrongly assume that there is one way of laying out and accessing
> arrays that is appropriate for all purposes.  For instance you can
> choose a flat array, 'float *a', or a hierarchy like 'float ***a'.

C's flexibility has two problems:

1. it is mandatory.
2. it is confined to a set of uninteresting choices.

In C, can I choose to have the compiler autoparallelize my dusty
deck onto a 16-processors SparcServer 2000?  In C, can I choose to
have my loops automatically interchanged for better cache use?
How about cache blocking?  Any automatic vectorizers for C?

Oh, I guess I don't get those choices.

Given detailed knowledge of a particular chip, working in C,
my attempt at a too-big-for-the-cache double-precision matrix
multiply obtained only 30 Mflops (this was on a 50 or 55 Mhz
SuperSparc, back in 1994).  The code in Fortran was about
1/10th the size, and ran at 34 Mflops.  Both bits of code
contained some hand-optimization, but the C code had much
more (the Fortran was simply blocked for cache locality;
the C code was blocked for both register and cache locality,
as well as software pipelined).

Keep in mind that most C programmers don't know how to
software-pipeline a loop.  Most of them are not sure how
to do register blocking.  Most of them are not exactly
sure how many double-precision registers are on the chip
they are using.  Most of them don't know the size of the
cache on the chip (chip? how about box, since ecache sizes
can vary).  I'll bet most of them don't know what cache
blocking is.  And, if they do, I'll bet they forget to check
to see if the ecache is physically-indexed direct-mapped
or not, and whether the OS is taking care to "color" virtual
pages so that non-colliding virtual addresses will also be
non-colliding in the external cache.

This stuff is awful to do by hand.

And this, of course, was just matrix multiplication, it
wasn't something tricky to optimize for locality, like
GEPP or QR (for QR, so I am told, you must use a
different algorithm to get your factorization if you
wish to block it for locality).  This code is really
tedious to write.

So, this flexibility gets me what?  The privilege of writing 10
times as much code, that runs 10% slower, and is harder to port,
understand and maintain?

It is mind-boggling to me how persistently ignorant some people
working in C are.  Someone told you once that C is the fastest
language, and it grabbed your brain.  Maybe it feels like it
just must be fast, because you work so hard to make it fast.
Maybe you think that all high-level transformations alike,
that since (e.g.) SETL generally runs pretty slowly, that
all languages which are not "close to the machine" must
therefore run slowly.  It is not so.

-- 
David Chase, chase@world.std.com



