This is an interesting viewpoint but it, IMHO, misses the larger issue.
The picture you paint is of (in some form) society rationally deciding "If
there are more of us [via agriculture] we can dominate everyone else". But
what's the point of dominating everyone else if you still have to work
like a slave? A rational society would agree to limit its numbers and
allow everyone to live the good (hunter-gatherer work two hours a day)
life-style.

For this reason, I don't feel the causality you are presenting below is
accurate. I believe, rather, that its fundamental biological urges (not
society in the form of agricultural societies, capitalism or anything
else) that drive people to flood the world with more copies of themselves,
to the point that we all have to work hard just to keep ourselves afloat. 
Ignoring this and blaiming society for the problem, is, IMHO, a pointless
exercise doomed to failture because by re-arranging society but not
addressing the biological issues, your new society, communist, anarchist
or whatever, will continue just as before to generate baby people until no
more can be sustained while everyone works very hard.

Maynard


