We'd probably all be better off sticking to facts. While I can't speak for
Windows, MS IE 3.01 on a mac has three major advantages over Netscape,
advantages that have not been added to Communicator (in spite all of the
useless crap that was added to communicator):

� IE has much more intelligent scheme for allowing one to type whatever
one remembers of a URL and have that resolved into something useful.
Attempts are made to add stuff at the beginning and end of what is typed,
to match earlier URLs typed in etc. And the system is smart enough to
ensure that whatever if fills in for you about which it is not sure, is
already selected so one can just hit the delete key to override it.
��IE keeps a history of everwhere one has been over the last two or three
weeks, nicely organized by date. I find this invaluable.
� IE has the download manager that queues up FTP requests and trickles
them down in the background, and will automatically restart them if IE
quits then is restarted. Netscape opens a new window for each ftp request,
and has no memory of them across restarts, so there is no easy way when
visiting a big ftp site to simply select everything you want then leave
the site.

These are all trivial things to add. The fact that Netscape has not
bothered to add them (but seems to have spent a whole lot of effort on
stuff I have no interest whatever in) would imply to me that they're
hardly a great role model compared to MS. 
The simple fact is that I care about the usability of my browser more than
it's laundry list of features, and IE for Mac is WAY ahead of Communicator
in terms of usability features.
I see no evidence that IE mac 3.01 uses more memory than Communicator,
it's neither faster nor slower, and not obviously more or less robust to
crashes.

Maynard

