setok@FIX.TIN.DOMAIN (Kristoffer Lawson) wrote:

>Aaron Bilger (syserror@micro-net.com) wrote:
>
>: Then fine, you give voluntarily what you want to the causes you want.  I too am
>: willing to give voluntarily to charities of my choice.  As soon as you want to
>: make a coercive organization which takes money at the point of a gun for your
>: causes, though, it's made a mockery of the word 'charity'.
>
>But the logic is false.
>Premiss: We want to offer every person an equal chance for education and
>a wealthy life.
>This can not be done without the government. Charity is not enough.

Your logic is false.  You *assume* that the government institutions
are required and that charity alone will not be enough.  Prove it!  We
have yet to see this in action.

Here, anyway, it is more often the case that the so-called "welfare"
program is actually worsening the amount of poverty in our country. 

We now have 3rd and 4th generation people who have absolutely *no*
work ethic.  I have been introduced to some "mentoring" programs in
Michigan that hope to teach these people what "work ethic" is, and to
teach them that they *should* work to get ahead.  See, your
assumptions about the benefits of welfare don't work in the real world
because they tend to worsen the poverty level.  Since the
implementation of welfare changes in Michigan in which many people
*must* find work or be dropped off of welfare, there is a *much*
higher employment rate, and yet, there are still *many* job openings
that go unfilled.  Go figure!

The government isn't supposed to control us, we are supposed to
control the government... in fact, "We" are the government here,
according to the constitution.  Government is supposed to serve us,
not the other way around.

>So if we want to achieve our goal, it must be done through the government.
>If this is not your goal, then I can't really help that. But which do we
>chose? Do we satisfy the people who want to offer equal opportunities to
>everyone or do we satisfy the ones who don't care for that? Free charity
>does not satisfy the people who want equal opportunities for everyone
>so that means we decide to satisfy the ones who don't want to pay for
>other people's education. 

This is complete bull.  Lack of government welfare does not eliminate
equal opportunities for all.  The school system here pre-dates any
welfare programs in this country.  Basic education is *not* part of
the welfare system.

Now, if you want to talk about higher education, ie., colleges or
universities, then I agree that there is some discrepancy in
availability.  However, it is not completely unaccessible either.
Sure, not everyone can afford Harvard or Yale (of Univ of MI for that
matter), however there are quite a lot of other institutions of higher
learning that work as well.  In my business experiences, rarely does
the institution itself become a factor in hiring decisions (of course,
I'm not in law or medicine).

More government is not the answer.  Many of the charities did much
better before higher government taxes to support welfare.  They also
do it much more efficient.  Our government red tape and beauracracy
eliminates quite a lot of the tax money brought in to support the
welfare system.

But everyone is afraid that someone will slip through the cracks.
Why!?  Why do some people feel the need to guarantee complete equality
in all opportunities?  I didn't come from money, but I've been able to
work hard and build my experiences such that I have enough respect to
get good work.  The most I ever got from the government is some
student loans - on the upside, these helped me get education, however
until I paid them off, the additional payments were very repressive
financially and caused significant family problems.  

Handouts may look good on the face, but they can cause big long-term
problems in the end.  Hard work is the most valuable way, long-term,
to raise one's standard of living.

