Joe Ragosta wrote:
> In article <343A6911.40107357@scic.intel.com>, Allen Kim
> <allenkim@scic.intel.com> wrote:
> > So when AMD finds out that Socket 7 no longer allows them to continue to
> > produce very good alternatives to Intel's microprocessors and decides to
> > go with a new socket/slot which will require new motherboards, will you
> > be complaining?
> 
> Yep.
> 
> I bought a PowerMac 7500/100 last year. It had a 100 MHz 601 chip.
> For $99, I upgraded to a 604.
> For $300, I can upgrade to a 180 MHz 604e.
> For $500, I can get a dual 180 MHz 604e.
> For under $1 K, I can upgrade to G3.
> 
> That's 4 processor generations and I never had to spend money on a new
> motherboard.
> 
> Granted, this isn't the right solution for everyone, but it's nice to have
> the option of upgrading without throwing away your motherboard.

However, in your case you're still limited to the same low
frequency for your main bus.  It would certainly be possible
to use the same Slot 7 interface for a processor 5 years from
now, but the limitations in speed and access are still there.
Just like they are in your 7500.

The daughtercard design that Apple used is one of the main
reasons why the majority of Apple computers out there have
40-50 MHz busses. If you're content with that then you don't
need to go to a new and proprietary connector.
The advantage of the daughtercard design is that you *can*
put the L2 directly on the card.  You could also put other
things on there.  But at some point you have to realize
that you're just substituting the act of replacing those things
on the motherboard with replacing them on the daughtercard.

I'm not saying that it doesn't stink that Intel has made the
interface proprietary.  It does.  What I'm saying is that
sometimes you have to 'bite the bullet' and upgrade to take
advantage of the newest technologies.

Let's say I had a box with a 486 and a bunch of stuff in it.

For $177 you can get a Pentium motherboard & 133MHz Pentium.
 (I would have gone back farther to a 100 or a 90, but I
   couldn't quickly find a price for one)

For $276 you can get a Pentium motherboard & 200MHz Pentium.
 -or-
For $188 you can get a 200MHz Pentium to put in your old
         Pentium motherboard.

For $209 you can get a Pentium 200/MMX to put in your
         Pentium motherboard.
-or-
For $300 you can get a Pentium 200/MMX & a Pentium motherboard.

For $383 you can get a dual Pentium board & a single 200
         Pentium chip. + the old 200MHz Pentium you had,
         or +$188 for another new one.

For $799 you can get a Pentium II Motherboard with a
         266MHz Pentium II.

The costs of doing a similar upgrade seem to be just about
the same for each platform.
601 -> 604         -> 604e                     -> 750
486 -> Pentium/133 -> Pent/200 or Pent/200/MMX -> Pentium II

The main difference between the two seems to be that on the
Intel side you wind up getting newer/faster/more advanced
motherboard designs at the same time.

So the argument seems really to be whether you want to have
to discard a larger piece of PCB or not.

*shrug*

Eric



