In article <343A6911.40107357@scic.intel.com>,
Allen Kim  <allenkim@scic.intel.com> wrote:
>> That's _one_ way to sell lots of new motherboards.
>
>So when AMD finds out that Socket 7 no longer allows them to continue to
>produce very good alternatives to Intel's microprocessors and decides to
>go with a new socket/slot which will require new motherboards, will you
>be complaining?

I suppose you expect the answer "yes" but I can't see the problem.
Everyone claims the reason the NexGen CPU never really made a dent was that
it required a non-standard motherboard.  This surprises me, I always wanted
to buy one but only thing that held ME back was the fact that you could only
get the FPU or the L1 cache on-chip but not both at once.  Having to buy a
special motherboard is nothing new, I've had a whole bunch of Intel-compatible
boards and I've NEVER been able to upgrade the CPU w/o buying a whole new
board anyway.  Either they've changed the CPU voltages even still yet AGAIN,
or the CPU has some kind of trick on-chip cache that the motherboard chipset
keeps flushing, or the BIOS doesn't know how to handle the new CPU (OK flash
has made that less painful, but in the past buying a new motherboard was
generally cheaper than buying a BIOS alone).  Heck, I bought my last
motherboard just because it had a non-Intel chipset and didn't suck.  The
cost of the motherboard pales in comparison to the CPU so why fight it.

I may be a bit hazy on the exact history but I remember when the 386 came
out and all IBM would do was stick it in Microchannel machines everyone
was dumbfounded, since clearly IBM had gone berserk and was desperately
trying to shake off all those pesky customers that had been bothering their
sales guys lately.  So Intel figured "when you want something done right ..."
and came out with their own AT386 motherboard which was just an extended AT
style board, i.e. the same thing but bigger/better/faster.  So the whole
clone industry swerved away from IBM and headed down the path of
righteousness, giving users cheap fast backwards-compatible machines (for
better or worse) and leaving IBM in the dust, to the point that when they
finally reentered the ISA motherboard market they had to call their new stuff
"industry compatible", when it used to be *IBM* compatibility that meant
something.

I think this could happen again.  With Slot 1, Intel is telling its
customers to drop everything and follow Intel down some proprietary route
with a sucky price:performance and no practical benefit whatever to the user.
AMD/Cyrix/etc. are doing a wonderful job of letting many customers put off
falling in this pit for the time being, but soon the Intel proprietary
slot CPUs will be far enough ahead of the Socket 7 clones that customers
will REALLY have to abandon Socket 7.  If AMD/Cyrix/etc. can get together
to come up with their own OPEN high-performance slot/socket standard and
produce fast CPUs for it relatively soon, they could lead a giant chunk of
the market away from Intel permanently (or at least until Intel swallows its
arrogance and jumps back on the bandwagon that's actually going somewhere).

AMD and Cyrix have a history of playing catch-up so they'd normally miss
this chance, but the fact that Intel is already planning to abandon Slot 1
in favor of Slot 2 some day means that AMD/Cyrix have some time to play with
since they can blow off the Slot 1 fad entirely and then take a little more
time while users wonder whether to give up their remaining self-respect and
be led by the nose to Slot 2 when it eventually comes out.  If AMD/Cyrix have
a better, or at least cheaper, alternative available by then they could win
big.  They've gotta do SOMETHING...  And between the two of them they could
easily generate a live mutant motherboard industry, as long as they don't
fragment into more than one non-Intel socket/slot spec at a time.

John Wilson
D Bit



