Aaron Bilger (syserror@micro-net.com) wrote:
: >
: >Well this is probably a discussion that will never end. Ppl with a reasonable
: >amount of money have things OK as they can afford education etc. The fact
: >is that to my knowledge in the states, a lot of families simply can't afford
: >to educate their children -> their children stay poor -> their children stay
: >poor etc. There's no real way out of it. I'm quite willing to pay extra
: >taxes to allow ppl to have the opportunity to educate themselves and gather
: >money themselves. 

: Then fine, you give voluntarily what you want to the causes you want.  I too am
: willing to give voluntarily to charities of my choice.  As soon as you want to
: make a coercive organization which takes money at the point of a gun for your
: causes, though, it's made a mockery of the word 'charity'.

But the logic is false.
Premiss: We want to offer every person an equal chance for education and
a wealthy life.
This can not be done without the government. Charity is not enough.
So if we want to achieve our goal, it must be done through the government.
If this is not your goal, then I can't really help that. But which do we
chose? Do we satisfy the people who want to offer equal opportunities to
everyone or do we satisfy the ones who don't care for that? Free charity
does not satisfy the people who want equal opportunities for everyone
so that means we decide to satisfy the ones who don't want to pay for
other people's education. 

That's really the problem here. You want a system that is free for everyone.
The end result, however, is that these free people choose to form a government.
That's what's wrong with so called anarchist opinions. Careful examination
proves that our system *is* anarchy. We just choose to have a central
group to control aspects of the whole society's needs.


