Wakko Ellington Warner-Warner III wrote:
> 
> Aaron Bilger (syserror@micro-net.com) wrote:
> : On Mon, 06 Oct 1997 01:13:56 GMT, dosixdos@super.zippo.com (Dosidos) wrote:
> : >
> : >Intel lost my respect when they came up with the proprietary Slot 1,
> : >and I will never buy one of their products again unless they decide to
> : >give AMD and Cyrix a chance to compete. That's my choice, and I don't
> : >want the government in this either.
> 
> : making pin compatible CPUs for Socket 8.  It puzzles me why so many people
> : attack Intel so much for their proprietary patented Slot 1 while the same
> : criticism was never given to their propriatary patented Socket 8.
> 
> Probably because the Pro was never advertised; Intel's advertising the
> hell out of the PII, and will probably sell quite a few of these
> no-upgrade-path beasts before they move on to Slot II.
> 
> - A. P. (who will positively wretch if he sees another moron in a pastel
> clean suit..)

	One reason Socket 8 was never attacked as a patented proprietary design
is that Socket 8 was never patented as such.  Only particular pins
within Socket 8 were patented.  AMD or Cyrix (or anybody else who wants
to deal with the stone-faced Intel legal department) could still be sued
right down to the ground for patent infringement for designing a Socket
8 CPU, though.  Perhaps Intel decided to fly under the radar of its most
vociferous enemies by using its patents in this clandestine way (pure
speculation).  At any rate, just another socket, however patented, could
never attract as much attention as a brand new slot did, and pehaps many
people overlooked Socket 8 because they didn't know what to look for and
it wasn't quite as popular in Usenet culture to criticize it.  After
all, Socket 8 didn't house a CPU (PPro) which was quite as poised to
take over the market as the one which sits in Slot 1 (PII); moreover,
Intel's chief critics in this space thought it better to promote the
allegedly superior market positioning of the CPUs they were promoting at
the time (Cyrix M1, AMD 'not ready for prime time' at the time), rather
than to disparage the legitimate business practices of Intel. 
"Legitimate?!" you cringe, "But how?"  As I reckon, the CPU marketplace
is one of the most quickly moving in the world.  For example, if
Scottowels stopped researching and implementing breakthrough designs in
toilet paper manufacture, then it would immediately be overtaken by
Kimberly-Clark in about 100,000 years.  If Intel did the same, their
competitors would make short work of them (loving every minute of it). 
So if Scottowels suddenly showed up on the scene with a new
"proprietary" cardboard core that couldn't fit on standard bathroom TP
installations, they would lose lots of business (or everybody would buy
in to their new "breakthrough" design).  Since Intel makes a product in
which differentation and design progress are of dire importance in
profit margins and performance (unlike TP) it is in their best interest,
and ours, that they continue to roll out new designs.  If those designs
are too expensive and take too sharp a break from what you consider the
norm, and if you chalk all that up to Intel's FBI/space alien warlords
who want to ruin your (yes your--John Doe's) life, then don't buy their
products.  If you cling to the notion that each new iteration of Intel
archetecture change brings on untold performance increases, even though
claims of such are largely amorphous, then buy their stuff (and get a
subscription to a Ziff-Davis magazine).  But don't rip Intel from here
to next Tuesday concerning their marketing and technological advances
just because your new K6 gives up 3.5 SpecMarks to the latest Intel
hardware.
	Sorry this post was so long, but I just had a really bad chili-cheese
burrito.  Of course, these are only my opinions and do not reflect those
of anybody else, fictional or otherwise.  Replies via email or public
posting welcomed.  My real email address is okrell_at_bayou_dot_uh_edu.



