  A VAXserver can be a member of a VMScluster, and can be used for
  downline loads, server operations, and as a batch engine, etc.

In article <MPG.e9f21725bc4ecbf989692@news.pnm.net>, christian.bauer@passau.netsurf.de (Christian J. Bauer) writes:
:In article <3434BE29.41C6@hep.phy.cam.ac.uk>, hill@hep.phy.cam.ac.uk 
:says...
: 
:> We used to have a VAXserver 4000/300 as the boot node for a VMScluster.
:> Among the satellites was a MicroVAX II, and it worked fine. The
:> VAXservers were (are??) effectively single-user MicroVAXes intended to
:> act as file and boot servers rather than for interactive sessions.
:
:Hmmmm, "single-user". My license pack states "User/Operating System 0 
:Users" but the number of units says "1443". 

  A VAXserver is technically licensed as a zero-user system.

:Does "VAXServer" mean that I can only interactively connect to the System 
:via OPA0: , and is there ANY other Login (via DECNet/LAT/DSH32) e.g. "SET 
:HOST" prohibitet? 

  Zero user logins are permitted.  Certain OpenVMS versions and
  certain situations allow one login for system management purposes.

:Have I use OPA0: even when invoking "SYSGEN" to change pagefile sizes?

  OPA0: is a "back door", and allows basic system operations needed
  to get an OpenVMS system up and running.  But at the risk of
  berating the point, per the OpenVMS Software Product Description
  (SPD), the VAXserver is a zero-user system.

