I would not include any machines which depend so much on 'advanced' 
techniques ( i am not being sarcastic abotu advanced, just that there isnt 
a commonly accepted term for these kind of techniques to wring the last drop
of performance out of a piece of silicon. They are VERY advanced.) in the
implementation. The complicated pipelining and out-of-ordring if executions
and speculative predictions and so on and so forth, all of that contributes
to complexity, even though it is for the most part hidden from the ISA level
of things. To me, that much complexity impairs a machine's 'cleanness'
For example, the early MIPS chips are very clean, but even with the same ISA
when we get to the 3rd generation MIPS and beyond, they get really gross 
and even start to violate the original RISC principles to get a little
extra performance. So, i wouldnt consider an R8000 to be clean at all, 
tis an ugly mess. But the R2000-R3000 are very clean machines. 

Alpha is so complex in its implementation, that it doesent really meet
my impression of clean. Generally a small amount of silicon is part of
my definition.  

The  reason the Z80000 got on my list was because for a CISC design, it 
has a remarkably general-purpose-ness to it which makes a hell of 
a difference in programming technique. 
The Z80 is on there for obvious reasons- its a beautiful deisgn.
