:>@"Hey, %s! You're a NAZI, and you can't spell!"

    I'm not the CTO (there is no CTO), but I am heading up R&D and am 
    one of the four original founders.

    We are using FreeBSD on just about all the machines.  They are extremely
    reliable systems.

    We have tried twice now to use NT for *something* ... the latest
    attempt being to run FrontPage on the thing.  Unfortunately, there
    have been enough serious problems that we've had to trash the attempt.
    There is virtually no security, and we need to be able to configure
    thousands of frontpage accounts.  The frontpage software and NT admin 
    tools were designed to handle a few dozen or a few hundred accounts
    at best.  It's virtually impossible to manage large datasets, disk space 
    utilization, quotas, security, resource partitioning, resource utilization,
    or deal with other issues such as DOS attacks.  NT is too much of a black
    box. ( The FreeBSD boxes, on the other hand, are trivial to manage in
    all respects and can take just about anything thrown at them, even better
    then SGI and Sun boxes ).  The NT machines also became rather unstable 
    over time.   Short of rebooting them once a day, we couldn't get reliable 
    operation.

    At the moment, NT makes a good desktop box and a good office-server for
    MS utilities and applications, but I do not trust it enough to sit it on
    the internet or handle large user loads and I would dread having to
    manage 20 or 30 NT boxes, let alone a few hundred.  One person can manage
    all 25 of our FreeBSD boxes.

    Microsoft is getting closer, but they still have a long, long way to go
    with NT.  NT is about where UNIX was 10 years ago in terms of reliability.
    I hear lots of people sing it's praises, but when I ask them what
    they are running on their NT boxes it turns out to be about 1/10 of what 
    we run on our FreeBSD boxes.  Any OS will work if you only run a handful
    of apps on it, and we are not particularly interested in wasting money
    on 10 platforms to run applications that ought to fit on one platform.

    We are still keeping our fingers in.  Eventually we figure we will be
    forced to use NT for something.  All I can say is:  Thank god most internet
    protocols were standards before MS got into the game!

    Who knows, maybe someone will come up with an NT emulator for UNIX :-)

					-Matt




