Your average home-user knows about as much about computers as he does
about internal combustion engines.  Ask the average user about each of
the above OS's, and how many of them will they have heard of?
Windows 3.x (including Win95 and NT) and MacOS, and possibly OS/2.
It is as much about advertising and availability as it is about
choice.  All the choices in the world are useless if you are only
aware of one or two of them.

Just because the average home user can't be bothered to research
their choices (and hence pick whoever advertises the most, i.e. M$)
doesn't mean I should be penalized (by other OS's dying out for lack of
interest and support).

Fortunately, some OS's (like Linux and FreeBSD) can't ever really 
truly die, since they are not proprietary, and anybody can pick it up
and support it, but how many average computer users want to learn
UNIX?  That's why they use windows in the first place (be in M$ Windows 
or Machintrashes) - to avoid those gory details that UNIX requires the 
user to know (i.e., windows is simpler).

> I'm saying alternatives exist and you can choose them.  If you choose 
> to go with Microsoft despite there being other alternatives, fine.  
> Just don't go whining that you had no alternatives and that Microsoft 
> has a monopoly then.

I do use alternatives, but the problem is:
The average user doesn't know enough to know or care about having
alternatives.  This is an ideal situation for a producer, and M$ is
exploiting it quite well :P

I try to introduce as many people as possible to Linux, but in the 
end, it doesn't run the latest game or their favorite word processor
or whatever application they want to run, so they can't be bothered 
with it.
Other OS's have the same problem.  
i.e., it isn't just M$, but all the companies that sell out to M$ and
write for their OS exclusively or almost exclusively (or wait several 
years before porting to any other OS - usually MacOS).

It is a circle - the software companies won't write for other OS's
because there isn't a large market for them, but nobody wants to switch
to another OS unless there is a large base of supported applications
available for it.  Catch-22 :P

Obviously, certain exceptions (like networking stuff for UNIX) do exist,
but go to your local computer store and look at the shelves - 95% of the
stuff at the stores in my area are for PC's (of which ~100% is for 
M$ Windows or DOS), 5% for Mac and ~0% for other OS's (like, one copy of 
RedHat's Complete Linux on CD and that's it...).  
Not counting stuff for console systems like Nintendo or Sega.

> >People use M$ not because it is the best, but because it is the
> >only [in their price range].
> 
> Last I checked OS/2 was <$200, MacOS <$100, Linux and FreeBSD at $0.  
