Digital UNIX is $1K, VMS $2K, which is what I had in mind ;)
But then, what average user wants to learn these OS's anyway (free,
like Linux, or not)?


> >We don't - we want the government to insure fair competition in
> >a free market :P
> 
> There is competition.  If you choose to buy from Bill despite 
> competition, you can't avoid blaming yourself for what results come 
> from that choice.  It's not Microsoft's choice, not the government's 
> choice, but *your* choice if you download MSIE 4.0 and goo and gah 
> over it rather than choosing Netscape, for example.

I avoid M$ like the plague, but when the majority of people don't, 
and the result of *their* ignorance is that I lose *my* ability to
choose, I get annoyed...

> >How about a situation like this: I really like WordPerfect, but my
> >company goes out and buys Word (as a part of Office, perhaps).
> >I don't have enough clout in my company to dissaude them from this,
> >so M$ gets a lot of business from my company, I am stuck using their
> >products, and I had absolutely no choice in the matter :P
> 
> You can still choose to run WordPerfect on your own, try to influence 
> others to choose it rather than word.  Again it sounds like you are 
> trying to shift the blame, this time from your company's decision to 
> Microsoft or the government.  Your company is responsible for its 
> choices, the rest of the world is not your company's keeper.

I can't run WordPerfect on my machine at work, cause the tech support
people will take it off (and get angry with me for putting it on in the
first place).
So let's say I use it at home.  And I convince my co-workers to use it
at home.  Guess what?  We still don't make the decision as to what 
software to purchase at work.  If we request the change, we'll just be 
told that we already have a word processor (and the hundreds of site 
licenses for everyone to use it) and we can't afford to switch (i.e. buy 
a couple hundred site licenses for a different package which would just 
be redundant anyway since we have Word already).

The corporate accounts are still a LOT more $$ than the home users,
and the home users are very likely to use what they use at work, both
because of familiarity and also the ability to work from home (whether 
it be telecommuting, you have been duped into working hundreds of hours
of unpaid overtime (Dilbert!), or you are a workaholic ;)

Most companies choose M$.  That only helps M$ maintain its dominance.  
And what company is going to switch?  If my company suddenly switch to 
Linux or FreeBSD, we'd no longer be able to share files with out 
clients, who would not switch (because we are not responsible for the 
other company's choices), and we'd go out of business.

So, my company is locked into using M$ because our clients do (we have 
another Catch-22 developing here...) which means that I have to use M$ 
even though I don't want to (at least at work, I use Linux at home, but 
I never take work home with me ;)



