>    This is a purist's view of the world:  "The better software will win".

Depends how you define 'better'.  I don't necessarily expect the software I
consider technically superior to always be the most popular, but neither do I
think others should be forced to provide or to use the software I favor.  

>    It just doesn't happen that way.  The MOB mentality tends to rule the
>    computer world as much as it rules the real world.  This is why marketing
>    and hype have as great an effect as they do.  This is why companies like
>    AOL are able to dupe so many people into using their services.  This is

I think putting their diskettes in every magazine, mailer, etc. and stopping
just short of giving them away on bubble gum or condom packages had something to
do with their widespread usage, though I don't see how this qualifies their
actions as 'duping'.

>    why something as simple as a voice-over-internet, which I could write 
>    in a good day,

Inluding a decent codec?  I don't consider simple bidirectional pipe of raw
audio to be a feat either, but the waveform and compression folks can still
impress me with fitting reasonable quality through a narrow pipe.

>    you normally don't hear the horror stories.  The blame game is easy:
>    "Oh, you didn't upgrade".  Nevermind that "upgrading" often requires a
>    costly complete replacement, bigger boxes, and retraining, and is not

Upgrading is overhyped in the computer world in general.  People pay hundreds to
get an extra %10 Mhz increase in a CPU that will result in maybe 2% real world
speed increase, people rush out and buy x.0 software when it comes out, even
though almost no x.0 products from *any* company lack major bugs, and so on.
People should learn that 'latest and greatest' isn't really greatest if it
doesn't work right or costs a lot for features that offer no gain.  This
mentality isn't something you can pin on Microsoft, however, unless you want to
again confirm your statement "the blame game is easy."

>    always backwards compatible.  Remember the 'demo' word release that
>    couldn't edit older word documents without rewriting them into an
>    incompatible 'new' format, and then had no way to convert back to the
>    'old' format?  How often do we hear Windows 95 reinstallation
>    horror stories?  Does that change your mind?

I've had enough experience with various operating systems to have DOS, Win95,
OS/2, NT, and FreeBSD horror stories (the only OS I've used for substantial time
without horrible incident is Linux).  Just last week I tried removing a FAT
partition with DOS fdisk to find that it instead removed my main NTFS partition
where my bootable NT resides, and had to recontruct my partition table by hand
to resurrect it.  OS/2 I loved for months, til one day it decided to no longer
be able to read FAT (could still read HPFS, though, which was really
surprising), FreeBSD was the worst shock, one day after exitting X, the system
crashed and was never able to boot even in single user mode again.  Anyway,
Microsoft has its share of buggy software, it is true, but it certainly doesn't
have a monopoly on bugs.

>  Most people just grin and
>    bear it, yet just about anyone who has tried to install a modern
>    game under windows 95 has also had to spend hours uninstalling and 
>    reinstalling drivers 'in the right order' to make the game function.

And under other OSes on PCs with their cards that can have conflicting
interrupts, not-really-plug-and-play architecture, etc. you aren't likely to do
any better.

>    If you install DirectX the wrong way, you can kiss goodbye to your
>    windows system and might as well plop in the CD, reformat the disk, and
>    start from scratch.

I've seen such drastic results happen putting TCP/IP X server on OS/2.  I don't
have corresponding horror stories for any UNIX platform in this case, but again
bugs are Microsoft's sole domain.

>  How about when you install random third party software
>    that 'requires' microsoft explorer and will happily destroy your netscape
