> That strange flapping sound you're hearing is the disappearance of choice
> in high-speed microprocessors. HP's PA-RISC will die as soon as Merced
> appears; Alpha (assuming Intel doesn't suddenly develop an unprecedented
> degree of hacker-friendliness) will die at the same time. Maybe MIPS and
> Sparc will survive, but neither are as fast (at present) as Alpha or
> PA-RISC.
> 
> The RISC experiment is over. Scores to follow.

  Yes, RISC won.

  The 68K, NS32000, Clipper (like the Transputer, it tried to pass as a
  RISC but the charade didn't last), and the mighty VAX all lie moldering
  in their grave thanks to RISC.  Of course RISC took a few casualties of
  its own (88K and 29K).

  The most resilient, profitable, ubiquitous, and ugliest of the CISCs, x86
  will be the last to go of course but it is inevitable.  Intel wouldn't be
  risking its franchise to print money by developing and marketing IA-64 if
  the end of the road for x86 wasn't clearly in sight.  It doesn't matter if
  it is Merced, Alpha, or whatever, all roads lead to RISC (although at some
  point a RISC shakeout is also inevitable, both on the desktop and in embedded
  control as there is likely too many marginal players; HP already has got
