In article <EHoJnw.Ds@utcc.utoronto.ca>,
Thomas May <thomas.may@utoronto.ca> wrote:
>Who makes the world's fastest CPU chips, and just how fast are they? Find
>out at http://compreviews.tqn.com/mbody.htm

This article is written from a desktop computing point of view.
Its conclusion that Alphas are faster than current Intel offerings
is hardly earthshaking.

For those of us who need to run large scientific applications, it is
always amusing to see Intel ads touting Pentium chips when in fact,
Intel makes the *slowest* chips of the major producers. A benchmark
that correlates very roughly with large compute bound problems is
the Specfp95 number. PentiumPro/II tops out in the most recent numbers
around 8 or 9. These numbers were posted recently:


      fp95
      BASE  fp95  Chip             Clock        Date
--------------------------------------------------------
DEC   19.9  21.3 Alpha A21164     ??/600        Jun97
HP    20.1  23.0 PA8200           200           Jul97
IBM   15.4  17.6 Power2S          135           Oct96
SGI   17.4  19.0 R10000           195           Nov96
SUN         18.3 UltraSP          100/300       Jul97
--------------------------------------------------------
Intel  6.4   6.9 PentiumII         66/266       May97
--------------------------------------------------------

Of course, these systems are not all in the same price range.
The point of the article is that Alpha based systems are
cheap enough to be an alternative for desktop systems.




