   The 21264 will premier likely >= 500 MHz at >30 SPECint95 and >50 SPECfp95 and
   go very quickly to 600 MHz (~40 SPECint95, and ~60 SPECfp95).  Then next year
   will see it shrink to a 0.28 MHz so the clock rate will jump once again.  The
   hottest non-Alpha I have seen is the brand spanking new HP PA-8200 at 236 MHz
   which gives 17.3 SPECInt95 and 25.4 SPECfp95.  The 21264 is significantly faster
   on both integer and floating point code and the main memory bandwidth is scaled
   up by a factor of roughly 5x over the 21164. 

   The PA-8500 is potentially interesting as a worthy competitor but the 21264
   probably won't be seriously challenged until Merced ships in 1999 (maybe) and
   the 21264 might even see a second shrink by then.

