On Sat,  4 Oct 1997 22:09:07 -0400, Roger Severino <roger2+@andrew.cmu.edu>
wrote:

>> Unless you count the 'minor' facts that it does not recognize peoples'
>>rights to their own person and property and dictates that anyone can be
>>sacrificed to the 'good of the many' in the name of the 'People' or the
>>'Proletariat' or 'Society' or whatever other nameless faceless
>collective >they wish to invoke.
>
>If you can please elaborate; Where does the "right" to property come
>from?  On what philosophical and ethical schema?  If you look at
>history, enlightenment thinkers (particularyly Locke) examined the
>misfortunes of Feudalism and invoked "Natural Law" to establish a
>person's "right" to property merely through his exercise of possession
>and transformation of things in nature.  However, he goes on to say that
>destruction (a form of transformation) does not count and should not be
>allowed since it takes away from "others fair share." This foundation is

Original claim to property is from transformation of raw materials, a
'homesteading' like situation.  Beyond that point, the property may then be
traded, given, bought, sold, etc.  Destruction of one's own property is valid,
destruction of others' is not.  I was not familiar with that aspect of Locke's
philosophical viewpoint.  Since you do read some political philosophy, the
political writer offering a description closest to justification I've seen is
Rothbard.

>that American Capitalism is built upon.  The notion of Property was no
>longer impossessible thing that was overseen by the king's divine right.
> It became everyone's divine right and all they had to do to claim it,
>was to go out and snatch it from nature.

'From nature' as in taming untamed wilderness, yes, but not from other people.

>  Let's look at the result shall
>we? Let's see, we massacred the American Indians, Mexicans, Spanish,
>British, Colombians, Canadians, Cubans, Filipinos, Libyans, and everyone

Careful with your collective pronouns.  'We' never massacred anyone.  I have not
killed any American Indians, Mexicans, Spaniards, etc., and assume you have not
either.  I agree there are a large number of people that have looted and killed,
both in history and in present day, and they were or are criminal.  However,
claiming 'we' is invalid, unless you wish the atrocity of "suffer[ing] the sins
of the fathers upon the sons".

>else that was in the way of America's "Manifest Destiny".  Look in the
>original Constitution, it accepted the fact that black people could be
>owned because they weren't truly people.  Heck, if you breed them, feed
>them, raise them, and teach them how to work, then you must own them,
>just like a dog right?  What happened to destruction not counting
>towards property?  

When did I ever promote the constitution as being a document that fully
recognized peoples' rights to life and property?  With the exception of
particular extreme portions such as the 5/9s rule, it does provide an outline a
government with fewer systematic violations of human rights than most
governments -- however, this is still a far cry from perfection.  (one quote
I've seen and admire concerning the current state of affairs: "the constitution
isn't a perfect document, but it's better than what we have now")

>The right to property is built on shaky ground.  Just because someone
>possesses something does not in anyway mean that he/she *should*. 
>Humans cannot truly create anything (e=mc^2), but merely transform, so
>how can someone truly claim something as being theirs?  Is it still
>theirs when they die? If you look through history, you will see that
>concept of MINE was invented. In Communist countries, it became OURS. 
>There was a time when it just used to be GOD'S.

People have a right to pass on what they own to whom they wish.  Unless or until
collective thought is truly possible (I highly doubt this possibility, but
recognize it may someday be a feat of technology), the concept of group thought
and action and ownership is a farce; it will always be one or a few controlling
and deciding what will be done with the property and claim it in the name of the
many.  

>So please, explain this idea of the "right" to property to me.  Does a
>person have a "right" to buy all the greatest works of art in the world
>and burn them because he owns them?

Absolutely.  I would hope people would not sell the works to him for that
purpose, and even make sales contractual upon not destroying the art.  However,
if they flat out sold it with no contractual stipulations on its use, the art
could be used as kindling or bird cage liner.

>Since Neil Armstrong was the first
>to land on the Moon, does he own it because he put a little flag on it?

Ownership comes from the actual transformation and usage of the raw materials,
so could be claimed only for where that applied.  Neil can only validly claim
the little plot of land where instruments were set up, soil samples taken,
experiments done, and claim the moon rocks he brought back.  

>Since Brazil owns the rain forest, can they slash and burn it to the
>ground?  

'Brazil' owns nothing, it's another abstract nameless faceless collective.  Do
you wish to speak of individual owners of plots of land in South America?

>Who owns the oceans,

Currenly, no one, but this is only from people being unwilling or unable to
apply the same property rules to the water.  It is valid to claim stocking and
harvesting fish, building underwater drilling, etc. as transformation and
claiming areas of the water.  People have the right to set up nets around the
area they stock and fish, for example.  

>who owns the air,

You can claim the area immediately around you that you breath.  If you do some
other real transformation and production with it (e.g. making liquid nitrogen
from it, using air cooled ventilation systems), you can claim that (at least as
long as you keep and control it).  The nature of air is such that it will
generally leave your property and control on its own though, so these instances
aren't very relevant.  In an environment where air was scarce and real work
required to produce it (e.g. electrolysis to obtain oxygen and rebreathing
apparatus to recycle as much as possible on a colony on the moon or another
planet), ownership would be much more an issue.

>who owns the Sun? 

Whoever wants to go there and claim a piece can have their own little part.
Best of luck to them.

>If
>Bill Gates finds a way to block out the Sun and charge money for it he
>would, but does he have the "right" too?

While your scenario made for an entertaining "Simpson's" episode, it has far
less relevace than owning fluids or even portions of stellar objects.  If he can
do something like build a Dyson sphere between the orbit of Venus and Earth, I
can argue he has the right to do so, you can argue you have the right to try to
stop him, but in truth it's as meaningful as debating whether he has the right
to grow wings and fly.  If some amazing technological breakthrough would make it
possible for your Mr. Burns (or Mr. Gates) to try such a thing, we can worry
about it.

>> From each according to his ability, to each according to his ability-
>>  
>> Aaron
>
>Hmm...The above logic would justify a mother giving more food and
>housing to the harder working son right?  Better yet, that would justify
>the starvation of the retarded, crippled, elderly, and children would it
>not?  They can't and don't contribute to society, so let them fend for
>themselves!  

The quote is obvious satire of the Communist's ludicrous saying "From each
according to his ability, to each according to his need."  The Communist scheme,
in addition to ignoring peoples' rights as rational creatures, rewards need and
punishes ability.  "From each according to his ability, to each according to his
ability" does mean to recognize peoples' rights to themselves and to work to
support themselves.  It does not preclude someone voluntarily giving food,
housing, etc. to others.

>According too US Census Statistics, the highest 20% of the population
>receive 50% of the income, the top 5% receive 20% of the income,  and it
>is vastly more unequal around the world and the gap has been widening. 
>Is it because these people deserve it?  I would propose that a small
>part of it is effort, or as you might say "ability" while the majority
>is due to the unfair advantage of inheretances and oftentimes just luck.

<figures>

And how laissez faire do you think the current system is?  Among the
interferences of government into peoples' lives that help propagate the
discepancies you give:

Taxation.  Taxes are pure, simple, unjustifiable theft in any case.  It relates
even more to your figures in the fact that since the poorer segments of
population require a greater proportion of their income to subsist, taxation,
even with severe gradation, means taking an amount relatively larger to the
amount they need to get by, thus eliminating a greater proportion of what they
could save, pushing some below the amount they need to subsist, and hence into:

The welfare state.  Another system operating on theft and redistribution of the
loot, a system which propagates and promote poverty by rewarding people for
staying below a certain income.  If making gross below $15,000 a year means
getting several hundred dollars a month in handouts, but making gross just above
$15,000 a year means no handouts and getting taxed at 25% total on the earnings,
why choose to try to better yourself if near that division point?  And if having
more children to support means more handouts and less money taken in taxes, what
kind of self-advancement does this promote?  Trillions have been spent on the
'war on poverty' in the past 30 years, and the result is greater poverty.
Other, often even more incidious, recipients of welfare also make a big
contribution to the discrepancies you see.  They are involved in:

Corporate welfare.  The government taking hundreds of millions of dollars it
stole from citizens and handing it over to 'poor' recipients such as Chrysler or
Lockheed/Martin.  Laissez-faire does not mean bailing out failed multi-million
dollar corporations, especially not with pilfered funds.  'Ability' is not a
word that can be applied to someone who got or kept their wealth by such
handouts (or more subtle interferences in the market such as subsidies,
protectionist tariffs, etc.).  And while we're on the topic, why not:

Corporate status.  I've stated before that property isn't owned by nameless
faceless ambiguous collectives, but by individuals.  Insofar as corporations
operate as large, complex partnerships where people own set numbers of shares of
certain value and ownership percentages can be traced back to individuals, they
avoid being such a nebulous collective.  However, the current view of a separate
corporate identity owning things on its own goes beyond that.  More importantly,
corporations currently have limited liability.  While this no doubt holds appeal
for businessmen, it is unjustifiable that individuals suddenly can absolve
themselves of responsibility when joining into a 'corporation'.  The individuals
who make corporate decisions should be liable for their decisions, financially
liable for the debts of the corporations, and liable even criminally if they
decide to take criminal action.  Business executives without 'ability' should
have to be concerned with really paying off debts of their company rather than
writing it off as "the corporation went bankrupt, but we aren't liable."  The
decision makers for corporations which choose paths such as dumping waste on
others' property should be punished as surely as if they had personally violated
the property and dumped waste upon it.  

If these and other abuses of the current US system were fixed, would it suddenly
mean no one smart and able would ever end up less than a millionaire or that no
dolts could ever live in luxury? -- no, certainly not, no system can or will
achieve completely that goal.  Laissez-faire capitalism means a system without
systematic violations of peoples' person and property. It's the background
necessary for rational minds to pursue success and happiness, not the guarantee
they will be achieved for all -- no system can provide that.  However, as above,
laissez-faire would have the added advantage of not embodying many of the rights
violating mechanisms of the current system which also contribute to the
discrepancies with which you are concerned.

>If we examine numbers around the world, where there is much more
>starvation and poverty, we note that over 80% of all the worlds wealth
>is owned by 1% of the population.

I'm less concerned with your world figures than the US ones.  Other parts of the
world may still have shieks or kings or sultans or other autocrats, blatantly
corrupt or criminal political systems, not even giving lip service to individual
rights or free trade.  The US is still a long way from laissez-faire, but it's
closer than say, an absolute monarchy.  



Aaron




