On Sat, 04 Oct 1997 21:02:34 GMT, dosixdos@super.zippo.com (Dosidos) wrote:

>On Sat, 04 Oct 1997 03:27:15 -0400, syserror@micro-net.com (Aaron
>Bilger) wrote:
>
><love it or leave it type comment towards me>
>
>What I was really thinking was people refusing to pay their taxes, and
>we have seen many of those in the last 30 years or so. It's one thing
>to speak out, protest etc.. it's everyone's right, but when they stop
>paying taxes it's time they get on that boat (or in jail.)

Taxation is an arbitrarily applied, assigned an arbitrary numerical amount by a
government with no accountability, no restraint on being able to increase how
much it takes on a whim.  The most common argument I've seen from people that
take any kind of 'love it or leave it' approach when I speak out against
taxation as theft is that we have some kind of implied social contract with the
government and are obligated to pay it for its services.  However, the problem
is we _don't_ have such a contract.  If things were to go in that direction, to
where we have explicit contracts, prices and services negotiated, accountability
for action, that is justifiable, that is free individuals interacting.  I would
be happy to be able to sign up to pay a market amount for police and courts and
have contracts on prices and amount and type of services possible, to forego any
contracts with welfare organizations, funding art, subsidies to farmers or auto
manufacturers or anyone else, etc.  The current system isn't like that, now we
have simply a body of a few people able to decide to take what they want for
whatever reason.  Explicit contracts and user fees for certain services from a
large body like a government would be rational dealings of people in a free
society.  The current scheme is merely on par with Mafia extortion.

>>Unfortunately what was the freeest country in the world recently got taken over
>>by the world's largest tyranny.  Currently there are only one or two left with
>>less oppression overall than the United States.
>
>When this country was the freeest country in the world many of its

I don't know if this country was ever the freeest in the world, since I'm not
familiar with the status of all the rest of the world's nations throughout the
past two centuries.  Now the main contenders I know of are Switzerland and New
Zealand.  I think you continue down completely the wrong path for the rest of
your paragraph, evidently assuming I meant the US was the freeest nation.  I
actually was referring to Hong Kong, in the past decade about the closest to
laissez-faire existing in the world, which recently got absorbed by China.

>citizens had no rights at all, workers lived in shacks and were
>busting rocks 16 hours a day. Only a few could afford an education and
>the wealth belonged to just a handful of white men. 

Since I was not claiming a miraculously free U.S. in the past either, these
aren't really relevant.  The U.S. has had its share of atrocities (slavery,
secondary status for women, etc.) which have been addressed while other more
general infringements on liberty (income tax, banning certain chemicals from
consumption, firearm bans and limitations, etc.) have been added, so it's tough
to say when the U.S. would have been freeest.  It's not really a concern since I
don't have a really rosy nostalgic view of the U.S. in the past either.

>Then the unions
>came upon, and some called them "reds"... should I go on?

If unions are organizations formed by consenting workers to collectively bargain
with management, they are perfectly compatible with laissez-faire capitalism,
and I see how forming unions could be a reasonable choice in many industries.
However, when unions and unions interactions _aren't_ by choice, it's quite
another matter.  Some teamsters who use intimidation and violence to achieve
their goals are obviously criminals, as were the 'strike-busters' who used
violence to end non-violent strikes early in this century.  The government
joined such criminal activity early in the century by calling out national guard
troops to break strikes or to forces employees back to work -- and continues
this kind of abuse in more subtle ways in the form of the 'Railroad Labor Act',
the act invoked to force American pilots back to work this spring.  Also, forced
membership in unions, where they make new employees join and pay dues, or
forcing employers to have formal negotiations with a majority union rather than
excercising their right to fire all employees, a stipulation required by the
'Norris-Laguardia Act', are also unjustifiable infringements on the rights of
either employees or employers.  Unionization can certain be rational in a free
society, but unfortunately the past current U.S. laws towards it often violates
the rights of citizens on both sides of the bargaining wall.

>>By the way, are
>>you saying that projects I don't use shouldn't get tax money from me?  That
>>would be a start, to pay only for roads, police and courts, and to not have
>>money filtered to pay for a war on drugs, increasing poverty by rewarding poor
>>people for having more children, etc.
>
>I don't have a problem with that either, I think that there are enough
>people to choose going the other way and at the end there would be
>enough money for all these projects. 
>But what would you say if the result was that there wasn't enough
>money to operate the courts or the prisons?

The results would be dire indeed, but if people chose not to support these very
basic institutions (which are about the only parts of government actually geared
at _protecting_ individual rights), it means there's a much deeper problem in
peoples' attitudes that no government could ever solve.  Assuming that most
people do recognize the importance of protecting individuals from murder,
assault, theft, etc., which I consider a safe assumption, it should be easy to
fund and run a rational court and police system.  Of course, this would also be
aided by trimming down the abusive and/or wasteful parts of these systems, such
as eliminating laws against consensual crimes such as drug use, firearms
ownership, gambling, and consenting sex between adults, discouraging harassment
suits by means such as charging the plaintiff for court costs in a failed civil
suit, trimming down number of appeals for case review, appeals for habeus corpus
concerns and other tactics which merely clog the system, etc.

>Or maybe this year's
>popular theme was "Save the yellow frogs" and we end up with a $300
>billion budget for the APA, but had to cut on Police forces because
>some cop decided to beat the crap out of some little old lady and the
>video tape was all over TV and newspapers?

The rational way of dealing with police abuses is not to decide to contribute
nothing, but to punish the actual abusers.  I see how in the current system your
outcome could be the result, because police unfortunately aren't considered very
liable.  When no one really expects law enforcement officers to be punished, I
see how they could see cutting it all to be the only way to address the issue.
This is the result of an injustice which is certainly a top priority to correct.
When a police officer does something like beat an unarmed civilian, they
shouldn't just get a stern lecture and suspension, but full liability for
assault and battery.  When an FBI sniper shoots an unarmed woman holding her
infant in her arms, they don't deserve a medal for their performance, but do
deserve the chair for murder.  Since law enforcement is a very important
position with a lot of power to abuse, the abuses possible only to law
enforcement should also be treated with utmost severity.  If a law enforcement
officer is found to have attempted to frame someone for a crime, that officer
should be liable not only for fraud in the framing, but for the very crime for
which they were attempting to frame the individual.  If police search and sieze
property for evidence and the defendant turns out to be innocent, the police
should be liable for any property they took, any other damage they caused, etc.
With these kinds of improvements to the justice system, which are desperately
needed even now, a different outcome from police abuses would result than what
you would predict from the existing system.

>Do we rename Cops to Yellow Frogs and save them anyway? :)

*grin*



Aaron




