    This is a purist's view of the world:  "The better software will win".
    It just doesn't happen that way.  The MOB mentality tends to rule the
    computer world as much as it rules the real world.  This is why marketing
    and hype have as great an effect as they do.  This is why companies like
    AOL are able to dupe so many people into using their services.  This is
    why something as simple as a voice-over-internet, which I could write 
    in a good day, winds up splashing the frontpage and causes telcos to
    start suing people.  I am not so foolish as to believe that my choices are
    effecting microsoft's bottom line.

    What scares me the most is not microsoft's ability to hype and market their
    software to make it sound better then everyone else's, it's the fact that
    people in positions of power actually believe them!  People bet whole
    companies on microsoft software and a lot of them get fried for it, but
    you normally don't hear the horror stories.  The blame game is easy:
    "Oh, you didn't upgrade".  Nevermind that "upgrading" often requires a
    costly complete replacement, bigger boxes, and retraining, and is not
    always backwards compatible.  Remember the 'demo' word release that
    couldn't edit older word documents without rewriting them into an
    incompatible 'new' format, and then had no way to convert back to the
    'old' format?  How often do we hear Windows 95 reinstallation
    horror stories?  Does that change your mind?  Most people just grin and
    bear it, yet just about anyone who has tried to install a modern
    game under windows 95 has also had to spend hours uninstalling and 
    reinstalling drivers 'in the right order' to make the game function.
    If you install DirectX the wrong way, you can kiss goodbye to your
    windows system and might as well plop in the CD, reformat the disk, and
    start from scratch.  How about when you install random third party software
    that 'requires' microsoft explorer and will happily destroy your netscape
    browser?  Or some of those nice shiny ICONs on the screen that microsoft
    does not allow you to throw into the trash because they want you to
    accidently hit them one day.  Or how about the microsoft network
    installation?   Oh boy, you can't quit out of THAT until you've entered
    a credit card number and the thing has actually dialed MSN, and try
    to reboot your machine in the middle of it and you are S C R E W E D.

    This is the bullshit that microsoft users have to deal with, and they
    do it happily because they believe it is the proper order of things.
    This is the MOB mentality.

    Other companies are starting to catch on as well. I just saw a full
    page magazine add from GTE, "the people who brought you the internet".
    Excuse me?

						-Matt




