Aaron Bilger <syserror@micro-net.com> wrote:
>Giving away a product is someone's right to do with their property.  No one has
>an obligation to charge a certain price for their product, just as no one has an
>obligation to buy at a given asking price (even if that price is 0 :-) ).
>
>Companies cannot and should not worry about whether they will 'hurt' another
>company by having customers switch loyalties; it's not a rational or even
>feasible way to run a business.  In the end it's the customers' choice.  No one
>has an obligation to use anything from Microsoft, or from Netscape, so the idea
>that customers switching which is their choice means somehow one company is
>being 'unfair' does not hold water.

Are you interested in defending MicroSoft in particular or companies which
undercut other companies in general?  Do you own MicroSoft stock?  What do
you do for a living?
 
If it were not for the selective enforcement of the antitrust laws of this
country, then MicroSoft itself would soon fall prey to undercutting.
There are organizations which are larger than MicroSoft with much deeper
pockets than even MicroSoft, organizations which could sustain losses for a
longer time than MicroSoft for the sole purpose of putting MicroSoft out of
business.  If the leaders of Japan, Inc. were to decide that MicroSoft
were a long-term threat to their computer software industry, which it is,
then they could decide to wage an all-out price war, developing and
selling matching software products at a 20% or more discount.  The GDP
of Japan in 1994 was estimated by the CIA to be 2.5274 trillion dollars,
numbers that MicroSoft cannot match.  However, that scenario is not going
to happen, because MicroSoft leaders would cry out to their local
congressional representatives and anyone else who would listen that the
Japanese were dumping their products and engaged in unfair trade practices.
The truth is that many consumers will take what they can get for free,
regardless of the possible long-term effects of their buying decisions.
In agreement with the general economic theory that there is greater demand
for lower-priced goods, many consumers in the scenario above would drive
MicroSoft to be a much smaller company than it is today.  If it were not
for the selective enforcement of the antitrust laws of this country, then
Japan would be dominant in many industries in the United States, because
the Japanese have the capacity to act as one large company which can
subsidize unprofitable industries with profitable ones until competitors
go out of business.

If you are consistent in your beliefs, then you should have no qualms
about Japan dominating industries in the United States. 

-Ed L



