Gregg E Economou wrote:
> 
> In article <nasadowsk-0210971316030001@137.49.238.182>,
> Philip Nasadowski <nasadowsk@uhavax.hartford.edu> wrote:
> >know a single person out there who prefers it to a cleaner design like the
> >68k or the Alpha, or (gasp!) even the PowerPC.
> 
> Clean? geez, lets revise that list.
> 68K? ok, the 68000 was very clean, nice to rpogram for.
> Alpha? geez, it has incredible performance but do you know how complex it is?

  If it is so complex how come DEC makes 600 MHz implementations when its
  competition is in the 200 - 350 MHz range?  DEC's 0.35 CMOS process is
  not significantly faster than say Intel's so its not there.  Some of the
  difference is due to DEC circuit design and clocking methodologies but
  most is due to Alpha's simplified and streamlined architecture.

  Have you even read the Alpha architecture manual?  It is such a classic
  RISC design it is almost boring.  Where it differs from earlier RISCs
  like MIPS is mainly in simplifications like the elimination of delayed
  branches and allowing imprecise floating point exceptions.

> PPC? forget it!

  Motorola evidently has or it wouldn't once again have spent loads of
  shareholder's dough coming up with yet another ISA - M*core.

  PowerPC does have a few cute instruction mnemonics though:

     Old Macdonald had a RISC CPU,  EIEIO!
     with a pipeline flush here, and a pipeline flush there...

  BTW, it would make a ok RISC embedded control microprocessor but
  MIPS and ARM CPUs are already there and doing a mighty fine job.

> 
> my personal idea of a list of clean and beautiful architectures probably  goes
> like this: (ive left out mahcines i havent worked with to avoid going on
> folklorish issues)
> the Z80 and Z80000. If you dont know how it works grab a book from your library.

  Z80 - 6809 was a much cleaner 8 bit design and compiler friendly too.

  Z80000 - maybe if the Z8000 showed up on time and worked correctly someone
           could have given a damn.  The Z8000 ISA was similar to System 360
           but with 64K segmentation thrown in - yuk!

> the R2000. MAybe Im biased from having so many DECstations around ;)

  The MIPS ISA still looks amazingly clean and well thought out more than
  a decade later.  Well done!  Of course it is getting a bit thick around
  the middle with all the extensions (are we up to MIPS VI yet?) but middle
  age will do that to the finest ISA.

> the 68000 - but none of the others in the series.

  Compared to x86, the linear addressing and comparitively large 32 bit gp
  register set were heaven sent.  Compared to more orthogonal CISC designs
  like NS32000 and the VAX, the 68K instruction set still had a bunch of
  irregularities that could be annoying at times.

> 
> Ive never worked on a machine with an acorn processor but the architecture
> looks very interesting on paper ;)

  The ARM is an interesting ISA.  The gp register set is a bit small but the
  predication (conditional execution) of all instructions was ahead of its
  time and this feature will likely show up in Merced.  It packs a suprisingly
  lot of features into most instruction for a RISC and the thumb extension
  is a clever trick too.  Since virtually every major semi vendor has licensed
  it, ARM cores buried in ASICs will lurk in countless consumer and telecom
  gadgets.  The DEC SA-110 and SA-1100 implementations of ARM are so far ahead
  of the competition in the low power, high performance market it is scary.
  ARM and MIPS will be in furious horse race to be the Windows CE ISA of
  choice  (Hitachi SH? ROTFL; anyone remember any of the TRON micros?  :-)

> 
> Not everything is 'clean' just because its not intel- though intel is
> anything BUT clean.

  Yes, you should really wash your hands after handling a lot of cash  :-(

  x86 ISA - breaks virtually every "rule" of modern computer architecture
            design and is uglier than a turkey vulture with acne.  But
            thanks to the captive software base, inept competition, first
            class manufacturing processes, reasonably clever implementat-
            ions, sharp mass marketing and branding, x86 is Intel's license
            to print money.  I bet the tobacco and crack cocaine industry
            looks on Intel's x86 market with envy.


> please feel free to disagree with me, i wanna see what everyone else thinks

  I would with or without your permission but thanks anyways  ;-)


> is clean too
> 
> isildur

All opinions strictly my own (obviously)

--
