On Fri, 03 Oct 1997 12:06:04 -0400, John Price <jprice@chiinc.com> wrote:

>Aaron Bilger wrote:
>
>> A free market means no government screwing with people's choice to buy, sell, or
>> trade as they please.  If there is only one supplier in a market, you're free to
>> start a competing product.
>
>You'd best go re-read your history. Check under monopolies for Standard Oil (? --
>my memories failing me on the name, but I know it was an oil company.) It was

That's the oil company.  If you aren't even sure of the name, though, why do you
seem so sure of your facts?  Is this something you had in high school years ago
and just kind of remember?

>started as a free enterprise in every sense of the word, ran their competitors out
>of business and soon became the *only* oil company in america. That's right -- the

I see how accurate your facts are.  By shrewd maneuvers such as buyouts, rebates
and shipping agreements with railroads (and possibly some more vicious maneuvers
such as you do mention below), John Rockefeller did manage to capture
approximately 80% of the late 1800s oil trade for his standard oil.  Impressive,
but hardly the '*only*'.

>very definition of "no competion". Sure, people *tried* to start competing
>businesses but it didn't work. SO would lower their prices so low that the
>competion couldn't last, and then they'd raise them outrageously. 

Predatory pricing never played a big part in Rockefeller's game.  Indeed,
predatory pricing tends to be more destructive of the larger company than
smaller ones.  He wisely chose buyouts, shipping agreements, etc.

>(They also
>resorted to blatently illegal practices, such as torching the refineries, gas
>companies, murder, ... ). 

And if any company resorts to such destructive tactics, they are no longer
performing free trade but terrorism.  Trade agreements, changing pricing, etc.
are rational things you, Rockefeller, Gates, anyone can choose to do with their
business.  It's their company, their product, their right.  As soon as anyone
steals, torches, murders, or otherwise attacks anyone, they should be fully
liable for so violating peoples' rights.  If you have evidence that Microsoft is
bombing Sun buildings, please present it and I'll be the first to clamor for
criminal liability all the way up to Bill Gates.  But if you are talking only
about creating a legitimate product and selling it or giving it away, that is
anyone's right and not an attack on others.

>There was also no forms of price regulation. The railroad (another famous monopoly
>in those times)

'The railroad' was not one company.  However, it is true many areas did have
locally monopolistic railroad companies.  If a company buys land and builds a
railroad somewhere, it is their right to charge what they want for their
service.  However, nothing in the free market would prevent other companies from
building their own tracks on their own land and competition arising, driving
prices down.  Monopolies occurred despite this precisely because government gave
land grants to certain favored companies, and enforced favored companies being
the only companies to service certain areas.  You might want to learn about
companies such as Union Pacific sometime, and wonder where they would have been
had Big Brother not interfered with the free market for them.

>As to your comments on utilities in the US -- notice that , one by one, they are
>being deregulated.

And that is a very positive thing.  I am glad for what cable deregulation took
place in the recent telecomm act, etc.  And it serves to reinforce, not
contradict, my statement that government interference is what forces monopolies.
You give examples of competition arising as the government gets its hands out of
things:

> In Pennsylvania the Electric companies are now being deregulated
>and free competion is emerging. Now suddenly Peco energy (one of the highest energy
>prices in the US is going to have to compete with PP&L (one of the *lowest* priced
>in the US ... and right next door, too ...), and suddenly their rates are dropping
>... cable is being de-regulated piece by piece ... The only one I don't see
>de-regulating is the Water company ...


Aaron




