You'd best go re-read your history. Check under monopolies for Standard Oil (? --
my memories failing me on the name, but I know it was an oil company.) It was
started as a free enterprise in every sense of the word, ran their competitors out
of business and soon became the *only* oil company in america. That's right -- the
very definition of "no competion". Sure, people *tried* to start competing
businesses but it didn't work. SO would lower their prices so low that the
competion couldn't last, and then they'd raise them outrageously. (They also
resorted to blatently illegal practices, such as torching the refineries, gas
companies, murder, ... ). Only one person had control, and there was *no* way
anyone could compete. Period.

There was also no forms of price regulation. The railroad (another famous monopoly
in those times) had a habit of charging whatever they could get out of the
shippers. Drove alot of farmers out of business when their rates went up by 1000%
overnite (right after harvest, amazingly enough ...), and there was *no* other way
to get their product out.

Dumping, Gauging (?sp), and other practices are called "Predatory", because their
sole purpose is to drive competion out of business and then abuse the public with
their price control. Railroads were far worse because you *had* to have railroads.
There was no other (realistic) form of transportation.

As to your comments on utilities in the US -- notice that , one by one, they are
being deregulated. In Pennsylvania the Electric companies are now being deregulated
and free competion is emerging. Now suddenly Peco energy (one of the highest energy
prices in the US is going to have to compete with PP&L (one of the *lowest* priced
in the US ... and right next door, too ...), and suddenly their rates are dropping
... cable is being de-regulated piece by piece ... The only one I don't see
de-regulating is the Water company ...

Microsoft is, unarguably, the dominant power in the computer industry. So powerful,
that they have become (many say) a "de-facto monopoly". Sure there's competion. But
there's no *serious* competition. Even IBM had to bow to microsoft. Kinda.

(now for something different ...)

IMHO -- IBM got a "black eye" in recent years, but they came back actually
*listening* to the customers. And now their business is *booming*. (Where's this
all going ...?)

If and when BG & Co. get pious enough to stop listening, IBM will be in a position
to take advantage of that. After all, they own most of the competition.

No one sees it now, but when the time is right the IBM panther will leap on the
Microsoft Elephant ...

(and you'll be surpised what other animals will get in on it ...)

