I think for most people it comes down to:

If it *benefits* me, it isn't illegal, immoral, etc.
If it *harms* me, it is illegal, immoral, etc.

The fact is that the anti-trust laws have a reason for their existence.  
For example, let's say Microsoft successfully puts Netscape out of
business, then they say they simply cannot continue to offer IE for free. 
Since there is no competition, they can charge whatever they want.  Anytime
an 'upstart' comes along, MS just lowers the price sufficiently to make
sure the competitor never gains any market share.

Will MS do this?  Who can say, but if they are so generous, how come they
don't give away NT, or Win95, or Office, or....?    IMHO, it is simply
because there is no real competition.

This is no different than when a hardware manufacturer dumps product on the
market below cost simply to prevent others from being able to stay in
business and compete.  The anti-trust laws are in place to keep the playing
field level (though they are often used for political reasons).  Capitalism
works only when there is actual competition - but when that competition is
removed, the one who holds all the cards can set whatever price he/she
wants.  

You may argue that people will only pay what they think an item is worth,
so that limits the price even a monopoly can charge, but that is only half
of the issue.  The other half is that progress is typically hindered, since
it costs more to develop new technology than many companies would be
willing to pay were it not for the competition.  After all, would MS have
even developed IE had it not been for Netscape??

Regards,
	Dean

