>> All antitrust laws are an attack against peoples'
>> rights to buy and sell and trade honestly.
>
>No competion (due to monopolies or other reasons) means no free market.
>However impractical a free market economy is, I still find it to be the
>better way to obtain  "rights to buy and sell and trade honestly". So IMHO
>it is a lesser evil to have laws to keep a free market than to have no
>laws (which is even freer).

A free market means no government screwing with people's choice to buy, sell, or
trade as they please.  If there is only one supplier in a market, you're free to
start a competing product.  It would be absurd to try to claim Microsoft or
Intel have no competition.  I use Linux in addition to NT, work with MIPS chips,
have an AMD K5 in my home PC.  Anyone is free to choose DEC, IBM, Cyrix, Sun,
dozens of other chip vendors and hundreds of other software vendors.  This is
competition and free market so long as the government doesn't step in.  Real
monopolies, where you have no choice and must deal with one company at the rates
they demand, seem to be the realm of the government.  For example, electric,
water, or cable companies granted by government the right to be the only company
of their kind in an area.  Government laws work against free market.


Aaron




