How about the right of an individual (or group) to try to *honestly*
develop and sell a product?  Just when you create a market, some guy with a
bunch of money comes to take it away because he *can*.   Let us not forget
our history about *why* the antitrust laws were put into place.

Granted, the laws are many times used for political reasons (as are all
laws at one point or another), but when making a determination about
whether a company is in violation, you need to look at their overall method
of operation, not just one aspect.  For example

1) Do they use their market position to persuade resellers to sign
'exclusive' contracts which penalize them (like taking away their
'authorized' position)  if they want to offer another other options (I want
choices too)

2) Do they withhold information from competitors about product interfaces,
yet exploit them in their own products to gain a competitive advantage?

3) Do they use 'stongarm' tactics (i.e., threats of product availability)
to keep their business 'partners' in line so that they don't produce
products that are either competitive with, or work well with a competitor's
product?

4) Do they spend millions on developing a product, and sell it for less
than it cost them (or give it away) - merely to gain market share?  In
other words, why don't they give away products that have no other major
competitors, such as their Office products, Compilers or Operating Systems?

If all 4 questions are answered 'Yes', then that company seems to be using
their position to gain an unfair advantage over the competition.  Let's
face it, a company is in business to make money.  *Nobody* gives something
away unless they believe they will get something back for it.  What is it
that Microsoft expects to get back?   They have never denied they want
*market share* - and what possible use is market share if you don't plan on
making money??  Why would you care if it is your product or someone else's
being used if you don't plan on using the market position for your
financial gain?  Bill Gates is an extremely shrewd businessman - and shrewd
businessmen do not lose money on purpose, unless they have something up
their sleeve.

Anti-trust laws were implemented to try to make the playing field level for
everyone that wants to *honestly* compete.

I would be interested to hear what you say if Netscape is put out of
business, and Microsoft suddenly decides that their browser is no longer
free - and not only that, but it has features you don't even want, but you
have no choice about them.

Just my opinion...

Regards,
	Dean.

