> 
> We purchased lots of DEC systems, e.g. Alpha Station 500, 333Mhz and
> 400Mhz.
> 
> A few days ago, we had a big problem on our system.
> 
> Let me explain that disaster.
> 
> The root file system(/) of one of our machines was crashed.
> Thus we installed OSF 3.2 into new hard disk (Seagate 9GB).
> 
> That machine had an external disk for data storage(Seagate 9GB).
> The external disk used Advanced File System(advfs).
> 
> After we installed OS, we tried to mount the external disk.
> At first we made new file domain by using mkfdmn command,
> and then made new file set by using mkfset command.
> But unfortunately, we found that there was no original data
> in mounted external disk.
> 
> The data in that disk are very very inportant to us,
> therefore we are trying to recover them.
> 
> Anyone knows how to recover our original data?

Lets see if I got it straight.

Machine had two disks, lets name them RZ1 and RZ2, OK.
RZ1 contained system part of the machine, /, swap and (probably) /usr
RZ2 contained your user data, disk WAS AdvFS at the time.
Now RZ1 crashed and you brought in a new disk, RZ1.1
You installed the OSF 3.2 on RZ1.1

So far, so good.

But then you mounted (??!!) external RZ2 disk with [mkfdmn] and [mkfset]
commands??!!!
Those two commands CREATE a new AdvFS file-domain and file-set.
You deleted the old file system that was on the RZ2, sorry. It's just
like [mknewfs] for UFS or [format d:] for MS-DOS.

Getting your data back calls for a miracle or someone who's ready to dig
into the physical sectors on RZ2, piecing your data together. Oh, BTW, a
backup of RZ2 would come in handy.

Sorry,
Nix.

P.S. If this sounded sarcastic, know that there was no malice meant.
Shit happens. Good sence of humor is the only thing you've got right
now.



