In article <343265FC.32734227@chem.postech.ac.kr> Choi Hyuk Soon <chs@chem.postech.ac.kr> writes:

>   After we installed OS, we tried to mount the external disk.
>   At first we made new file domain by using mkfdmn command,
>   and then made new file set by using mkfset command.
>   But unfortunately, we found that there was no original data
>   in mounted external disk.

If you used mkfdmn to create a new domain on the external disk, you
implicitly wiped out whatever was there previously (if you used a
partition that was already part of an existing domain).  The mkfdmn
should have given you a warning that the operation would destroy
existing disk data.  To mount an existing domain/fileset that is not
known to the system, the way to do it is to create the appropriate
directory(ies) and symlinks to devices in the /etc/fdmns directory,
then simply use "mount domain#fileset /dir".

