> 
> BTW, I have never tried running a batch job with SYS$PRINT pointing to
> a null string (" "). You might want to try that.

Defining SYS$PRINT to be anything than isn't a valid queue will quite
happily consign the batch job logs to the bit bucket.

Depending on the exact requirements, one of the following might be the
correct approach:

a) Define SYS$PRINT in the system logical name table.  This will of course
affect all batch jobs unless users redefine it themselves.

b) Define SYS$PRINT in the process logical name table during login.  If all
the batch jobs in question run as the same username, this can be done in
that user's LOGIN.COM.  If the batch jobs run as various usernames but all
on the same batch queue, it could be done conditionally in SYLOGIN, e.g.

$ IF F$GETQUI("DISPLAY_JOB","QUEUE_NAME",,"THIS_JOB") .EQS. "..."
$	THEN
$	DEFINE SYS$PRINT NL:
$ ENDIF

where you replace "..." by the appropriate queue name of course.
