Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

> Maybe so, but the price is right, isn't it?

Did you read the ``kitchen-comparision'' on rec.humor.best-of-usenet?  

Few things can be as expensive as trusting your data to a single vendor,
just because the software is gratis, or more convenient.

Will you be able to read those documents in 10 years?  20 years? 100
years?  Is it important?  Can people access those documents today,
regardless of platform?  Can they manipulate them automatically?  Is
*that* important?

And if it matters at all, does the convenience of the readers weigh more
than layout issues?  Most people seem to think layout is more important
than content.  We had this discussion about HTML on Usenet, and many
users, especially new ones (that is, not the die hard old timers who
grew up in that warm, green glow), really really wanted to add fonts and
colors to their news messages.

Here, we take paper copies of every letter, and store it for some amount
of time - no doubt mandated by some authority.  It would be nice to do
this electronically, and facilitate simple searches and queries, etc.

But unfortunately, we ``standardize'' on a popular word processor, so
our data is ensured to suffer from quick bit rot, and we can't reliably
access electronic documents without knowing exactly what program,
program version, operating system version, and hardware it was produced
on.

I don't really want to flame you, because you are providing a lot of
valuable information to a large community with little or no
compensation.  Which I really do appreciate.  Honestly. But I am
frustrated to no end by the complete inability by so many users to
understand simple issues about data representation and how it differs
from presentation. 

~kzm
