I assume your interest is as a hobbiest. The DEC boxes are fast. Their best
uses are simulation and data analysis, scene rendering, anything heavily
floating point intensive. 

For every-day code hacking, mail, news, and web
browsing, I'm happy with a MooCow 486DX2/66 from gateway. 

At work, I
do process monitoring applications for power generation on DEC Alphas
running OpenVMS. OpenVMS is particularly good for this sort of thing --
it has the best event mechanisms out there for processes which must walk
and chew gum. The AST mechanism is nifty. Set Default in RMS is my pet
peve.

> 1) Can I have multiple OS installed (NT, LINUX, OSF, etc...)
> 
Probably as long as each is on its own disk. I've lived with a number of
VAXen and
AXP machines over the years. The current crop of AXP systems in the main
product
line wake up running a UNIX miniroot like critter from PROM/Flash. They are
bright
enough to boot by BOOTP/TFTP, DEC MOP, and from any of the SCSI disks.
There
is significant on line help and diagnostics, a simple shell, but no file
system. It is a
really nice startup environment.
> 
> 2) Where is a good source of info to find out hardware
compatability/drivers?
> 
See the release notes for the OS of interest. If it is genuine DEC, VMS or
ULTRIX
will just know what to do. These have been plug and play from the beginning
(well
of my experience with VMS). The LINUX hardware compatibility guide for the
AXP
version should tell you which boxes, devices, controllers, etc. work
reasonably well.
> 
> 3) Is there a good Shareware FTP site for AXP?
> 
//ftp:gatekeeper.dec,com/pub and also //www.digital.com library pages.
These change
names every time they reorganize the site. King's Colloge in London,
England and other
universities also have ftp stuff. Free Software Foundation has VMS ports of
many
of its most used stuff.
>
> 4) Any good Alpha user web-pages?
> 
I ususally visit genuine DEC for work. I've found InfoSeek a good source of
leads
provided you have some idea what you are looking for. It seems better at
making
appropriate suggestions than AltaVista. DEC does have an AltaVista server
for
its 20 internal web sites off its pages.



