Tom I Helbekkmo wrote:
> 
> "tim L." <"timled "@atlcom.net> writes:
> 
> > No the RA81 HDA will not work in the RA82's place. DEC modified the
> > write pcb and the servo on the 82 where it could handle the greater
> > density on the platter and give better transfer rate.
> 
> What if he moves all the electronics over, so that he's just using the
> mechanical parts (including platters) from the RA81?  Might he get
> lucky and find that his RA81 platters can handle the increased density?

Not likely.  An increase in density in achieved in two ways.  An
increase in the areal density, (i.e. write the bits closer together on a
track) and, decrease the spacing between tracks so more of them can be
written.  The RA82 uses both methods, and I would expect that the RA82
HDA has different heads (and internal electronics) and positioner from
the RA81.  I seriously doubt that the POST would complete without error
due to these differences and allow the drive to come on-line.

